Cumulative incidence of coronavirus (COVID-19) disease rising rapidly in the European Union/European Economic Area and the United Kingdom, 1 January to 15 March 2020
Cumulative incidence of coronavirus (COVID-19) cases shows a similar trend in European Union/Economic Area countries and the United Kingdom confirms that, while at a different level depending on the country, the COVID-19 pandemic is spreading rapidly in all countries.
Based on experience from Italy, countries, hospitals and intensive care units should increase their readiness for an increase in the number of patients with COVID-19 that will require health care, and in particular intensive care.
On 31 December 2019, an unknown etiological pneumonia cluster was reported in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
On 9 January 2020, China's Centers for Disease Control and Prevention reported the causative agent is a novel coronavirus, which is now referred to the acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Since then, the disease produced by SARS-CoV-2 infection has been named coronavirus (COVID-19).
To date, it has been proven that about 80% of individuals with COVID-19 have a mild illness, e.g. respiratory tract infections with or without pneumonia, and most of these patients are cured.
In about 14% of cases, COVID-19 developed into a more severe illness requiring hospitalization while the remaining 6% of cases suffered from critical illnesses that require intensive care.
The death toll in hospital due to COVID-19 is about 4%.
In this study, we assessed the trend in the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 in every European Union/European Economic Area (EU/EEA) and the United Kingdom (UK) as well as comparing those countries with Hubei Province, China.
We also compared the current number of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the UK with in Italy between 31 January–15 March 2020.
COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA and UK countries
After China, COVID-19 has experienced further geographical transmission and the dynamics of the COVID-19 pandemic around the world currently follows the country's dynamics.
On 11 March 2020, the Director General of the World Health Organization (WHO) announced COVID-19 as a pandemic.
In the issue of 5 March Eurosurveillance 2020, Spiteri et al. reported on the first confirmed COVID-19 cases in Europe according to the WHO’s case definition.
In the EU/EEA, the first three confirmed cases were reported by France on 24 January 2020 among those returning from Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
As of 15 March 2020, COVID-19 cases have been detected in all 30 EU/EEA countries and the United Kingdom (UK), between 31 December 2019 and including those dates, 39,768 cases and 1,727 deaths have been reported, with 17,750 cases and 1,441 deaths from Italy only.
Obtain the cumulative number and cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases
At the European Centers for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC), the contact of COVID-19 cases informed in every country around the world, is sourced for-only sources such as the country's Ministry of Health, national and regional health authorities as well as WHO, updated daily at 8:00 a.m.
This data is used to assess COVID-19 trends in the EU/EEA and the UK, as well as compare the data with Italy.
As an active COVID-19 case dissemination proxy, we have calculated cumulative-endimented incidents14 days of COVID-19 cases, thus taking into account the natural routes of COVID-19, in each EU/EEA and the UK countries, during the period of 1 January–15 March 2020.
We also presented the cumulative number of informed cases for each country on 15 March 2020 at 8:00 a.m. compared to the number of Italian cases for the period of 31 January-15 March 2020.
COVID-19 trends in EU/EEA and UK countries
The trend in the 14-day cumulative incidence of COVID-19 cases in EU/EEA countries and the UK generally follows the trend in Hubei Province (Chain) (Figure 1).
For the EU/EEA and the UK as a whole, the cumulative incidence of COVID-19 began rising around 21 February and then increased significantly around 28 February 2020 (Additional materials).
This was mostly driven by a rapid increase in the number of cases reported from Italy, however all other EU/EEA countries and the UK showed a similar upward trend for cumulative COVID-19 (Additional materials).
Diagram 2 shows the cumulative number of COVID-19 cases, in EU/EEA countries and the UK compared to cases in Italy for the period of 31 January-15 March 2020.
It emphasized that, as of March 15 at 8:00 a.m., 15 other EU/EEA countries and the UK have reported the same total number of cases as Italy just 3 weeks before or less.
Our results show that the number of informed COVID-19 cases is increasing rapidly in the EU/EEA and the UK.
The cumulative COVID-19 incident trend suggests that the pandemic is spreading at the same speed in all countries.
Although countries are at different levels, diversity in national public health responses and case definitions may vary in different countries as well as different protocols to select patients who must be tested for COVID-19 confirmation, including follow-up tests.
In early March 2020, doctors in the affected regions in Italy described a situation where 10% of patients infected with COVID-19 required intensive care and media sources reported that hospitals and intensive care units in the region had already reached maximum capacity.
Data on COVID-19 cases in hospitals and/or intensive care units are currently available at EU/EEA levels for cases of 6% and 1% only, respectively (data is not shown).
However, such data should be collected in a systematic way to support current monitoring data that focuses on the number of reported cases and the number of deaths.
A study conducted in 2010-11 showed substantial variations in the availability of intensive care beds and intermediary care in Europe, ranging from 29.2 in Germany to 4.2 beds per 100,000 population in Portugal.
This means that the country has more or less resources from Italy (12.5 intensive treatment beds and intermediary care per 100,000 population in 2010–11).
A modeling scenario associated with the saturation of healthcare capacity, with an estimate for each EU/EEA country and the UK for the spread of hospitalized COVID-19 cases associated with > 90% of the risk exceeds the capacity of intensive care beds, provided in the sixth update of the rapid risk assessment of COVID-19 by ECDC.
As so far, cases have formed clusters in certain districts in EU/EEA countries and the U.E., as well as hospitals and intensive care units typically handle clear area focus populations, information on cases and intensive care beds should be provided in Nominations for regional units for statistical level 2 (NUTS-2).
Experiences from Italy and current trends in other countries show the COVID-19 pandemic is spreading rapidly in the EU/EEA and the UK.
Countries, hospitals and intensivic care units therefore should be prepared for the scenario of a gradual community transmission of SARS-CoV-2 and an increase in the number of COVID-19-infected patients requiring health treatment, especially intensive care, as is the case in the affected regions in Italy.
As emphasized in ECDC’s fast-paced risk assessment recently, a fast-paced, progractive and comprehensive approach is important to slow the SARS-COV-2 outbreak, with shifts from a curbing approach, as expectations of a rapid increase in the number of cases may not provide decision-making and hospital enough time to understand, accept and adjust their response accordingly if not implemented earlier.
The rapid risk assessment also lists general health care measures to reduce the impact of the pandemic.
There are short windows of a chance that countries have the possibility to further increase their control efforts to slow the spread of SARS-CoV-2 as well as reduce stress on health care.
If this fails, it's possible that other EU/EEA health care systems will face an increase in patients who need intensive care in the coming days or weeks.
Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19), due to severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2), has so far killed more than 3,000 people and infected more than 80,000 people in China and other parts of the world.
Similar to its homologous virus, SARS-CoV, which caused SARS in thousands of humans in 2003, SARS-CoV-2 may also be spread from bats and cause similar symptoms through similar mechanisms.
Nevertheless, COVID-19 has a lower severity and number of deaths than SARS but is more contagious as well as affecting more elderly people than adolescents and more men than women.
In response to a rapidly increasing number of publications about the emerging disease, this article is trying to provide a timely and comprehensive insight into the rapidly-growing research subjects.
We will discuss the basics of epidemiology, etiology, virology, diagnosis, treatment, prognosis and prevention of the disease.
While many questions still need answers, we hope that this review helps in understanding and eradicating the life-threatening disease.
The Spring Festival on January 25, 2020 has become an unforgitable memory and will never be forgotten by Chinese people who are urged to stay at home during the holiday season and weeks after the outbreak of a novel virus.
The virus is very homologous to the coronavirus (COV) that caused severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) outbreak in 2003; therefore, it was named SARS-CoV-2 by the World Health Organization (WHO) on 11 February 2020 and a related disease called CoV Disease-19 (COVID-19).
This epidemic began in Wuhan, China and immediately spread throughout the country and to nearly 50 other countries around the world.
As of March 2, 2020, the virus has caused more than 80,000 confirmed COVID-19 cases, with more than 40,000 patients allowed to return home and more than 3,000 patients have died.
WHO warns that COVID-19 is "public enemy number 1" and has the potential to be stronger than terrorism.
According to PubMed (https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/), in less than two months, more than 20000 papers have been published about COVID-19 including virology, epidemiology, etiology, diagnosis and its treatment since the first report on January 7, 2020 which determines the sequence of viruses isolated from a number of patients.
This review is trying to summarize the progress of research in a new and fast-expanding subject.
When appropriate, we'll try to compare COVID-19 with SARS and another disease caused by CoV, Middle Eastern respiratory syndrome (MERS, erupted in 2012).
We'll also discuss things that have been known so far about the prevention and prognosis of the disease as well as a few more important questions.
CoV is traditionally regarded as a harmless pathogen to humans, which is usually the cause of about 15% of the common cold 4.
However, in this century, we've received twice the high-pathogenic human VOV, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, which led to outbreaks of their respective outbreaks in China in 2003 and Saudi Arabia in 2012, and soon spread to many other countries with rates of morbidity and severe deaths.
Therefore, COVID-19 is currently the fired CoV outbreak in recorded human history.
As shown in Diagram 1, the pneumonia cluster of unknown origins was first reported from Whuhan on December 31, 2019 to China's National Health Commission.
Seven days later the CoV sequence was released.
On 15 January 2020 the first death from Wuhan was reported.
Meanwhile, the epidemic spread rapidly to cities, regions and neighboring countries.
On January 20, an infection among health care staff has been reported, indicating that human-to-human transmission can occur.
On 23 January, the city of Wuhan was imposed a curfew, with all public transport services halted.
On January 24 the first clinical study of the disease reported that, out of 41 patients with confirmed cases, only 21 had direct contact with the Wuhan seafood market which was considered the starting point of infection from unknown animal sources.
On January 30, WHO declared the disease a global health emergency.
During this report, the disease has spread throughout China and nearly 50 other countries around the world (Figure 2).2).
As the situation is progressing rapidly, the final scope and severity of the disease have yet to be ascertained.
As of 11 February 2020, a multi-central study of 8,866 patients including 4,021 confirmed COVID-19 patients provided a more up-to-date illustration of the epidemic as follows (https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/UlBi-HX_rHPXa1qHA2bhdA).
SARS-CoV-2 infects patients of all ages, but mostly aged 30-65.
Almost half (47.7%) of infected patients are above 50 years old, very little under 20 years of age and only 14 infected individuals are under the age.
SARS-CoV-2 infects more men (0.31/100,000) than women (0.27/100,000).
COVID-19 is growing in clusters mostly in and around Hubei.
COVID-19 takes an average of 5 (2-9) days from the start to diagnosis.
The average incubation period is 4.8 (3.0-7.2).
The average time from start to death is 9.5 (4.8-13) days.
The basic number of reproduction (R0) is 3.77 (95% CI: 3.51-4.05), and an adjusted RO is 2.23-4.82.
The number of infected people increased exponentially before Jan. 23, 2020, corresponding to the time of mass movements before the Spring Festival in China.
Patient mortality rate with confirmed cases is 1.44% (95% CI: 1.10-1.86%), and an adjusted mortality rate for all patients is 3.06% (95% CI: 2.02-4.59%).
Three main factors for COVID-19 are gender (male), age (≥60) and severe pneumonia.
COV is a sub-family for a large and insulated virus that contains a singular thread of sensory RNA.
The virus can be divided into four genera, e.g., alpha, beta, gamma, and delta, among all alpha- and beta-CoV is known to infect humans.
Spike (S) surface glycoprotein binds to its cellular receptors of angiotensin 2 (ACE2) and dipeptidyl peptidase 4 (DPP4) for SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV respectively, and then the incorporation of membranes occurs.
Viral RNA genus is released into the cytoplasm; after the application of the viral genus, the genome RNA is accompanied by surface glycoproteins and nucleocapsid protein forming vesicles containing virions, which then merge with the plasma membrane to release the virus.
The first genome sequence of SARS-CoV-2 was reported on January 10, 2020.
SARS-CoV-2 was found to beta-coV with more than 99.98% genetic identity in 10 vehement samples taken from the disease's original site, the Huanan Seafood Market in Wuhan.
SARS-CoV-2 is genetically more similar to SARS-CoV than MERS-CoV.
Through the transfer electron microscropic, SARS-CoV-2 particles have been found in the thinnest part of the epithelium of the human respiratory tract.
Human ACE2 was found to be the recipient of SARS-CoV-2 as well as SARS-CoV.
However, the S protein for SARS-CoV-2 is tied to ACE2 weaker compared to SARS-CoV, which coincides similar to the fact that SARS-CoV-2 causes less severe infections in patients compared to SARS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 can also form new simple proteins encoded by orf3b and seceded by orf8.
orf3b for SARS-CoV-2 may play a role in the pathogenicity of the IFNβ; however, orf8 does not contain any functional domains or motives.
On 18 February 2020, Zhou, et al., reported that the cyro-EM structury of human ACE2 was entirely at 2.9 Å resolution in the complex with an amino-besis carrier B0AT1.
They found that the complex, which had open and closed validation, had been combined as dimers and the ACE-BOAT2 complex could combine two S proteins, which provided evidence for recognition and COV infection.
B0AT1 can be the target of drug filtration therapy to curb SARS-CoV-2 infection.
Original and intermediate hoses
It is well known that both SARS-CoV and MERS Cov are derived from bats and are transferred to humans through foxes and camels.
According to the phylogenetic comparison of SARS-CoV-2 with other CoVs, bats are considered the original host of SARS-CoV-2 because the new virus is 96% similar to two VOV-such as SARS from a bat-SL-VOVX45.
However, an intermediate hose that helps the virus to complicate a species barrier to infect humans remains unknown, and the transmission path has yet to be clarified.
Ji, et al., suggests a snake as a carrier of the viral from bats to humans involving a re-combination of homologs in S proteins.
According to the study, researchers in Guangzhou, China, suggested that long-annoted mammals of an ant-eatings commonly used in traditional Chinese mediacy - is a potential intermediate host for SARS-CoV-2 based on 99% genetic homology in COV found in pangolins and SARS-CoV-2.
Nevertheless, the 1% difference scattered across the two genomes is still a big difference; therefore, the final decision to get concrete evidence is pending (Fig.33).
The physicochemical properties of SRAS-CoV-2 are still unknown.
SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV can survive in vitro for 48 hours in a dry environment and up to 45 days below 20 °C and humidity 40%-50%.
SARS-CoV-2 may have similar properties.
It has been reported that SARS-CoV-2 is sensitive to ultraviolet and heat radiation at 56 °C for 30 minutes; ether, 75% ethanol, chlorine-containing disinfectants, peraspectic acids, chlorophomes, and other fat solvents, but not chlorhexidine, can inactivate the virus effectively.
The whole human population generally does not have immunity against SARS-CoV-2 and is therefore considered susceptible to viral novels.
Currently, no detailed studies have been reported in respect of immunological responses to SARS-CoV-2.
Therefore, we can only refer to previous studies on other CoVs, especially SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV (Figure (Figure 4).4).
In general, after the virus encroaches, the virus was first identified by the host's natural immune system through pattern identification receptors (PRRs) including receptors such as C-type lectins, receptors such as Toll (TLR), NOD-like (NLR), and receptors such as RIG-I (RLR).
Despite different pathways, the virus results in the expression of inflammatory factors, the maturity of dendritic cells and interferon systhesis type I (IFNS) that limits the spread of the virus and accelerates the phagocytosis macrophage of viral antigens.
SARS-CoV's N protein can help the virus avoid an immune response.
Shortly thereafter, the immune response of the adaptive also fights the virus.
T lymphocytes including CD4+ T cells and CD8+ play an important role in defense.
CD4+ T cells stimulate B cells to produce virus-specific antibodies and CD8+ T cells continue to kill virus-infected cells.
Auxiliary T cells produce inflammatory cytokines to help defense cells.
However, CoV can inhibit the function of T cells by resulting in apoptosis of T cells.
Humoral immunity including complementaries such as C3a and C5a as well as antibodies are also important in the fight against viral infections.
For example, antibodies isolated from patients who have recovered neutralized MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, the immune system's excessive response generates a large number of free radicals in the body that can cause severe damage to the lungs and other organs, as well as, in the worst scenario, the failure of some organs, even death.
SARS-CoV-2 infection, based on earlier clusters, is more likely to affect the elderly with comorbidities and pregnant women.
It is common for those who are exposed to a large number of viruses or whose immune function is affected, they have a higher chance of being infected compared to others.
The estimated average incubation period for SARS-CoV-2 is 1-14 days, usually 3-7 days based on a study of the first 425 cases in Wuhan.
However, a study of 1,099 cases showed that the incubation period is 3 days on average and ranges from 0 to 24 days.
A more up-to-date study, as mentioned above, showed that the incubation period was 4.8 (3.0-7.2) days based on a demographic of 8,866 cases.
It is important for the health authorities to adjust the effective quarantine time based on the most accurate incubation period, thus preventing those who are infected but do not have symptoms from transmitting the viral to others.
As a common practice, individuals who are exposed to, or are infected by, viruses usually need to be quarantined for 14 days.
Does the quarantine period should be extended to 24 days?
Fever is usually the main and early symptoms of COVID-19, which can be accompanied by symptoms or other symptoms such as dry cough, shortness of breath, muscle pain, dizziness, headache, sore throat, rinorrhea, chest pain, diarrhoea, nausea and vomiting.
Some patients experience dyspnea and/or hypoxemia a week after the onset of the disease.
In severe cases, the patient’s patient’s condition is immediately increased to acute respiratory syndrome, septic shock, metabolic acidosis and coagulopathy.
Patients suffering from fever and/or symptoms of breathing and acute fever, even without pulmonary imaging abnormalities, should be screened for viruses for early diagnosis.
Demographic studies at the end of December 2019 showed that the percentage of symptoms was 98% for fever, 76% for tuberculosis, 55% for dyspnea, and 3% for diarrhoea; 8% of patients needed respiratory support.
Similar discoveries have been reported in two recent studies of family clusters and clusters resulting from the spread of asymptomatic individuals.
By comparison, demographic studies in 2014 showed that MERS-CoV patients also had a fever (98%), tuberculosis (47%), and dyspnea (55%) as their primary symptoms.
However, 80% of them need respiratory assistance, far more than COVID-19 patients and are consistent with higher facing by MERS compared to COVID-19.
Diarrhea (26%) and sore throat (21%) were also seen in MERS patients.
In SARS patients, it has been shown that fever (99%-100%), dry cough (29%-75%), dispnea (40%-42%), diarrhea (20-25%), and sore throat (13-25%) are the main symptoms and respiratory advocacy has been required for about 14%-20% of patients.
As of February 14, the Covid-19 death toll was 2% when confirmed cases reached 66,576 worldwide.
Comparatively, the mortality rate from SARS up to November 2002 was 10% out of 8,096 confirmed cases.
For MERS, based on a demographic study in June 2012, the death rate was 37% out of 2,494 confirmed cases.
Preliminary studies reported that R0 for SARS-CoV-2 was as high as 6.47 with a 95% confidence cell (CI) 5.71-7.23, while R0 for SARS-CoV only ranges from 2 to 4.
Comparison between SARS-CoV-2 and MERS-CoV and SARA-CoV with respect to the symptoms, mortality rates and RO of the disease are shown in Table1.1.
The above numbers suggest that SARS-CoV-2 has a higher ability to contaminate compared to MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV, but is less fatal than the two diseases.
Therefore, it is much more challenging to control the SARS-CoV-2 epidemic compared to MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV.
Clusters often occur in the same family or from the same gatherings or vehicles such as cruise ships.
Patients usually have a history of traveling or living in Wuhan or other affected areas or in contact with an infected individual or patient in the latest two weeks before starting to get sick.
However, it has been reported that patients can carry the vira without symptoms for longer than two weeks and recovered patients who have been discharged from the hospital can carry the virus again, which sends a warning to increase the quarantine time.
The patient has a normal amount of peripheral white blood cells (especially lymphocytes) or decreases at an early stage.
For example, lymphopenia with a white blood cell count • 4×109/L including lymphocyte count < 1×109/L , and increased levels of aminotransferase asspartate and viremia have been found in 1,099 COVID-19 patients.”
Levels of liver and muscle and myoglobin enzymes have increased in the blood of some patients, and C-reactive protein as well as erythrocyte sedimentation has increased in the blood of most patients.
In patients with severe cases, the level of D-dimer, fibrin degradation products available in the blood, have increased, and lymphocyte count has deteriorated significantly.
Abnormalities in chest radiography are found in most COVID-19 patients and are manifested by bidial spotted shadows or opacity of ground glass in the lungs.
Patients usually experience atypic pneumonia, acute lung injuries and acute difficulty breathing syndrome (ARDS).
When ARDS occurs, uncontrollable inflammation, rapid accumulation of fluid and fibrosis adversely affect gas exchange.
Dysfunction pneumocytes of type-I and type-II reduces the level of surfactants and increases surface tension, thus reducing the ability of the lungs to expand and increase the risk of lung failure.
As such, the discovery of bad chest radiographics is usually parallel to the most severe stage of the disease.
On 18 February 2020, the first pathological analysis of COVID-19 showed a pneumocyté, the formation of hyaline membranes and the infiltration of interstitial lymphocytes as well as multinucleic sinsitium cells in the lungs of patients who died from the disease, are consistent with pathologies of viral and ARDS infections as well as similar the pathologies of SARS and MERS patients.
Detection of SARS-CoV-2 RNA through reversed-transcription polymerase (RT-PCR) has been used as the main criterion for the diagnosis of COVID-19.
However, as a result of high false negative rates, which may accelerate the epidemic, clinical manifestations began to be used for diagnosis (which is no longer solely dependent on RT-PCR) in China on February 13, 2020.
A similar situation also occurs with the diagnosis of SARS.
Therefore, a combination of the history of the disease, clinical manifestations, laboratory tests and radiological discoveries are important as well as imperative to make an effective diagnosis.
On February 14, 2020, the Feng Zhang group described the prototol using the CRISPR-based SHERLOCK technique to detect SARS-CoV-2, which detects a synthetic SARS-CoV-2 RNA fragment at 20 × 10-18 mol/L to 200 × 10-18 mol/L (10-100 copies per microliter input) using a dip stem in less than an hour without the need for complicated equipment.
Hopefully the new technique can significantly increase sensitivity and convenience if it is confirmed in a clinical sample.
As a result of a lack of experience with a novel CoV, doctors can generally provide supportive treatment to COVID-19 patients, while trying a variety of therapies that have been used or previously proposed for the treatment of other CoVs such as SARS-COV and MERS-CoV as well as other viral diseases (Table2).2).
These therapies include current and potential treatments with antiviral, immunosuppressants, steroids, plasmas from recovered patients, Chinese medicine and psychological support.
Plasma from recovered patients is also recommended to be used for treatment.
The pharmaceutical company is racing to develop antibodies and vaccines for the virus.
SARS-CoV-2 usually attacks the lungs at the initial stage and may also attack, at a lower level, other organs that produce ACE2, such as the gastrointestinal and kidney systems.
However, dysfunction and respiratory failure are a major threat to the patient and is the leading cause of death.
Therefore, respiratory support is important for relieving and life-saving symptoms as well as including general oxygen therapy, high-flow oxygen, non-invasive ventilation and invasive mechanical ventilation depending on the severity of the disease.
Patients with severe respiratory symptoms should be supported by the oxygenation of the excorporeum membrane (ECMO), a modified cardiopulmonary grafting technique used for the treatment of cardial or respiratory failure.
In addition, the retention of electrolyte balance, the prevention and treatment of secondary infections as well as sepsis shock, and the maintenance of vital organs is also necessary for SARS-CoV-2 patients.
It is known that cytokine storms result from an overreaction of the immune system in SARS and MERS patients.
Cytokine storms are a form of systemic inflammatory response characterized by cytokine series emissions including TNFα, IL-1β, IL-2, IL-6, IFNα, IFNβ, IFNγ, and MCP-1.
These cytokins trigger immune cells to release large numbers of free radicals that are the main causes of ARDS and multi-organ failure.
Immunoting is important in the treatment of cytokine storms, especially for severe patients.
Corticosteroids and tocilizumab, anti-IL6 monoclonal antibodies, have been used to treat cytokine storms.
Other immunot-trenching treatments for cytokine storms include the modulation of T-cell-directed immune responses; IFN-γ, IL-1, and TNF; JAK inhibition; blinatumomab; cytokine signal suppressor 4; and HDAC inhibition.
Steroids, as immunosuppressants, have been widely used in treating SARS to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
However, high-dose steroids are not beneficial for severe lung injuries in SARS and COVID-19 patients.
On the other hand, steroids at high doses can cause severe side effects, especially avascular osteonecrosis, significantly affecting the prognosis.
However, low to moderate doses of corticosteroids for a short period of time have been recommended to be used cautiously for critical COVID-19 patients.
When this article was written, no effective antiviral therapy was confirmed.
However, intravenous use with remdesivir, a nucleotide analogue, has been found to be effective against an American patient infected with COVID-19.
Remdesivir is a novel antiviral drug developed by Gilead initially for the treatment of diseases caused by the Ebola and Marlburg viruses.
Later, remdesivir also showed the possibility of inhibition of other single threaded RNA viruses including MERS and SARS viruses.
Based on these observations, Gilead has given the mixture to China to carry out a pair of trials against individuals infected with SARS-CoV-2, and the results are highly awaited.
In addition, baricitinb, interferon-α, lopinavir/ritonavir, and ribavirin have been proposed as potential therapies for patients with acute respiratory symptoms.
Diarrhea, nausea, vomiting, liver damage and other adverse effects can occur after combined therapy with lopinavir/ritonavir.
Interactions of this treatment with other drugs applied to the patient should be carefully monitored.
Plasma from patients who have recovered and the generation of antibodies
Accumulation of blood from patients who have recovered from infectious diseases to treat other patients suffering from the same disease or to protect healthy individuals from contracting the disease has a long history.
It's true, patients who have recovered usually have relatively high levels of antibodies against pathogens in their blood.
Antibodies are immunoglobulin (Ig) produced by B lymphocytes to fight pathogens and other foreign objects and they identify unique molecules in pathogens thus neutralizing them directly.
Based on this, plasma has been collected from the blood of a group of patients who have recovered from COVID-19 and have been injected into 10 patients who are in a serious condition.
Their symptoms improved within 24 hours, accompanied by reduced inflammation and viral load as well as increased saturation in the blood.
However, verification and clarification is needed to propose the method for large-scale uses before undeveloped specific therapies.
In addition, due to the presence of therapeutic effects, some of the disadvantages associated with the plasma should be carefully considered.
For example, antibodies can overstimulate immune responses and cause cytokine-relieving syndrome, which is a potentially life-threatening toxicity.
The concentration of antibodies in the blood is usually low and the demand for plasma is great for treating critical patients.
It's hard to develop and produce specific antibodies quickly to fight a global epidemic.
Therefore, it is more important and practical to isolate B cells from patients who have recovered as well as identify a genetic code that encodes effective antibodys or screating effective antibodies against important viral proteins.
In this way, we can increase the production of antibodies.
TCM has been used to treat various diseases in China for thousands of years.
However, the effect largely depends on the combination of various components in different formulas according to the diagnosis of the disease based on TCM theory.
Most effective components remain unknown or fuzzy as it is difficult to extract and verify such components or their optimal combination.
Currently, as a result of the specific and effective lack of therapy for COVID-19, TCM has become one of the main alternative treatments for patients with mild to moderate symptoms or for those who have recovered from severe levels.
For example, Shu Feng Jie Du capsules and Lian Hua Qing Wen capsules have been found to be effective for the COVID-19 treatment.
The highest recovery rate in the treatment of COVID-19 patients has been seen in several provinces in China that use TCM in 87% of their patients, including Gansu (63.7%), Ningxia (50%) and Hunan (50%), while Hubei province, which uses TCM in just about 30% of COVID-19 patients, has the lowest recovery rate (13%).
However, this is a rough comparison because many other impact factors such as the number and severity of the patient should be included in the assessment.
On February 18, 2020, Boli Zhang and his colleagues published a study to compare western drug treatment (WM) only in combination with WM and TCM treatments.
They found that the time required for body temperature recovery, loss of symptoms and hospital treatment was significantly shorter in the WM+TCM group than the WM group only.
Amazingly, rates for symptomatic conditions worsen (from mild to severe) are significantly lower for WM+TCM groups from WM group alone (7.4% compared to 46.2%) and the mortality rate is lower in the WM+TCM group from WM group only (8.8% versus 39%).
Nevertheless, TCM's effectiveness and safety are still waiting for more manageable attempts at a greater extent and in more centers.
It's also interesting to visualize the mechanism of action and explain the effective components in TCM's treatment or a combination of such treatments if possible.
Patients who are suspected or diagnosed with COVID-19 mostly experience great fear of a highly contagivable disease and can lead to death, while those who are quarantined also experience boredom, loneliness and arrupt.
In addition, symptoms of infections such as fever, hypoxia and cough as well as the adverse effects of treatment such as corticosteroids can lead to greater anxiety and mental dystres.
In the early phase of the SARS outbreak, various psychiatric morbidities including persistent depression, anxiety, panic attacks, psychomotor excitement, psychotic symptoms, delirium, and even suicidance were also reported.
Mandatory contact tracing and quarantine, as part of the public health response to the COVID-19 pandemic, can make people feel more worried and guilty about the effects of infection, quarantine as well as stigma on their families and friends.
Therefore, mental health treatment should be given to COVID-19 patients, suspected individuals and people in contact with them as well as the public in need.
Psychological support should include the establishment of a multi-disciplinary mental health team, clear communication with regular and accurate updates about the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak and treatment plans as well as the use of professional electronic devices and applications to avoid close contact with each other.
An effective vaccine is important to disrupt the transfer chain from the infected animal and human areas to an exposed hose as well as typically complement antiviral treatments in epidemic control caused by viruses that appear.
Efforts have been undertaken by developing S protein-based vaxises to generate long-term and/effective and/imminant antibody that protects against SARS-CoV.
Debilitating vaccines have been directly tested in animal models for SARS.
However, the effectiveness in vivo candidates of this vaccine in older individuals and dangerous challenge models and their protection against zoonotic viral infections should be determined before clinical studies begin.
This is probably because SARS had ended 17 years ago and no new cases have been reported since then.
In contrast, sporadic cases and MERS clusters continue to occur in the Middle East and spread to other regions due to continuous zoonotic resources in endemic areas.
Vaccination strategies have been developed for MERS by using non-activated viruses, DNA plasmids, virus vectors, nanoparticles, particles such as viruses and recombinant protein subunits as well as some have been tested in animal models.
The development of a safe and effective vaccine against SARS-CoV-2 for non-immum individuals is an urgent and important task to control the epidemics that are taking place.
However, it is challenging to overcome difficulties due to a long period of time (average of 18 months) is necessary for the development of the vaccine and dynamic variations of CoV.
As a new disease, COVID-19 has just shown a full clinical route in thousands of patients.
In most cases, the patient can recover slowly without being re-infected.
However, similar to SARS and MERS, COVID-19 is also associated with high rates of morbidity and mortality in patients with severe cases.
Therefore, constructing the prognosis model for the disease is important for health care agencies to prioritise their services, especially in areas lacking resources.
Based on clinical studies reported so far, the following factors may influence or be associated with the prognosis of COVID-19 patients (Table33):
Age: Age is the most important factor for the SARS prognosis, which is also true for COVID-19.
COVID-19 usually occurs at the age of 30-65 with 47.7% of those patients above the age of 50 in a study of 8,866 cases mentioned above.
Patients who need intensive care are more likely to have hidden comorbidities and complications and are much older than those who don't need it (at the median age of 66 versus 51 years old), suggests age as a prognosis factor for COVID-19 patients.
Gender: SARS-CoV-2 has infected more men than women (0.31/100,000 versus 0.27/100,000), as mentioned above.
Commorbidy and communities: Patients with COVID-19 who need intensive care are more likely to have acute cardium and arrhythmia injuries.
Cardium is also the cause of death in SARS patients.
It has been reported that SARS-CoV-2 may also be tied to ACE2-positive cholangiocytes, which may lead to liver dysfunction in COVID-19 patients.
It should be noted that hidden ages and diseases are interconnected and can interfere with each other.
Abnormal laboratory discoveries: C-reactive protein (CRP) levels in the blood indicate the severity of inflammation or tissue injury and have been proposed as a potential prognosis factor for the disease, a response to therapy and final healing.
The CRP's level of relevance to the severity and the COVID-19 prognosis has also been proposed.
In addition, lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), aspartate aminotransferase (AST), alanine aminotransferase (ALT), and increased kreatina kinase (CK) can also help predict results.
This enzyme is secreted abundantly in various organs, especially the heart and liver, and is excreted during tissue damage.
Therefore, it becomes a traditional marker for dysfunction of the heart or liver.
Primary clinical symptoms: Chest radiography and the progress of clinical symptoms on time should be considered along with other issues to predict the consequences and complications of COVID-19.
The use of steroids: As described above, steroids are an immunotressing that are commonly used as an adjunct therapy for infectious diseases to reduce the severity of inflammatory damage.
While high-dose corticosteroids have been widely used in severe SARS patients, many former patients suffer from avascular osteonecrosis with lifelong disability and poor quality of life.
Therefore, if necessary, steroids should be used at low doses and for a short period in COVID-19 patients.
Mental stress: As mentioned above, during the COVID-19 pandemic many patients have suffered due to extraordinary stress as they regularly faced a long period of quarantine and extreme uncertainty as well as witnessing the death of close family members and fellow patients.
It's important to provide long-term psychological and supportive counseling to help these patients recover from stress as well as return to normal life.
According to demographic studies so far, COVID-19 appears to have different epidemiological characteristics than SARS.
In addition to multiple in the lower respiratory tract, SARS-CoV-2 can efficiently multiply in the upper respiratory tract and cause mild or no symptoms in the early phases of the infection, similar to other COVs that cause the common cold.
Therefore, patients infected in the early phases or incubation period can produce large amounts of the virus during daily activities, making it very difficult to control the epidemic.
However, the spread of SARS-CoV is thought to occur when patients are seriously ill, while most transmission does not occur in the initial phase.
Therefore, the current COVID-19 outbreak is much worse and more difficult to control than the SARS outbreak.
Great efforts are being carried out in China including a curfew in Wuhan and the surrounding city as well as constant quarantine of almost the entire population in the hope of cutting the spread of SARS-CoV-2.
Although these actions severely damaged the economy and other sectors in the country, the number of new patients decreased, signaling the slowness of the epidemic.
The most optimistic estimate is that the pandemic will end at the end of March and the downside phase will run for 3-4 months.
However, some other experts are not very optimistic.
Paul Hunter, et al., estimated that COVID-19, which appears to be much more contagious than SARS, will not end in 2020.
Ira Longini, et al., made a model to predict the consequences of the epidemic and suggests that SARS-CoV-2 can infect two-thirds of the world's population.
A Canadian group reported that SARS-CoV-2 has been detected in both the central turbinate swabs and the patient's throat that had recovered as well as leaving the hospital 2 weeks earlier, signaling that the newly-identified virus could be a repetitive episode such as influenza.
However, convincing signals have occurred in China based on the declining number of new cases, suggesting that the current strategy may be effective.
Ebola was originally predicted to cause up to one million cases with half a million deaths.
However, with strict quarantine and isolation, the disease was finally successfully controlled.
Therefore, similar to SARS-CoV, it is possible that the infectivity of SARS-CoV-2 becomes weaker and eventually ends or becomes a virus of fewer pathogens that coexists with humans.
Comparison between the COVID-19 epidemic with SARS and MERS is provided below (Figure (Figure55).
SARS-CoV-2 is highly contagious through coughing or sneezing, and can also be transmitted through direct contact with a substance that has been contaminated by the virus.
The virus was also found in feces, which raises new possibilities of transmitting from feces to the mouth.
A recent study of 138 cases reported that 41% of cases may be caused by nosocomial infections, including 17 patients with other diseases and 40 health workers.
Therefore, careful precautions should be used to protect humans, especially health personnel, social workers, family members, friends, and even roadside people in contact with patients or infected persons.
The first defensive line that can be used to reduce the risk of infection is by wearing a face mask; both the use of surgical masks and N95 respiratory masks (series #1860) helps control the spread of the virus.
Surgical face masks prevent liquid droplets frequently infected from spreading through ventilation or sticking to the surface of the material, which in turn can be disseminated to others.
However, only N95-type masks (#1860s series) can protect against inhalation of virions as small as 10 to 80 nm, with only 5% virions that can penetrate the N95-type mask; SARS-CoV-2 is almost the same as SARS-CoV in terms of the size where both are approximately 85 nm in size.
Since viral particles can penetrate five surgical face masks worn together, health workers who are in direct contact with the patient need to wear a N95 mask (#1860s series) instead of a surgical face mask.
In addition to face masks, health workers are required to wear personal protective equipment to reduce contact with the virus.
Virals can also infect individuals through the eyes.
On 22 January 2020, a doctor was infected with SARS-CoV-2 despite wearing a N95 face mask; the virus may have entered his body through an inflammatory eye.
In this regard, health workers also need to wear transparent face shields or safety glasses when dealing with patients.
For the public in the affected areas or is likely to be affected, it is advisable for all residents to wash their hands with disinfectant soap regularly, stay at home and reduce contact with individuals who are likely to be infected.
A three-legged distance is considered an ideal distance to stay away from patients.
These measures are an effective method of reducing the risk of infection in addition to inhibiting the spread of the virus.
Although SARS-CoV-2 is a new viroid, homology high to SARS-CoV as reported on January 7, 2020 has warned China just like the SARS outbreak in 2003.
However, only on January 19, 2020 the Director of the Wuhan Center for Disease Control and Prevention tried to reduce its citizens' concerns by saying that the novel's virus has low rates of infection and limited reproduction in humans as well as the prevention and containment of the disease is not a problem.
The message eased public anxiety, especially when the rest of the country was preparing for the Autumn Festival, and it could not be welcomed to curb the spread of the outbreak on a minimum scale in Wuhan.
Disease control agencies in China can take heed of this and make improvements in the future.
For example, agencies should (1) be more careful in making announcements to the public as every detail conveyed affects the attitudes and decisions of the people; (2) is more sensitive and reactive to the extraordinary information received from the clinic rather than waiting for a formal report from a doctor or an officer; (3) is stricter in curbing the outbreak rather than calming the community of the public.
COVID-19 is caused by the SARS-CoV-2 novel virus that began at the end of December 2019.
In less than 2 months, it has spread throughout China and nearly 50 countries around the world at the time of writing.
Since the virus is very similar to SARS-CoV and the symptoms are also very similar to SARS, the spread of COVID-19 has created the sentiment that the SARS outbreak has recurred.
However, there is a significant difference between COVID-19 and SARS, which is important in curbing the epidemic as well as treating patients.
COVID-19 has an impact on older individuals compared to young people and affects men more than women, the severity and mortality rate is also higher in the elderly compared to youth.
SARS has a higher mortality rate compared to COVID-19 (10.91% versus 1.44%).
COVID-19 patients can spread the virus without experiencing any symptoms while SARS patients spread the virus in pain, which results in difficulties in curbing the spread of COVID-19 compared to SARS.
This explains the causes of SARS-CoV-2 spreading faster and wider than SARS-CoV.
The results of the RNA test for SARS-CoV-2 may be negative in COVID-19 patients.
However, patients who have recovered can be tested positive for the viral again.
This discovery indirectly increases the risk of transmission of the virus.
Based on rapid advances in COVID-19 studies, some critical issues still need to be resolved, including:
Where does SARS-CoV-2 come from?
Although 96% of genetic homologues are found in SARS-CoV-2 and two CoVs such as bat SARS, we can't conclude that SARS-CoV-2 also comes from bates.
What are the animals that mediated the spread of the viral from hosts, for example bats, to humans?
Without knowing the answers to #1 and 2, we can't break the spread efficiently, and outbreaks can recur at any time.
Although molecular and biochemical models tests have shown that SARS-CoV-2 is tied to ACE2, how exactly can viruses enter airway cells and subsequently lead to pathological changes?
Does the virus also tyens the ACE2 expression cells in other organs?
Without a clear answer to this question, we are unable to achieve a quick and accurate diagnosis as well as effective treatment.
How long will this epidemic survive?
How does the virus develop genetically through transmission in humans?
Will it become a pandemic around the world, wiped out like SARS or recurring periodically like a cold?
This is important but it will take the time to find the answers to the above questions and various other questions.
However, regardless of the expenses to be claimed, we have no choice but to stop this epidemic as soon as possible and return our lives back to normal.
Zoonotic origin of human coronavirus
Mutations and adaptations have prompted the co-exercision of the coronavirus (CoV) and its main hosts, including humans, for thousands of years.
Prior to 2003, two human CoVs (HCOVs) were known to cause mild diseases, such as colds.
Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome (SARS) and Middle Eastern Respiratory Syndrome (MERS) outbreaks have changed views and revealed how HCOV is terrible and can be life-threatening.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 in China at the end of 2019 has attracted attention to CoV and surprises us with its infectivibility which is simple but less pathogenic compared to SARS-CoV.
HCoV infection is a zoonosis and an understanding of zoonotic origin of HCoV can help us.
Most HCoV comes from non-pathogenic bats.
Intermediary reservoir hoses for some HCoVs are also known.
Identification of animal hoses has direct implications on the prevention of human diseases.
Investigations regarding the interaction of CoV hoses in animals allow the importance of comprehension obreed about the pathogenesises in humans.
In this study, we presented an overview of existing knowledge related to seven HCoVs, with a focus on the history of its discovery and zoonotic origins and interspect transmission.
What's important is that we compare and contrast HCoV's differences from a viral evolutionary perspective and recombination of the genome.
Epidemic diseases during COVID-19 are discussed in this context.
In addition, the need for a successful hose exchange as well as the implications for the evolution of the virus on the severity of the disease are also emphasized.
Coronaviruses (CoVs) are from the Coronaviridae family, which consists of a group of single-streddled RNA viruses, positive, shrouding.
This genomic length is between 26 to 32 kilobases and is the largest genome among RNA viruses and is called "CoV" due to its morphological shape that looks like a crown under an electron microscope.
In terms of structure, CoV has a non-segmented genome that shares the same compilation.
It is estimated that two-thirds of these genomes have two large open reading frames (ORF1a and ORF1b) intersperated, which are then translated into pp1a and p1ab polyprotein replicase.
Polyproteins are then processed to produce 16 non-structurized proteins, known as nsp1~16.
The remaining genome consists of ORFs for structured proteins, including squirrels (S), envelopes (E), membranes (M) and nucleoproteins (N).
Some protein-specific protein-specific nasabah is also coded according to different CoV.
Based on the difference in protein sequence, CoV is classified into four genera (alfa-CoV, beta-CoV, gamma-CoV and delta-CoV), where the beta-Cov genera consists mostly of HCoV and is divided into four sequences (A, B, C and D).
Phylogenetic evidence suggests that bats and rodents act as a source of genes to most alpha-CoV and beta-CoV, while birds are the main reservoirs for gamma-CoV and delta-coV.
For thousands of years, CoV has exceeded the resistance of the species and some have emerged as an important human pathogen.
To date, 7 human coronavirus (HCoVs) are known.
Among them are HCoV-229E and HCoV-NL63 which are alpha-CoVs.
The other five beta-CoVs include HCoV-OC43, HCOV-HKU1, severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV), Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS-CoV) and SARS-CoV-2.
HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63 usually cause moderate symptoms, such as flu and/or diarrhoea.
On the other hand, SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 are highly pathogenic, resulting in severe lower respiratory tract infections in patients who have a high probability of developing acute respiratory distres syndrome (ARDS) and extrapulmonary manifestations.
B814, has been isolated from the nasal discharge of patients who had flu in the mid-1960s.
Since then, more knowledge has been gathered through extensive studies on HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, both of which results in self-confidence symptoms.
This concept has been widely accepted that HCoV infection is harmless until the SARS outbreak appears.
The SARS outbreak occurred in 20003 and was one of the worst destruction in history, infecting more than 8,000 people with a gross mortality rate of approximately 10%.
Ten years later, the Middle East respiratory syndrome (MERS) outbreak resulted in an epidemic continuing in the Arabian Peninsula with sporadic spread around the world.
Novel HCoV 2019 (2019-nCoV), which was later named SARS-CoV-2, is the ongoing epidemic-causing agent of the coronavirus outbreak 2010 (COVID-1), which has killed more than 3,120 lives and infected more than 91,000 individuals as of 3 March 2020.
Warnings have been given and the world should be prepared for the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic.
All seven HCoVs have zoonotic origins from bats, rodents and domestic animals.
Various evidence supports the evolutionary origins of all HCov from bats, in which viruses are easily adaptable and non-pathogenic but show great genetic diversity.
The COVID-2019 epidemic has brought great medical, scientific, social and moral challenges to China as well as the rest of the world.
Detection of zoonotic origin of HCoV provides a framework for an understanding of natural history, driving force and restriction factors for species jumps.
This may also drive and facilitate the search for reservoir animal hosts, intermediaries and amplators for SARS-CoV-2, with important implications in the prevention of overflighting in the future.
In this review we present an overview of zoonotic origins, the transmission between the species and the pathogenesis of HCoV.
Particularly, we outline and discuss a common theme where HCoV virion is non-pathogenic inside the host's natural reservoir but becomes pathogenic after the transmission of interspesis to the new hose.
We also reviewed the evolutionary trends of HCoV where increased condisposition often emerges with pathophetic reduction.
As a result of the ongoing SARS-CoV-2 outbreak was also discussed in this context.
Veterinary CoV has been known since the late of the 1930's.
Prior to the first isolation of HCoV-229E strain B814 from the nasal discharge of patients suffering from the flu, different CoVs were isolated in a variety of infected animals, including turkeys, mice, cows, pigs, cats and dogs.
In the past few decades, seven HCoVs have been identified.
A brief summary of the history of HCoV's discoveries in chronological rules (Table 1) can provide information and teaching.
The first HCoV-229E strain was isolated from the respiratory tract of patients with upper respiratory tract infections in 1966, and have subsequently been adapted to grow in the WI-38 lung cell line.
Patients infected with HCoV-229E are seen with flu symptoms, including headaches, sneezing, lethargy and sore throat, with fever and cough seen in 10~20% of cases.
Later in 1967, HCoV-OC43 was isolated from organ culture and subsequently a serial excerption in the brains of a nursing mice.
The clinical description of HCoV-OC43 infection is similar to that of HCoV-229E, which is symptomatically indistinguishable from infections with other respiratory tract pathogens such as influenza A and rhinovirus viruses.
Both HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43 are spread around the world and tend to contagious more during whines in temperate regions.
Usually, the incubation period of these two viruses is less than one week, followed by the disease for about 2 weeks.
According to a study on human volunteers, healthy individuals infected with HCoV-229E experienced a mild common cold.
Only a few immunocompromised patients showed severe lower respiratory tract infections.
SARS, also known as “atypical pneumonia”, is the first well-recorded HCoV pandemic in human history and its etiological agent is SARS-CoV, the third HCOV found.
The first case of SARS can be traced back to the end of 2002 in Guangdong Province in China.
The SARS epidemic resulted in 8,096 reported cases with 774 deaths, spread across many countries and continents.
Apart frequent super disseminants, it is estimated that each case can lead to about two secondary cases, with an incubation period ranging from 4 to 7 days and a virus-free-free culmination appears on the 10th day of illness.
Patients infected with SARS-CoV initially showed mialgia, headache, fever, circulation and cold, followed by dyspnea, coughing and respiratory distress as late symptoms.
Lymphophenia, a scattering liver function and increased creatina kinase is the normal laboratory abnormalities of SARS.
Alveolar difusal damage, expansion of epithelial cells and an increase in macrophages are also observed in SARS patients.
Approximately 20-30% of patients then require intensive care and mechanical ventilation.
In addition to infections of the lower respiratory tract, various organs including the gastrointestinal tract, liver and kidneys can also be infected in severe cases, usually accompanied by cytokine storms, which can lead to death especially immunomopromised patients.
The virus was first isolated from an open lung biopsy of a relative of an index patient who traveled to Hong Kong from Guangzhou.
Since then, many efforts have been carried out for HCoV research.
HCoV-NL63 was isolated from a 7-month-old child from the Netherlands in late 2004.
Initially the virus was found to be widespread in children, the elderly and immunocompromised patients with respiratory diseases.
The presence of coriza, conjunctivitis, fever and bronchiolitis is common in diseases caused by HCoV-NL63.
Another independent study describes the isolation of the same viral from an 8-month-old-year-old child suffering from pneumonia in the Netherlands.
Although identified in the Netherlands, the virus is actually spreading all over the world.
It is estimated that HCoV-NL63 is the cause of about 4.7% of normal respirational diseases and the peak time of the occurrence of the disease is during the beginning of summer, spring and winter.
HCoV-NL63 is associated with obstructive laryngitis, also known as crupping.
In the same year, HCoV-HKU1 was isolated from a 71-year-old man who was hospitalized with pneumonia and bronchiolitis in Hong Kong.
In addition to pneumonia and bronchiolitis infected in the community, HCoV-HKU1 has also been reported to be associated with acutic exacerbations.
Similar to HCoV-NL63, HCoV-229E and HCoV-OC43, HCoV-HKU1 is found worldwide, causing mild respiratory diseases.
All four infected HCoVs in these communities have been well adapted to humans and are generally less likely to mutate and cause very pathogenic diseases, although accidents have indeed occurred for unknown reasons because there have been rare cases of a more harmful HCoV-NL63 sub-type, which have recently been reported to have caused severe lower respiratory tract infections in China.
Generally speaking, when HCoV acquires the ability to spread efficiently and to remain persistently in the human body, the viral also becomes less harmful or pathogenic.
MERS-CoV was first isolated in 20212 from the lungs of a 60-year-old patient suffering from acute pneumonia and kidney failure in Saudi Arabia.
While most laboratory-confirmed cases originated in the Middle East, cases imported with occasional secondary transmission to close contacts have been reported in various European and Tunisian countries.
Another secondary outbreak occurred in South Korea in 2015 with 186 confirmed cases.
The clinical manifestations for MERS are similar to SARS, characterized by progressive acute pneumonia.
Unlike SARS, many patients with MERS also experience acute kidney failure, which is so far unique to MERS among diseases caused by HCoV.
More than 30% of patients show gastrointestinal symptoms, such as diarrhoea and vomiting.
As of 14 February 2020, more than 2500 laboratory-confirmed cases have been reported with 34.4% fatalities, making MERS-CoV one of the most severe viruses affecting humans.
During the middle to the end of December 2019, the retrospective cluster of pneumonia patients was identified in connection with SARS-CoV-2 infection was detected in Wuhan, Hubei Province, China.
The World Health Organization has declared an outbreak of a lower respiratory tract infection caused by SARS-CoV-2 as an International Level of Public Health Emergency and also named the disease.
As of March 3, 2020, 90,053 cases have been reported around the world, with a total of 3.4%.
Significantly, the death toll in Hubei, China was 4.2%, while the death toll outside the area was 1.2%.
SARS-CoV-2 causes severe respiratory infections such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, emerging as fever, cough and dyspnea.
Diarrhea also occurs in some patients.
Pneumonia is one of the most severe symptoms and can increase rapidly into an accute prone syndrome.
Although SARS-CoV and SARS-COV-2 are very similar due to 82% high nucleotide sequence homology, the virus clusters become different branches of the philogenetic tree.
SARS-CoV-2 appears to be less pathogenic but is more contagious compared to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Asymptomatic subjects infected with SARS-CoV-2 have been reported and may contribute to rapid transmission around the world.
Comparing and distinguishing SARS-CoV-2 with the other six HCoVs reveals very interesting similarities as well as differences.
Firstly, the time of incubation and the duration of the occurrence of HCoV is very similar.
In this regard, SARS-CoV-2 follows the general trend of six other HCoVs.
Secondly, the severity of COVID-19 symptoms are between SARS-CoV and four HCoVs obtained from the community (e.g. HCoV-229E, HCoV-OC43, HCOV-HKU1 and HCoV-NL63).
In some terms, the SARS-CoV-2 infection shows features that are more frequently seen during infection by HCOV found from the community, including the presence of non-specific symptoms, mild or no symptoms.
On the other hand, a small subset of severe COVID-19 cases can also be seen as in the case of SARS-CoV infection, although the ratio is slightly lower.
Thirdly, SARS-CoV-2 contagion has also shown an interesting pattern feature for both HCOV obtained from the community and SARS-CoV.
In some terms, the infectivity of SARS-CoV-2 is at least as high as HCoV available from the community.
On the other hand, it is difficult to confirm whether the infectivity of SARS-CoV-2 decreases after passing through humans such as SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV cases.
Finally, just like other HCoVs, SARS-CoV-2 can be detected in fecal samples.
Where the transfusion of the vengeal-to-mouth for SARS-CoV-2 plays an important role as in the case of SARS-CoV at least under certain circumstances waiting for enlightenment through future studies.
It's also interesting to see if SARS-COV-2 may occur according to the season as in the case of HCoV infected in the community.
However, the SARS-CoV-2 features include infectivity, pathogenity and continuous diffusion after humans will influence the final fate of the ongoing COVID-19 outbreak.
All of the four-infected HCoVs in the community cause mild symptoms have been adaptable to humans.
From another point of view, it may also be true that humans have adapted well to these four HCoVs.
In other words, both may have survived the previous HCoV pandemic.
HCoV, which causes severe diseases in humans and humans suffering from severe HCoV disease, has been eliminated.
In order for this to occur, HCoV needs to multiply in humans up to a sufficient extent to enable the accumulation of adaptive mutations that fight hose restriction factors.
Therefore, the longer the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak survives and the most of infected humans, the greater the opportunity for the virus to adapt completely to humans.
If the virus adapts well, its transmission in humans will be difficult to be prevented by quarantine or other infection control measures.
For many years, four infected CoVs in communities circulated in the human population, triggering the common flu in immunocompromicial subjects.
The virus does not requisite an animal reservoir.
On the other hand, SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV have not yet adapted to humans well and the transmission of the virus in humans cannot be maintained.
The virus needs to remain and reproduce in its zoonotic reservoir as well as find an opportunity to spread to an infected human target, possibly through one or more intermediary hoses and breeders.
SARS-CoV-2 has similar characteristics to both SARS-CoV/MERS-CoV and all four infected HCOVs in the community.
It is very contagious such as infected HCoV in the community, at least at the moment.
However, the virus is more pathogenic than HCoV infected in communities and less pathogenic than SARS-CoV or MERS-CoV.
It's time to determine whether the viravirus will be fully adaptable to humans and spread among humans without reservoirs or intermediarial hoses.
Before discussing the origins of animals that are the causes of HCoV, it is better to discuss the definitions and characteristics of evolutionary, natural, reservoirs, intermediaries and reproduction of HCoV.
An animal acts as an evolutionary host for HCoV if he or she owns a very close heirs who share high homology at nucleotide sequence levels.
Hereditary viruses are usually well adapted and not pathogenic in this hose.
Similarly, the hose reservoir stores HCoV continuously and for the long-term.
In both cases, the hose is usually naturally infected and is a natural host for HCoV or its virion.
On the other hand, if the new HCoV is introduced to an intermediary host before or around its introduction to humans, the virus does not adapt well to a new hose and is usually pathogenic.
This intermediary hose can act as a zoonotic source for human infection and play the role of a reproductive host by allowing the virus to multiply temporarily and then spread the virus to humans to strengthen the scale of human infection.
HCoV can develop a dead-end if it is unable to maintain its infectivity in an intermediary hose.
On the other hand, HCoV can also adapt to an intermediary host and also create long-term identity.
In this case, the intermediary hose becomes a natural reservoir hose.
Epidemiological data revealed resprospectively that SARS index cases have a history of contact with fugitive animals.
Seroprevalent investigations further suggest that animal sellers have a much higher-level prevalence of SARS-CoV IgG than the general public.
Lamri musang (Paguma larvata) and raccoon dog in live animal markets were first identified to carry viruses such as SARS-COV similar to SARS-CoV.
This is indirectly supported by the fact that no SARS was reported again after killing all foxes in the market.
However, it has been reported that lamri fox from forests or farms without exposure to the live animal market is mostly SARS-CoV-negotiation, suggesting that a lamri fox may only become a host reinforces an intermediary but is not a natural reservoir of SARS-CoV.
Most notably, given that 80% of different animals in the market in Guangzhou have anti SARS-CoV antibodies, it is likely that a variety of small mammal species may also be hosts to strengthen intermediaries for SARS-CoV inevitable.
All of this appears to be the host of the dead path for SARS-CoV.
Further searches for natural animal hoses for SARS-CoV revealed closely-linked bat CoV, called HKU3 (SARSr-Rh-BatCoV HKU3) of Rhinolophus-related bats, which exists in China's flats.
These bats are positive for SARS-CoV anti-anti-genome anti-sequences of SARS-Rh-BatCoV HKU3.
These bats and other CoV bats share 88-92% homogeneous nucleotides with SARS-CoV.
This study has provided the basis for the new concept that bat hosts produce human pathogens.
Some CoVs such as SARS (SL-CoVs) have also been identified from bats, but none except one called WIV1 can be isolated as a living virus.
Human angiotensin 2 (ACE2) converter enzyme is known as the recipient for SARS-CoV.
WIV1 obtained from bat feces samples is indicated using bats, foxes and human ACE2 as recipients for cell entry.
What's even more interesting is that SARS patients are found to be able to neutralize WIV1.
So far, WIV1 represents the most closely related hereditary for SARS-CoV in bats, sharing 95% nucleotide sequence homology.
Although there is a high homology between these two viruses, it is generally believed that WIV1 is not a direct parent viroid for SARS-CoV and that the bat is not a direct reservoir host of SARS-CoV.
Phylogenetic analysis put MERS-CoV into the same group as HKU4 CoV bats and COV-HKU5 bats.
COV-HKU4 and MERS-CoV bat use the same hose receptor, dipeptidil peptidase 4 (DPP4), for the entry of viruses.
The RNA polymerase sequence depending on RNA from MERS-CoV is philogenetically closer to the vira virus in bat beta-CoV identified from Europe and Africa.
To date, no live MERS-CoV can be found in wild bats.
MERS-CoV and the nearest CoV-HKU25 bat shared only 87% homogeneous nucleotide sequence.
Therefore, bats may be not a reservoir host directly to MERS-CoV.
On the other hand, studies in the Middle East have shown positive effects on MERS-CoV-specific neutralization antibodies, similar to camels in the Middle Eyard that originated in various African countries.
A living MERS-CoV similar to a virus found in humans has been isolated through an Arabic camel nasal swab, further indicating that camels serve as a genuine MERS-CoV reservoir host.
It is also worth noting that generally moderate symptoms but a large admission of the virus has been observed in an infected camel with MERS-CoV.
Mainly, an infected camel releases viruses not only through the respiratory tract but also through the passage of feces, which is also the route for the virus to be released from bats.
However, there has been a permanent question since many confirmed MERS cases have not had a history of contact with a camel prior to the onset of symptoms, may be linked to human-to-human spread routes or unknown spread routes involving unfamiliar animal species carrying MERS-CoV.
SARS-CoV-2 shared 96.2% homologous nucleotides with bat RaTG13 COV separated from affinis Rhinolophus bat.
In cases of SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, the sequential deviation between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is too large to determine the parent relationship.
Arguably speaking, bats may not be the nearest reservoir host to SARS-CoV-2 unless similar bat CoV is discovered in the future.
Possibly, an intermediary animal hose to SARS-CoV-2 is from wild species sold and killed at the Huanan Seafood Wholesale Market, which is linked to many early cases of COVID-19, indicating the possibility of animal dissemination events to humans.
Some recent studies based on metagenomic sequences have suggested that a group of endangered small mammals known as pangolin (Manis javanica) can also store hereditary beta-CoV-related by SARS-CoV-2.
This mangolin CoV genome shares 85-92% nucleotide sequence homology with SARS-CoV-2.
However, they are equally closely related to RaTG13 with about 90% identity at nucleotide sequencing levels.
They are grouped in two subreviants from viruses such as SARS-CoV-2 in pylogogenetic trees, one of which shares receptor-binding domain (RDB) more similar to SARS-CoV-2, with 97.4% amino-acinal sequential identity.
In real differences, RBD from SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is more distorted, despite having a higher degree of homological sequence of genomes.
A previous study of infected pangolins also reported the detection of viral contigs from a sample of the lungs, which was later found to be similarly linked to SARS-CoV-2.
The study uses the method of collecting and selected manual layouts to generate a partial genome sequence that accounts for approximately 86.3% of the full viral genome.
We can't ignore the possibility that pangolins are one of an intermediary host for SARS-CoV-2.
However, there is currently no evidence to support SARS-CoV-2 originating directly from pangolins due to the sequential deviation between SARS-CoV-2 and beta-COV-connected SARS-COV-2.
In addition, the distance between SARS-CoV-2 and RaTG13 is shorter compared to SARS-CoV-2 and beta-CoV-linked SARS-COV-2.
SARS-CoV-2′′′’s evolutionary route in bats, pangolins and other mammals still needs to be prescribed.
Whereas the highest homological sequence has been found in RBD between SARS-COV-2 and pangolins, beta-CoV-2, SARS-CoV-2 and RATG12 which shares the homologology of the highest genomes.
Strong speculation states that high-level similarity between RBD from beta-CoV-related SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-COV-2 SARS-CoV-2 is driven by-focused evolution.
A counter-proposal in favor of a recombination between beta-CoV-related SARS-CoV-2 pangolin and RATG13 in third wild animal species.
As an evolutionary drive force, re-grainting occurs widely in beta-CoV.
Studies are still conducted on direct zoonotic origin of SARS-CoV-2.
In addition to highly pathogenic HCoV, zoonotic origin of HCoV-229, HCoV-OC43, HCoV-NL63 and HCOV-HKU1 have also been researched.
Phylogenetic evidence shows both HCoV-NL63 and HCOV-229E may have originated from bat COV, while virion HCoV-OC43 and HCov-HKU1 have been found in rodents.
It has been reported that a bat COV called ARCoV.2 (Appalachian Ridge CoV) detected in North America's three-coloured bat indicates close contact with HCoV-NL63.
On the other hand, HCoV-229E is geneticaly associated with another bat CoV, called Hipposideros/GhanaKwam/19/2008, which has been detected in Ghanana, when camelid was also suspected of being an intermediary host.
To be more clear, the current knowledge of the animal origins of HCoV is known to be summarized in Diagram 1 and Table 2.
Phylogenetic analysis has provided evidence for HCoV transmission events between species in history.
When HCoV-OC43 across species to infect humans from domestic livestock around 1890, a respiratory infection pandemic was recorded.
The history of transmission of HCoV-229E among the species is ambiguous.
But bat alpha-CoV closely linked to HCoV-229E has been discovered.
Among them is alpha-CoV.
Some line of evidence supports the transmission of the virus from bats to humans directly.
First, humans but non-alpaka may have come into contact with bats in a shared ecological niche.
On the other hand, humans have close contact with alpaka.
Secondly, alfa-CoV-linked hCoV-229E is diverse and non-pathogenic in bats, whereas alpha-CoV alpaka triggers respiratory diseases in infected animals.
Ultimately, the alpha-CoV alpaka is not found in wild animals.
Therefore, it is possible that it cannot be excluded that the alpaka gets the alpha-CoV-linked HCoV-2229E from humans.
In fact, bats are a direct source of human pathogenic viruses including rabies viruses, Ebola viruses, Nipah and Hendra viruses.
As such, it's not too surprising that bats can directly transfer HCoV-229E to humans.
As an alternative, although alpha-CoV bats into a HCoV-229E gene, alpaka and castes may be the host of intermediaries that transmit the viral virus to humans, exactly as in the case of MERS-CoV.
MERS-CoV is the best example for the transmission of species from bats to Arabic camels and from Arabic camels to humans.
MERS-CoV’s original evolution from bats is known at the introduction and has been reinforced by subsequent discoveries.
It is clear that bats provide viral species for the exchange of genetic fragments between species and inter-spective transmission.
Longevity, dense congested colonies, close social interactions and strong flying capabilities are all favorable conditions for bats to become an ideal 'viral disseminator'.
In addition, MERS-CoV has been introduced to an Arabic camel for decades.
It adapts well to these camels that have switched from an intermediary host to a stable and natural reservoir hose.
MERS-CoV causes a very mild disease and maintains a relatively low rate of mutatation in these animals.
Its sporadic transmission to humans is unintentional and humans remain the final host of MERS-CoV because its transmission cannot be sustained.
In contrast to the function of camels in MERS-CoV, the function of mangolin, if any, in the transmission of SARS-CoV-2 is different.
Primarily, pangolin beta-CoV is very pathogenic in pangolins.
They may be the final hoste for SARS-CoV-related beta-CoV-2, similar to the fox in the case of SARS-CoV.
Some possibilities for the transmission of SARS-CoV-2 between species from animals to humans should be accepted or rejected in future studies.
Firstly, bats can host reservoirs for SARS-CoV-2-related viruses that are very similar to SARS-CoV-2.
Humans may share an ecological niche with bats through slaughtering or coal mining.
Secondly, pangolins can be one of the new intermediary amplification hoses recognized by SARS-CoV-2-related viruses.
Humans are infected with the viral through slaughtering and eating fugitive animals.
Perhaps many mammals including domestic animals are exposed to SARS-CoV-2.
A review of domestic and wild animals for antibodies is required.
Thirdly, as mentioned above, the recombination and adaptation of SARS-CoV-2 may have occurred in the third species in contact with both bats and pangkolin.
The search for animal origin for SARS-CoV-2 is still ongoing.
Apart from different types of hose animals, the three main factors in the viral part are also important in making it easier for CoVs to surpass species barriers.
First of all, the rate of their mutation is high in multiplination of RNA.
Compared to a singular thread RNA virus, the estimated rate of COV mutation can be considered "moderate" to "high" with the average exchange rate being £10-4 per yangles per 2 sites, depending on the adaptation phase of CoV to the novel hose.
COV has proof-redicting exoribonuclears, its extinguishing causes very high variability and weakness or may also be the absence of vitality.
Interestingly, remdesivir nucleotide analogues are known to suppress the multipling of CoVs through the inhibition of this exoribonuclease and RNA-dependent polymerases.
Remdesivir is one of the best anti-SARS-CoV-2 agents tested in clinical trials.
Despite this, the rate of CoV mutations is about a million times higher than its hosts.
In addition, mutation rates are often high when COV are not well adapted to the hose.
Compared to SARS-CoV with a high mutation rate, the mutation rate in SARS-CoV-2 appears to be low, suggesting a high degree of adaptation to humans.
Perhaps, it's already adapted to other hosts who are close to humans.
In addition to SARS-CoV-2, this also applies to MERS-CoV, which is well adapted to Arabic camels.
In theory, it is not possible that genetic displacement will make vaccines and antivirals against SARS-CoV-2 incompetiable in a short period of time.
Secondly, the largest RNA genomes in CoV use additional plasticity in genome changes for mutations and re-combinations, thus increasing the probability of common evolution among the species, which is beneficial for the emergence of novel CoV when conditions are appropriate.
This is supported by many unique open-reading frameworks and protein-encoded functions towards the 3' genome end.
Thirdly, COV converts templates randomly and regularly during multiplulation of RNA through a unique "copy-of-the-selection" mechanism.
In a hose that serves as a mixed container, thread exchanges often occur during the transcription of RNA CoV.
A highly homologous subgenome RNA can be recompinated to produce a new CoV.
philogenetic evidence of natural incorporation has been found in both HCOV-HKU1 and HCoV-OC43, as well as COV animals such as Bat SL-COV and Bat-HKU9.
Viral hose interactions are associated with transmission.
In addition to the three viral factors mentioned above, viral interaction with host receptors is another major factor that is influential in transmission between species.
Here, the reincorporation of SARS-COV is taken as a special example, which also shows evidence of a positive choice during an interspective contagiation event.
Based on the comparative analysis between human isolates and SARS-CoV foxes, SARS-CoV is thought to have undergone rapid adaptations in different hoses, especially with mutations at RBD on S proteins.
In general, RBD on protein S on CoV interacts with cells and is strongly selected by the hosal antibody reaction.
In SARS-CoV, RBD is in the 318 to 510 amino acid on S1 fragments, which bind to human ACE2 as well as co-receptors for the influx of the virus.
RBD from SARS-CoV is able to identify ACE2 receptors from a variety of animals, including bats, foxes, rodents and raccoon dogs, allowing the spread of viruses between species.
In fact, only 6 remaining amino acids are observed as different from the isolates of human viruses and foxes in RBD and 4 of them are located inside receptor-binding motifs for interactions with ACE2 receptors.
SARS-CoV fox has mutations of K479N and S487T in its RBD, which may increase the afforation of pacak protein interactions with human ACE2 receptors.
In other words, both exchanges of amino acids may be important for the adaptation of the virus to humans.
It should be noted that SARS-CoV-2 shares receptor cells similar to SARS-CoV.
30% of the difference between SARS-CoV-2 and SARS-CoV in the Q1 unit of the S protein has the effect that the bond of this S-protein affinity with human ACE2 may have been altered.
Naturally, cryo-EM's research shows the affinity of this bond, which is 10 to 20 times higher than between human ACE2 and SARS-CoV S proteins.
It is also interesting to determine whether any other co-repiled receptors may be necessary for the spread of SARS-CoV-2.
Interestingly, HCoV-NL63 is also bonded to ACE2 but with different parts of S.
There are many other HCoV receptors that exist, such as aminopeptidase N for HCoV-229E, and 9-O coremethyl sialik acid for HCoV-OC43.
It may also be responsible for the successful adaptation of this CoV in humans after the transmission between species from its animal hosts.
In addition to cellular receptors, as a result of the transmission between HCoV species also depends on the host's dependency and other barrier factors.
The difference in this hose protein between humans and HCoV's natural reservoir hoses such as bats, Arabian camels and rodents may create a barrier to interspecies transmission.
HCoV needs to seize hose dependency factors and sub-distribute the host restriction factors for the transmission between successful species.
In this regard, the molecular determinant in an important area of the interaction of these viruses has not yet been identified and characterized.
Unbiased genomes around the genome for hose dependencies and restriction factors for SARS-CoV-2 using advanced CRISPR technology may bear fruit.
The emergence of novel HCoV: back to the beginning
Diversification of bat CoV provides ample opportunities for the emergence of novel HCoV.
In this testement, the bat CoV acts as the HCoV gene pool.
In addition, rapid mutations and genetic recombinations also drive the evolution of HCoV and act as two important steps in this process.
For example, the acquisition or loss of novel protein-encoding genes has the potential to drastically modify viral phenotypes.
Among the SARS-CoV accessory proteins, ORF8 has been considered important in adapting with humans, as SARS-CoV-related bat viruses have been isolated but were found to encode the diverged ORF8 proteins.
SARS-CoV nucleotide-29 exficiency features have been discovered in strains isolated at the onset of the human epidemic.
This deletion separates ORF8 into ORFa and ORF8b as well as being regarded as an adaptative mutation that encourages hose exchange.
In addition, SARS-CoV has a possible history of recombination with the descendants of alpha and gama CoV, a large number of smaller recombination areas have been identified in RNA-dependent polymerase on RNA.
The recombination location is also identified in nsp9, most nsp10 and part of nsp14.
Similarly, MERS-CoV epidemics have been shown to experience a recommunitation incident between different descendants, which occurs in Arabic camels in Saudi Arabia.
In addition to SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV, recombinations have also been observed in other HCOVs, whit has seen HCoV rejoining with other animal CoVs in non-structural genes.
Warnings should also be provided that false selections can contribute to accidental changes in the genome of the virus, which is most likely resulting from freeing the virus from the pressure of the selection exerted, such as the host's immune system.
An example of this effect is the loss ORF4 full length in the thread of the HCoV-229E prototype, as a result of extinguition of two nucleotides.
While strong ORF4 can be seen in bat viruses and camels associated with HCoV-229E, the alpha-CoV alpaka displays the insertion of a single nucleotide, which results in skeletal displacement.
Finally, the evolution of the novel HCoV is also driven by the pressure of the selection in their reservoir hosts.
Asymptomatics or mild symptoms alone have been detected when bats are infected with CoV, signaling mutual adaptation between CoV and bats.
It seems that the bat adapts well tomanically and psychologically.
For example, damage in the activation of pro-inflammatory responses in bats efficiently reduce pathologies triggered by CoV.
In addition, the activity of natural killer cells in bats is constrained due to the increased regulation of NKG2/CD94 natural killer cell receptors and a low level of expression for a class 1 molecular main hystoceration complex.
Furthermore, high levels of reactive oxygen (ROS) species generated from high-matuset metabolic activity of bats can both resist the multiplication of CoVs and affect pruprupt readings by exoribonucleases, thus putting pressure on the selection for the care of highly pathogenic virus strains when introduced to a new hose.
More strains of pathogenic CoV may also evolve through reincorporation, leading to the acquisition of novel proteins or protein characteristics for hosting adaptation.
Therefore, it is no coincidence that three novel HCoVs have appeared in the last two decades.
COV is non-pathogenic or causes mild symptoms in their reservoir hoses such as bats and camels.
It doubled quickly without triggering a strong hose’s immune response.
This is where the secrets of asymptomatic carriers are seen and the cause for severe cases in human infections.
Severe symptoms are mostly due to the activation of hyperimmune response and cytokine storms where the stronger the immune response, the more severe lung damage.
On the other hand, in asymptomatic carriers, the immune response has been companded from the multipling of CoV.
Similar strategies that disseminate an immune response may have beneficial effects in SARS-CoV-2 anti-therapies.
Interferon response is very strong in bats.
Therefore, the administration of type 1 interferon at least in the early phase of SARS-CoV-2 infection in humans should be beneficial.
Moreover, inflamasom activation in bats is ineffective.
For this reason, NLRP3 inflamasom inhibition with MCC950 may be useful in treating COVID-19.
The emergence of SARS-CoV-2 follows a common theme of the emergence of SARS-CoV and MERS-CoV.
Although bat beta-CoV that shares 95% nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV has been discovered, there are also bat CoVs that share 96% nucleotide homology with SARS-CoV-2.
Although foxes and other animals in the market have been found to have a virus similar to SARS-CoV, the direct intermediary host for SARS-CoV-2 has not yet been identified.
Highly homologous pangolin beta-CoV with SARS-CoV-2 has been discovered, signaling that pangolin may be one of the intermediary hosts or beta-Cospinols can contribute gene fragments to the final version of SARS-CoV-2.
Despite questions, there is no evidence that SARS-CoV-2 was produced by humans intentionally or unintentionally.
COV has returned to attention due to the SARS-CoV-2 outbreak recently.
The study of CoV in bats and other animals has drastically changed our views on the importance of zoonotic origin and animal reservoirs for HCoV in human transmission.
Thorough evidence has shown that SARS-CoV, MERS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 have the origin of bats and are transferred to humans through intermediary hosts.
Since the SARS-CoV infection is caused by contact between humans with foxes in the market, closing the wet market and killing a fox should be able to effectively end the SARS epidemic.
For the same reason, pangolins should be removed from wet markets in order to prevent the spread of zoonotics, due to the discovery of various hereditaries of pangolin beta-CoV closely related to SARS-CoV-2.
However, the weather and the way SARS-CoV-2 is transferred to humans through pangolins and other mammals need to be explained in future investigations.
On the other hand, MERS-CoV has existed in Arabic camels for a long time.
This camel has become an essential tool for transportation as well as the main sources of meat, milk, skin and wool products for the local community.
This cast is found throughout the Middle East and Africa.
It is therefore impossible to sacrifice all camels to control MERS, as admitted in wild animal markets in China to prevent the spread of SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2.
To prevent the re-inflection of the MERS outbreak, a comprehensive approach should be taken to develop an effective vaccine against MERS-CoV for camels, combined with other infection control measures.
Since we can't get rid of this virus, new genotypes may appear to cause an outbreak.
A variety of zoonotic CoVs are scattered in the forest.
Mainly, bat CoV with very diverse zoonotic potential.
There are many opportunities for this zoonotic CoV to evolve and rejoin, resulting in the emergence of a more contagious and/or can kill in humans in the future.
The culture of eating wild animals in some parts of China should be stopped to reduce unnecessary contacts between humans and animals.
With the ordeals of SARS, MERS and COVID-19, preparations and improved response plans should be made.
In fact, many viruses have existed on this planet for a long time.
The virus stays in a natural reservoir until there's a chance to spread.
Although bats have many characteristics that facilitate the spread of the virus, the chances of humans having contact with bats and other wildlife species can be minimized if the community is educated to stay away from them.
Continuous monitoring in mammals is necessary to better understand the ecology of CoV and its natural hoses, which will be proven useful in preventing the transmission of animals to humans as well as outbreaks in the future.
In conclusion, the most effective way to prevent viral zoonosis is with humans away from natural reservoir ecological nic for zoonotic viruses.
Some of the questions about the zoonotic origin of SARS-CoV-2 are still unanswered.
Firstly, if bats transmit the SARS-CoV-2 heritage virus to pangolins, it will be interesting to see how bats and pangolins can share the same ecological nic.
Secondly, if bats play a more leading role in transmission to humans, we have to determine how humans get in contact with bats.
Thirdly, if the third mammal acts as a real intermediary host, how it interacts with different species including humans, bats and pangolin should be clarified.
Finally, since many mammals including exotic animals may be exposed to SARS-CoV-2, both experimental monitoring and infection should be carried out.
Whether bats, pangolins or other mammals, it is expected that SARS-CoV-2 or a typical parent virus will be identified in its natural hoses in the future.
Continuous investigations in this area will explain the evolutionary route of SARS-CoV-2 in animals, with important implications in the prevention and control of COVID-19 in humans.
Updating diagnostic criterions "suspicable cases" and "certificated cases" for COVID-19 is required
On 6 February 2020, our team has published fast advisory guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of novel coronavirus infections 2019 (2019-nCoV), and these guidelines provide our experience as a good reference to fight this pandemic around the world.
Nevertheless, coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) is a new disease, our awareness and increased knowledge slowly based on ongoing research findings and clinical practice experiences; therefore, diagnosis and treatment strategies are also continuously updated.
In this letter, we've answered a review on our guidelines and prepared the latest diagnostic criteria for "suspicious cases" and "confirmed cases" in accordance with the latest COVID-19 Diagnosis and Treatment Guidelines (the seventh version) issued by the National Health Committee of the People's Republic of China.
In December 2019, the novel coronavirus 2019 (2019-nCoV) has caused an outbreak, which is now officially named coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and the virus has been named severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
On 11 March 2020, WHO announced COVID-10 as a pandemic.
To fight the SARS-CoV-2 infection, our team has developed rapid advisory guidelines and such guidance has been published in Military Health Reviection on February 06, 2020.
The guide has attracted a lot of attention since it was published.
However please note that COVID-19 is a new disease, our awareness and knowledge increases slowly based on ongoing research findings and clinical practice experiences; therefore, diagnosis and treatment strategies to be continuously updated.
For example the Guidelines for Diagnosis and Treatment for COVID-19 issued by the National Health Committee of the People's Republic of China (http://www.nhc.gov.cn/), between 16 January 2020 to March 3, 2020, has released a total of seven editions with some actually altered context.
Now our guidelines received reviews by Zhou et al., they introduced simple scoring recommendations based on their clinical experience.
Their efforts to add new evidence for our guidelines and also become a valuable reference for this pandemic around the world.
We recognize their important efforts and thank you.
However, their efforts will also require updates in accordance with the latest Diagnosis and Treatment Guidelines for COVID-19 (Sextential trial edition) and the latest studies.
According to the seventh edition (March 3, 2020), to confirm a suspected case requires a combination of any one item from an epidemiological historical feature with two items of clinical manifestations to produce a thorough analysis, or need to meet three items of clinical manifestations if without a clear epidemiological history:
Epidemiological history: (1) The history of travel or living in the city of Wuhan and the surrounding area, or other communities where COVID-19 cases were reported within 14 days before the symptoms began; (2) the history of contact with SAR-CoV-2 cases (with a positive nucleic acid test); (3) the history in relation to a patient with fever or respiratory symptoms from the city of Wuhan and the surrounding area, or other communities where COVID-19 has been reported within 14 days before the symptoms begin; (4) the history in relation to the cluster of confirmed cases(> 2 cases with fever and/or respiratory symptoms occurred within 2 weeks in a small area.
Clinical manifestations: (1) fever and/or respiratory symptoms; (2) with the imaging characteristic of COVID-19 infection; (3) the number of white blood cell count indicates normal, impaired, or reduced lymphocytes in the early initiatial stages.
The confirmed diagnosis of cases should be dependent on a suspected case with any pathogenic item or cerological evidence as follows: (1) a positive real-time PCR test for SARS-CoV-2; (2) genomic sequence of the entire virus shows a high homogenetity to known novel coronavirus; (3) positive for IgM antibodies and IgG antibodies specific to SARS-CoV-2 in a serum test; or the exchange of SaRS-CoV-2 specific IgG antibodies from negative to positive, or an increase of titer > 4 times in the recovery phase above the acute phase.
We can see that real-time PCR tests for nucleic acid in the respiratory tract or blood samples have been added to the second edition (18 January 2020) and third (22 January 2020).
Pathogenic detection of the blood samples was inserted into the fourth edition (27 January 2020) and the fifth (8 February 2020); and then serological evidence was inserted into the seventh edition.
This modification is based on the ongoing work of researchers looking for optimal nucleic acid detection kits for immediate detection, as well as samples from the respiratory ducts including blood samples, which increase the availability of different specimens, and supports bringing positive antibody results specifically into confirmed criteria.
In addition, there is an increasing number of evidence that reminds us to be alert to typical or asymptomatic symptomatic patients.
Therefore, flowcharts from Zhou et al. should be updated, as they classify individuals without clinical symptoms as "low-risk".
The score system should also be confirmed in practice and further clinical studies.
In conclusion, we hope that more direct evidence will come and urge readers to comment.
For the diagnosis of "suspected cases" and "confirmed cases", we recommend to detect and adhere to new guidelines of their countries of origin.
Our team will also update our guidelines from time to time to offer assistance.
Bangladesh reports five new deaths due to COVID-19, the highest daily number
Yesterday, Bangladesh confirmed five new deaths due to COVID-19 that day.
This is the highest death toll in a day due to a virus.
As of yesterday, the Institute of Epidemiology, Control and Outbreak Study (IEDCR) had reported the total number of infections including 114 active cases and 33 recoverive cases sitting at home.
A total of 17 deaths have been recorded.
In an online news briefing, IEDCR's director, Dr Meerjady Sabrina Flora, said the deaths included four men and a woman.
According to Dr Meerjady, two cases were aged over 60, two of which were 51 and 60, and 41-50 years.
He also said that two victims were from Dhaka.
The World Health Organization (WHO) declared COVID 19 a pandemic on March 11.
Hospital officials told a local news source, Anadolu Agency that one of them was Jalal Saifur Rahman, director of the Bengali Anti-Corruption Commission, who was being treated at Kuwait Maitree Hospital.
On Saturday, in an online video announcement, Bangladesh's Minister of Road and Bridge Transport Obaidul Quader said public transport would be closed longer than its original plans, until next Saturday.
The closure of public transport was originally started on March 26 and was planned to end on Saturday, April 4.
The transport of essential goods -- medicines, oil and food -- is still allowed.
The 1st COVID-19 infection incident recorded in Bangladesh was on March 8, in two people returning from Italy as well as the wife of one of them.
As of March 19, all three have recovered.
SARS-CoV-2 exceeds one million infections worldwide.
On Thursday, the total number of SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus cases exceeded one million worldwide, according to data from Johns Hopkins University.
At least 52 thousand deaths are linked to COVID-19, an outbreak caused by the coronavirus.
These benchmarks were present on the same day that Malawi confirmed the first coronovirus infection and Zambia had the first coronavirus-linked deaths.
North Korea claimed that as of Thursday, it was among several countries that were free from coronavirus infections.
As of yesterday, the World Health Organization reported 1,051,635 confirmed cases, including 79,332 cases in twenty-four hours leading 10 a.m. Central European Time (0800 UTC) on April 4.
In the United States, more than 244 thousand coronavirus cases were reported, contributing to at least 5,900 deaths.
According to CBS news, citing data from Johns Hopksins University, more than 1,000 deaths in the U.S. on Wednesday due to coronavirus infections.
Around the world, countries announced stricter measures to stop the outbreak from spreading.
On Thursday, Sergie Sobyanin, the head of the city of Moscow, extended the city's closure until May 1.
Across the country, President Vladimir Putin declares that Russian citizens may continue to be paid without going to work until April 30.
Portugal's parliament voted to extend the state of emergency to 15 days; votes were validated with 215 votes in favour, ten abstinence, and one opposition.
Saudi Arabia extends curfews in the holy cities of Mecca and Medina to the rest of the day; previously, curfew was only imposed between 3 p.m. and 6 a.m.
Thailand intends to impose a curfew between 10 p.m. and 4 a.m.
Ohio Governor Mike DeWine announced that the state had extended a stay-at-home order until May 1.
