The World Health Organization declares COVID-19 a pandemic
On Wednesday, the World Health Organization (WHO) COVID-19 outbreak is occurring — a disease caused by the SARS-CoV-2 coronavirus — as a pandemic.
While the word "pandemic" only refers to the extent that the disease has spread, not how dangerous the particular case is, the WHO recognizes the need to urge the government to act:
All countries can still change the course of this pandemic.
If countries detect, test, treate, track and mobilize their citizens to act, said Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus, WHO Director-General.
We are very concerned about both levels of contagion and alarming severity as well as alarming levels of non-action.
According to Dr. Tom Frieden, former director of the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, the pandemic was "never happened."
He said in an excerpt published by CNN in February, "Apart from influenza, no other respiratory viral virus has been detected from the emergence to an ongoing worldwide transmission."
Ghebreyesus expressed a similar view, saying "we've never seen a pandemic triggered by the coronavirus."
He continued, “and we’ve never seen a pandemic that can be controlled at the same time.”
New status as a pandemic follows WHO's decision in January to announce the outbreak as an international public health emergency.
Dr. Anthony Fauci, director of the U.S. National Institute of Allergies and Infectious Infectious Diseases, "In short, the situation will worsen."
As of Thursday, the Associated Press reported there were at least 126,000 COVID-19 cases around the world, causing more than 4,600 deaths.
The 2019–20 coronavirus pandemic is an ongoing coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic, due to severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
The outbreak has been identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019, announced as an International General Health Emergency on 30 January 2020, and was declared a pandemic on 11 March 2020.
As of 10 April 2020, about 1.61 million COVID-19 cases have been reported in 210 countries and regions, resulting in approximately 97,000 deaths.
About 36,000 people have recovered.
The death toll rate has been estimated at 4% in China, while worldwide between 13.04% in Algeria to .08% in New Zealand.
Typical symptoms include fever, coughing and shortness of breath.
Complications may include pneumonia and acute respiratory distres syndrome.
The time from exposure to the onset of symptoms is usually about five days, but it may range from two to fourteen days.
There is no vaxis or known specific antiviral treatment.
The main treatment is symptomatic and supportive therapy. Proposed preventive measures include hand washing, closing the mouth during coughing, maintaining distance from others, and monitoring and self-isolation for those who suspect they are infected.
Authorities around the world have responded by imposing travel restrictions, quarantines, curfews, workplace hazard controls and facility closures.
This pandemic has led to global socio-economic disruptions, the suspension or cancellation of sporting, religious, political and cultural events, as well as extensive supply shortages driven by panic buying.
Schools and universities have been closed nationwide or at the local level in 193 countries, affecting about 99.4 percent of the world's student population.
Misinformation about the virus has circulated online, and there has been incidents of xenophobia and discrimination against the Chinese, other people descendants and the appearance of East and Southeast Asia, as well as those from areas with significant viral cases.
As a result of travel reductions and heavy industry closures, there was a decline in air pollution as well as carbon emissions.
Health authorities in Wuhan, China (the capital of Hubei province) reported a cluster of cases of pneumonia from an unknown cause on December 31, 2019, and an investigation was launched in early January 2020.
Most of these cases are linked to the Huanan Seafood Wholesale Market and caused the viral to be thought to have zoonotic origin.
The virus that caused the outbreak was known as SARS-CoV-2, a newly discovered virus that was closely linked to the bat coronavirus, pangolin coronavirus, and SARS-CoV. The earliest known person experiencing the symptoms was later found to have fallen ill on December 1, 2019, and the person had no visible contact with the wet market cluster later.
For the initial cluster of cases reported in December 2019, two-thirds were found to be linked to the market.
On 13 March 2020, an unconfirmed report from the South China Morning Post suggested that a case detected back to 17 November 2019, in a 55-year-old patient from Hubei province, may be the first. On 26 February 2020, WHO reported that, during new cases reported according to China but suddenly increased in Italy, Iran and South Koea, the number of new cases outside China has exceeded the number of new cases in China for the first time.
There may be a corresponding number of unreported cases, especially in those with milder symptoms.
As of 26 February, relatively few cases have been reported among adolescents, with those aged 19 and below equal to 2.4% of cases around the world. United Kingdom’s chief scientific advisor, Patrick Vallance, estimates that 60% of the British population needs to be infected before effective herd immunity can be achieved.”
Case refers to the number of people who have been tested for COVID-19, and whose tests have tested positive according to official protocols.
As of March 23, none countries have tested more than 3% of its population, and many countries that have a formal policy of not testing those with only mild symptoms, such as Italy, the Netherlands, Spain, and Switzerland.
A study published on March 16 found that in China, as of January 23, an estimated 86% of COVID-19 infections have yet to be detected, and this unrecordicated infection is the source for 79% of recorded cases.
Statistical analysis published on March 30 estimates that the number of infections in Italy is greater than reported cases.
The initial estimate of an increase (R0) number for COVID-19 is 1.4 to 2.4.
A study published by U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention concluded that the number may be 5.7.
Most people with COVID-19.
For those who don't, the time of development of symptoms up to death is between 6 and 41 days, with the most common being 14 days.
As of 10 April 2020, approximately 97,000 deaths have been linked to COVID-19.
In China, as of February 5, about 80% of deaths are among those over the age of 60, and 75% have existing health problems including cardiovascular disease and diabetes. The official death count from the COVID-19 pandemic generally refers to people who have tested positive for COVID according to official protocols.
The actual number of deaths from COVID-19 may be much higher, as it may not include those who die without being tested - e.g. at home, in daycare centres, etc.
Partial data from Italy found that the excessive number of deaths during the pandemic exceeded the official COVID death count by a factor of 4-5x.
Women’s spokeswoman for the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) stated “We know “We know “the number of fatalities is a lack of estimation””, a statement supported by an anecdoted report on the U.
The first death outside mainland China occurred on February 1 in the Philippines, and the first death outside of Asia was in France on February 14.
As of February 28, beyond mainland China, more than a dozen deaths per country have been recorded in Iran, South Korea, and Italy.
As of March 13, more than forty countries and territories have reported deaths, on every continent except Antarctica. Several measures have been commonly used to state the quantity of deaths.
These figures vary by area and over time, as well as affected by the number of tests, the quality of the health care system, the choice of treatment options, the time since the initial epidemic, and population characteristics such as age, gender, and overall health. The death-to-case ration describes the number of deaths divided by the number of cases diagnosed in a given time interval.
Based on Johns Hopkins University statistics, the death-to-case global mortality ratio was 6.0% (97,039/1,617,204) as of April 10, 2020.
The number varies by region.
In China, the estimate for the death-to-case ratio decreases from 17.3% (for those with symptoms from 1–10 January 2020) to 0.7% (for those with symptoms began after 1 February 2020). Other measurements include case death rate (CFR), which reflects the percentage of those diagnosed who died from the disease, and the infection mortality rate (IFR), which describes the percentage of those infected (diagnosis and undiagnosed) who died from the disease.
These statistics aren't tied to time and follows a specific population of infection through case resolution.
A number of academics have tried to calculate this number for a specific population.
The Oxford University University's Evidence-Based Drug Centre estimates that the infection mortality rate for the pandemic overall is between 0.1% and 0.39%.
The top estimates for this range are consistent with the results from the first randomized trials for COVID-19 in Germany, and statistical studies that analyze the effect of the test on CFR estimates.
WHO asserts that the pandemic can be controlled.
The peak and peak periods of the epidemic are uncertain and can vary according to the location.
Maciej Boni of Penn State University stated, "Leaven unchecked, infectious outbreaks are usually horizontal and then begin to deteriorate when the disease runs out of available hosts.
But it is almost impossible to make any reasonable projections now about the time the situation will happen."
China's senior medic adviser Zhong Nanshan argued that "the pandemic could end by June" if all countries could be mobilised to comply with WHO's advice on measures to halt the spread of the virus.
On March 17, Adam Kucharski from the London School of Hygiene & Tropical Medicine stated that SARS-CoV-2 "will continue to circulate, possibly for a year or two".
According to an Imperial College study led by Neil Ferguson, physical distancing and other measures will be required "until the vaccine is available (possibly 18 months or more)".
William Schaffner from Vanderbilt University stated, “I don’t think it’s possible—because it’s very contagious—will disappear completely” and it "may be a seasonal disease, reappearing every year”.
The harm of the reappearance will depend on herd immunity and the degree of mutation.
The symptoms of COVID-19 can be relatively non-specific and infected people may be asymptomatic.
The two most common symptoms are fever (88%) and dry cough (68%).
Less common symptoms include fatigue, respiratory sputum production (speakness), loss of sense of smell, shortness of breath, muscle and joint pain, sore throat, headache, cold, vomiting, hemoptisis, diarrhoea or cyanosis. The WHO states that about one in six people become very sick and have difficulty breathing.
U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) lists emergency symptoms as breathing difficulties, pain or constant pressure on the chest, sudden confusion, difficulty waking up, and eyes or lips become bluish; immediate medical treatment is advised if these symptoms present. An increase in the disease can further lead to severe penumonia, acute respiratory distres syndrome, sepsis, septic shock and death.
Some infected people may be asymptomatic, with no clinical symptoms but with the results of tests confirming the infection, so the researchers have issued advice that those with close contact with an infected person should be closely monitored and checked to make sure there is no infection.
China estimates asymptomatic ratios range from slightly to 44%.
The normal incubation period (the time between infections and symptoms begins) ranges from one to 14 days; most commonly five days. For example uncertainty, the estimated breakdown of people with COVID-19 who lost their sense of smell was initially 30% and then decreased to 15%.
Some details about how the disease spread is still being determined.
The disease is believed to spread primarily during close contact and through small droplets produced during coughing, sneezing, or talking; with close contact at a distance of 1 to 2 meters (3 to 6 feet).
Studies have found that unprotected cough will led the droplets to travel up to 4.5 meters (15 feet) to 8.2 meters (27 feet).
Some people have suggested that the virus may also be transferred through small droplets that remain for a longer period of time in the air, which may be generated while speaking. Respiratory drops may also result when removing, including while talking, even if the virus is generally non-aired.
Those drops may land in the mouth or nose of a nearby person or may have been inhaled into the lungs.
Some medical procedures such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) can cause respiratory secretions to become sprained and therefore produce airborne dissemination.
The virus can also diffuse when a person touches contaminated surfaces, including the skin, and then they touch their eyes, nose or mouth.
Although there are concerns that the virus can spread through feces, this risk is believed to be low.
The Chinese government denied the possibility of transmitting stool into the mouth of SARS-CoV-2. The virus was most transmissible during the first three days after the onset of symptoms, but the infection may occur before the symptoms appear and at a later stage for the disease.
People have tested positive for the disease up to three days before the onset of symptoms suggests that transmission can occur before significant symptoms.
There are few reports of laboratory-confirmed asymptomatic cases, but asymptomatic transmission has been identified by several countries during contact tracing investigations.
European Centers for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) stated that although it is not so clear how easily the disease spreads, one usually infects two to three others. The virus lasts for hours to days on the surface.
In particular, the virus was found to be detectable for up to three days on plastic (polypropylene) and 304 stainless steel, for a day on cardboard, and for up to four hours on copper.
This, however, varies based on humidity and temperature. Pets and other animals have tested positive for COVID-19.
There is no evidence that animals can transmit the virus to humans, but the British authorities have advised to wash their hands after touching animals, such as after touching other surfaces that may have been touched by those infected.
Severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2) is a novel of the virus, first isolated from three people with pneumonia associated with a cluster of cases of acute respiratory diseases in Wuhan.
All features of the novel of the SARS-CoV-2 virus are related to coronaviruses in nature. Outside the human body, the virus is killed by a home soap, which destroys its protective sheath.SARS-CoV-2 is very closely related to SARS-CoV origin.
The virus is believed to have a zoonotic origin.
Genetic analysis has revealed that the coronavirus was genetically clustered with the genus Betacoronavirus, in the subgenus Sarbecovirus (hereditary B) along with two threads derived from bats.
The virus is 96% similar at full genome levels with other bat coronavirus samples (BatCov RaTG13).
In February 2020, Chinese researchers found that there was only one amino acidic difference in certain parts of the genome sequence between the nangolin and the viral from humans.
Differences in the entire genome to date have found at least 92% of genetic material being shared between pangolin coronavirus and SARS-CoV-2, which is insufficient to provite pangolins as intermediate hosts.
Infection by the virus can be temporarily diagnosed based on symptoms, even if the authenticator eventually goes through reversible transcription of the polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) for infected secretions or CT imaging.
A study comparing PCR with CT in Wuhan had suggested that CT was significantly more sensitive than PCR, although less specialized, with most of its imaging properties overlapping with the pneumonia process and other diseases.
As of March 2020, the American College of Radiology recommended that "CT should not be used for filtering or as a first-line test to diagnose COVID-19".
WHO has published several RNA testing protocols for SARS-CoV-1, with the first issued on January 17.
The test used real-time polymerase (rRT-PCR) reversible transcription.
This test can be carried out on respiratory or blood samples.
Test results are usually available within a few hours to a few days.
Usually this test is carried out with a nasopharyngeal swab but a throat swab can also be used. A number of laboratories and companies are developing serological tests, which detect antibodies.
As of 6 April 2020, none of these tests have been proven accurate enough to be approved for widespread use.
In the U.S. serological tests developed by Cellex have been approved for emergency use by-certifiated laboratories only.
Features of radiograph and tomography (CT) features for people with symptoms include asymmetric peripheral ground appeagles and non-pleural effusion.
The Italian Radiological Association is gathering an international online database to search for imaging for confirmed cases.
As a result of overlap with other infections such as adenovirus, unconfirmed by PCR has a limited specialization in identifying COVID-19.
A large study in China compared chest CT results with PCR and showed that although the imaging was less specific to the infection, the method was faster and more sensitive, suggested consideration of the method as a screening device in the epidemic area.
An artificial intelligence-based circular neural network has been developed to detect viral imaming chies with both radiographs and CTS.
Strategies to prevent the spread of the disease include maintaining overall good personal hygiene, hand washing, avoid touching the eyes, nose, or mouth with unwashed hands, and coughing or sneezing on the tissue and placing the tissue directly into the garbage container.
Those who may have already been infected have been advised to wear surgical masks in public places.
Physical distancing measures have also been proposed to prevent transmission. Many governments have been restricting or advised to avoid all non-significant travel to and from countries as well as areas affected by the pandemic.
However, the virus has reached community transmission in large parts of the world.
This means that the virus is dispersed in the community, and some community members don’t know where or how they’re infected. Health care providers who care for someone who may be infected are recommended to use standard precautions, contact precautions, and eye protection. Contact tracking is an important method of determining the source of infection and to prevent further transmission.
The use of location datages from mobile phones by the government for this purpose has raised concerns about privacy, with Amnesty International and more than 100 other organisations issuing statements calling for limits on such oversight.
A variety of mobile apps have been implemented or proposed for voluntary use, and as of April 7, 2020, more than a dozen groups of experts are working on privacy-friendly solutions, such as using Bluetooth to log around a user with another cell phone.
Users then receive a message if they've been in close contact with someone who has tested positive for COVID-19. The wrong response is circulating on how to prevent infection; for example, rinsing of the nose and rinsing with mouthwash is ineffective.
There are no COVID-19 vaccines, although many organizations are working to develop vaccines.
Washing hands is recommended to prevent the spread of the epidemic.
The CDC recommends that people wash their hands regularly using soap and water for at least twenty seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when the hands appear dirty; before eating; and after exhaling the nose, cough, or sneezing.
This is because outside the human body, the virus is killed by house soap, which destroys its protective bubble.
The CDC further recommends using alcohol-based hand sanitization with at least 60% of alcohol according to content when soap and water aren't available for use.
THE WHO advises people to avoid touching the eyes, nose, or mouth with unwashed hands.
The surface may be contaminated with a certain amount of solution (within a minute exposure to infedigent for stainless steel surfaces), including 62–71% ethanol, 50–100% isopropanol, 0.1% sodium hypochlorite, 0.5% hydrogen peroxide, and 0.2–7.5% povidon-iodine.
Other solutions, such as benzalkonium chloride and chlorexidine gluconate, are less effective.
The CDC recommends that if COVID cases are suspected or verified in facilities such as offices or day care centers, all areas such as offices, bathrooms, general areas, shared electronic equipment such as tablets, touch screens, keyboards, remote controls and ATM machines used by a sick person, should be decommissioned.
Health organizations recommend that people cover their mouths and noses with bented sleeves or tissues during coughing or sneezing, and remove any tissues immediately.
Surgical masks are recommended for those who may be infected, since wearing a mask can limit the amount and distance of expiracy drops that are dispersed while talking, sneezing, and coughing.
WHO has issued instructions on the time and how to use a mask.
According to Stephen Griffin, virologist at the University of Leeds, "Wearing masks can reduce people's tendency to touch their faces, which is the primary source of the infection without proper hand hygienic care.
WHO has recommended wearing masks by those who are healthy only if they are at high risk, such as those caring for people with COVID-19, although they also admit that wearing a mask can help people avoid touching their faces.
Several countries have begun to encourage the use of face masks by community members.
In the U.S., the CDC recommends wearing non-medical face masks made of fabric. China has specifically recommended the use of disposable medical masks by members of a healthy society, especially in close contact (1 meter (3 feet) or less) with others.
Hong Kong recommends wearing surgical masks while using public transport or in crowded places.
Thailand's health official encourages people to make cloth face masks at home and wash them every day.
The Czech Republic and Slovakia have prohibited going out in public without wearing a mask or covering a person's nose and mouth.
On March 16, Vietnam asked everyone to wear a face mask when going to public places to protect themselves and others.
The government of Austria determined that everyone who enters the grocery store must wear face masks.
Israel has askepted all residents to wear face masks in public.
Taiwan, which has produced ten million masks daily since mid-March, asked inter-city train passengers and buses to wear face masks on April 1.
Panamana has made it mandatory to wear face masks when going out, while also recommending the manufacture of face masks at home for those who are unable to buy face masks.
Face masks have also been widely used in Japan, South Korea, Malaysia and Singapore.
Social distancing (also known as physical distancing) is accompanied by infection-controlled actions aimed at slowing the spread of diseases by minimizing close contact between individuals.
Methods include quarantine; travel restrictions; and closure of schools, workplaces, stadiums, theaters or shopping malls.
Individuals can implement social distancing methods by staying at home, limit travel, avoid crowded areas, use contactless greetings, and physically distance themselves from others.
Many governments are now mandating or recommend social distancing in regions affected by the outbreak.
The size of the maximum assembly recommended by U.S. government bodies and health organizations is quickly reduced from 250 people (in the absence of known COVID-19 outbreaks in the region) to 50 people, and then to 10 people.
On 22 March 2020, Germany banned public gatherings by more than two. Older adults and those with hidden health problems such as diabetes, heart disease, respiratory diseases, hypertension, and the compromised immune system face increased risks for serious diseases and complications as well as have been advised by the CDIC to stay at home as possible in areas with community outbreaks. At the end of March 2020, WHO and other health bodies began to replace the term “social distancing” with “physical distancing”.
The use of the term “social distancing” has led to the implication that people should undergo social segregation completely, instead of encouraging them to walk in contact with others through an alternative way. Some have issued sexual health guidelines for use during the pandemic.
This includes recommendations for having sex only with someone who lives with you, who does not have the viral or symptoms of the virus.
Segregation at home has been proposed to those who have been detected with COVID-19 and those suspected to have been infected.
Health agencies have issued detailed instructions for proper self-isolation. Many governments have mandated or proposed self-quarantine to the entire population living in the affected areas.
The strictest self-quarantine instructions have been issued to those who belong to high-risk groups.
Those who may have been exposed to someone with COVID-19 and those who had previously travelled to countries or regions with widespread spreads have been advised to undergo self-quarantine for 14 day from the possible time of the final exposure.
The strategy in controlling an epidemic is curbing or suppressing, and reduction.
Containment is carried out in the early stages of the outbreak and aims to track and isolate those infected as well as introduce infection control and other vaccination measures to stop the disease from spreading to the remainder of the population.
When it is no longer possible to curb the spread of the disease, efforts then move on to the reducing stage: measures are taken to slow the spread and reduce its impact on the healthcare system as well as the community.
A combination of cursing and reducing measures may be carried out at the same time.
Stress requires stricter measures to reverse the pandemic by reducing the number of basic breeding to less than 1. Part of managing the spread of infectious diseases is trying to lower the epidemic peak, known as leveling the epidemic curve.
This reduces the risk of health services being overloaded and providing more time for the vaccine as well as the treatment to be developed.
Non-pharmaceutical interventions that can manage contagion include personal preventive measures, such as hand hygiene, wearing face masks, and self-quarantine; society's measures targeted at physical distancing such as shutting down schools and cancalling large-scale events; community engagement to encourage acceptance and participation in such interventions; so are environmental measures such as surface cleaning. More drastic actions are targeted to curb contagion have been taken in China’ after the severity of the pandemic became clear, e.g.
Other countries have also taken a variety of measures to limit the spread of the virus.
South Korea introduced large-scale screening and local quarantination, as well as issued warnings about the movement of infective individuals.
Singapore provides financial support to infected people who quarantine themselves and impose high fines on those who fail to do so.
Taiwan increases the production of face masks and fined the hideout of medical supplies. Simulations for Great Britain and the United States indicate that the reduction (slowing but does not halt the spread of the epidemic) and the emphasis (reversing the growth of the epidemic) has great challenges.
Optimimulation reduction policies may reduce health care claims that peak to 2/3 and death by half, but still cause hundreds of thousands of deaths and health systems to become burdened.
Ansopisation can be an option but needs to be maintained as long as the virus circulates in the human population (or until the vaccine exists, if it comes first), as the dissemination will quickly bounce back when preventive measures are relaxed.
Long-term intervention to depress the pandemic results in social and economic costs.
No antiviral drugs are specifically approved for COVID-19, but efforts to develop them are underway, including testing existing drugs.
Consuming flu medications given over the over-the-counter, drinking water and resting will help alleviate the symptoms.
Depending on the severity, oxygen therapy, intravenous fluid and respiratory support may be required.
The use of steroids may aggravate the consequences.
Some compounds that were previously approved for the treatment of other viral diseases are being found for use in the treatment of COVID-19.
WHO also stated that some "traditional and at-home treatments" can provide relief to symptoms caused by SARS-CoV-19.
The increased capacity and adaptation of health care for the needs of COVID-19 patients is described by the WHO as a basic epidemic response measure.
ECDC and WHO's European territories offices have issued guidelines for primary hospitals and healthcare services to mobilize resources at various levels, including focusing laboratory services towards COVID-19 testing, nullifying elective procedures when necessary, separating and separating COVID-19-positive patients, as well as improving intensive care capabilities by training personnel and increasing the number of available ventilators and beds.
There are various theories about the place of the first case (which is called an vacant number patient) originated.
The first known case of the novel coronavirus can be traced back to December 1, 2019 in Wuhan, Hubei, China.
Within a month, the number of coronavirus cases in Hubei is slowly increasing.
This case is mostly associated with the Huanan Seafood Wholesale Market, which also sells live animals, and one theory is that the virus originates from one of these types of animals; or, in other words, has zoonotic origins. An unknown lung cluster the cause has been observed on December 26 and was treated by Zhang Jixian's doctor at the Hubei Province Hospital, who informed the CDC Wuhan Jianghan on December 27.
On December 30, a group of doctors at the Wuhan Central Hospital informed their co-workers about "coronaviruses such as SARS".
These eight doctors, including Li Wenliang, have been alerted by the police for spreading false rumours, and another, Ai Fen, was scolded by his superiors for raising the matter.
The Wuhan Municipal Health Commission then issued a public notice on December 31 and informed the WHO.
Sufficient cases of unknown pneumonia have been reported to health authorities in Wuhan to trigger an investigation in early January. In the early stages of the outbreak, the number of multiple cases is approximately every seven and a half days.
In early and mid-January 2020, the virus was spread to other Chinese territory, aided by China's New Year migration and Wuhan's transportation hub as well as a major railway exchange site.
On January 20, China reported nearly 140 new cases in a day, including two people in Beijing and one in Shenzhen.
Official data later showed that 6,174 people had already shown symptoms as of 20 January 2020. As of March 26, the United States has surpassed China and Italy with the highest number of confirmed cases in the world. As of April 9, 2020, more than 1.61 million cases have been reported worldwide; more than 97,000 people have died and more than 364,000 people have recovered.
About 200 countries and territories already have at least one case.
As a result of this pandemic in Europe, many countries in the Schengen Area have limited free movement and implemented border controls.
As of April 2, nearly 300 million people, or about 90% of the population, are under a form of a state of emergency in the United States, more than 50 million people are in an emergency in the Philippines.
On March 26, 1.7 billion people around the world were in the form of a state of emergency, rising to 2.6 billion people two days later—about one-third of the world's population.
The first confirmed COVID-19 case was traced back to 1 December 2019 in Wuhan; an unconfirmed report suggested that the earliest case was on November 17.
Doctor Zhang Jixiang observed an unknown cluster of pneumonia cases on December 26, then his hospital informed CDC Wuhan Jianghan on December 27.
An initial genetic test for a sample of patients on December 27, 2019 showed the presence of coronavirus such as SARS.
A public notice was issued by the Wuhan Municipal Health Commission on December 31.
WHO was announced on the same day.
During this notification, doctors in Wuhan were alerted by the police for "spreading rumours" about the outbreak.
China’s National Health Commission initially claimed that there was no "climinous evidence" of human transmission.
At the end of January, the Chinese government launched a radical campaign that was later described by Chinese Communist Party secretarity chief Xi Jinping as a "people's war" to prevent the spread of the virus.
In a situation that has been dubbed "the largest quarantine in human history", the enclosure of quarantine was announced on 23 January that halted travel into and out of Wuhan, which has been extended to a total of 15 cities in Hubei, affecting a total at least 57 million people.
The use of private vehicles has been banned in the city.
China's New Year (January 25) was cancelled in many places.
Authorities also announced the construction of the temporary hospital, Huoshenshan Hospital, which has been completed within 10 days.
Another hospital was built afterwards, Leishenshan Hospital, to handle additional patients.
In addition to the newly built hospitals, China also renovated 14 other facilities in Wuhan such as convention centers and stadiums, into temporary hospitals. On January 26, the government embarked on further steps to contain the COVID-19 outbreak, including issuing health declarations for travelers and extending the Spring Festival holidays.
Universities and schools across the country are also closed.
The provinces of Hong Kong and Macau initiated a number of steps, specifically related to schools and universities.
Long-than-the-art working measures have been started in parts of China.
Travel restrictions are planned inside and outside Hubei.
Public transport has been renovated, and museums across China have been temporarily closed.
Civil movement control has been implemented in many cities, and it has been estimated about 760 million people (more than half of the population) are faced with some types of external restrictions. After outbreaks entered the global phase in March, the Chinese government took strict measures to prevent the virus from being "imported" from other countries.
For example, Beijing has enforced a mandatory 14-day quarantine for all international travelers entering the city. On March 23, Mainland China is just a case of domestic dissemination within 5 days, in this example the traveler returns to Guangzhou from Istanbul.
On 24 March 2020, Chinese Prime Minister Li Keqiang reported that domestic contagion cases were fundamentally blocked and that the transmission was controlled by China.
China’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs announced on March 26, “2020” that entry by visa or residents’ permit holders would be suspended from March 28 and beyond, without specific details of the policy’s time.
Those wishing to enter China need to apply for a visa at China's embassy or consulate.
The Chinese government encourages businesses and factories to reopen on March 30, and the provision of financial stimulus packages to firms. The State Council declared a day of mourning starting with three minutes of silent time at 10:00 April 4, in accordance with the Qingming Festival, although the federal government asked the families to show respect online in compliance with physical distancing to prevent a repeat of the COVID-19 pandemic.
COVID-19 was confirmed to have spread to South Korea on January 20, 2020 from China.
The national health agency reported a significant increase in confirmed cases on February 20, largely linked to a rally in Daegu from a new religious movement known as Jesus Shincheonji Church.
Shincheonji, who visited Daegu from Wuhan, is believed to be the cause of transmission.
As of February 22, some 9,336 followers of the church, 1,261, or about 13% reported symptoms. South Korea declared its highest level warning on 23 February 2020.
On February 28, more than 2,000 confirmed cases were reported in Korea, rising to 3,150 cases on February 29.
All South Korea's military base has been quarantined after testing confirmed that three military personnel tested positive for the virus.
The airline's schedule has also been disrupted. South Korea introduces a program that is considered to be the world’s largest and most orderly program to filter populations to detect the virus, and isolate any infected individual as well as tracking and quarantining people contact with them.
Screening methods include mandatory self-report reporting of symptons by new international arrivals via mobile applications, drive-through virus testing with results available the next day, and an increase in testing capabilities allowing up to 20,000 people to be tested daily.
South Korea's program is thought to be successful in controlling the pandemic despite not quarantinizing the entire city. The South Korean community was initially divided against President Moon Jae-in’s response to the crisis.
Most Koreans signed a petition whether to demand Moon's dismissal for what they claimed to be a violation by the government against the pandemic, or praised his response.
On March 23, it was reported that South Korea had the lowest daily number of cases in four weeks.
On 29 March it was reported that starting 1 April all new overseas arrivals will be quarantined for two weeks.
As reported by the media on April 1, South Korea has received requests for virus testing assistance from 121 different countries.
Iran reported its first-verified SARS-CoV-2 infection on February 19 in Qom, which, according to the Ministry of Health and Medical Education, two people died later that day.
Preliminary preventive measures announced by the government include the cancellation of concerts and other cultural events, sporting events, and Friday prayers, and the closure of universities, institutes of higher education, and schools.
Iran allocates five trillion rials to fight the virus.
President Hassan Rouhani said on February 26, 2020 that there were no plans to quarantine the areas affected by the outbreak, and only individuals would be quarantined.
Plans to limit inter-city travel were announced in March, although crowded traffic between cities before the Persian New Year Nowruz continued.
Shia eating in Qom remains open to visitors until March 16, 2020. Iran is the hub for the spread of the virus after China in February.
Despite allegations about the level of the outbreak in Iran being protected, more than ten countries have tracked their cases of returning to Iran by February 28, suggesting that levels of the outbreak may be worse than 388 cases reported by the Iranian government as of that day.
Iran's parliament has been closed, with 23 of its 290 members reported to have tested positive for the virus on March 3.
On March 12, Human Rights Watch urged Iranian prisons to unconditionally release human rights fighters detained for peaceful protests, and to also release all eligible inmates temporarily.
It stated that there is a greater risk for the virus to spread in closed institutions such as detention centres, which also lack adequate health care.
On March 15, Iran's government reported 100 deaths in a day, the country's largest number recorded since the outbreak.
At least 12 politicians and government officials or formers have died from the disease until March 17.
As of March 23, Iran is experiencing 50 new cases per hour and one new deaths every ten minutes due to the coronavirus.
According to WHO officials, there may be five times the number of cases in Iran than those reported.
It also suggested that U.S. sanctions on Iran may affect the country's financial ability to respond to the virus outbreak.
The UN High Commissioner for Human Rights has called for economic sanctions to be eased for countries most affected by the pandemic, including Iran.
The outbreak was confirmed to have spread to Italy on January 31, when two Chinese tourists tested positive for SARS-CoV-2 in Rome.
Cases began to rise rapidly, prompting the Italian government to suspend all flights into and from China and announced emergencies.
Unrelevant COVID-19 case clusters were later detected, starting with 16 confirmed cases in Lombardy on 21 February. On 22 February, the Cabinet announced a new decree law to contain the outbreak, including quarantining more than 50,000 people from 11 different municipalities in northern Italy.
Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte said, "In outbreak areas, entry and exit will not be allowed.
The suspension of work and sporting events has already been directed in the area. "On March 4, the Italian government ordered the complete closure of all schools and universities across the country as Italy reaches 100 deaths.
All major sporting events, including Serie A football matches, were supposed to be held behind closed doors until April, but on March 9, all sports were fully suspended for at least one month.
On 11 March, Prime Minister Conte ordered the discontinuation of almost all commercial activities except supermarkets and pharmacies. On 6 March, the College of Anaesthesia, Analgesia, Resuscitation and Intensive Intensive (SIAARTI) published medical ethical recommendations regarding triage protocols that may be used.
On March 19, Italy overthrewen China as the world's highest-grossing coronavirus-related deaths after reporting 3,405 deaths from the pandemic.
On 22 March, it was reported that Russia had sent nine military aircraft with medical equipment to Italy.
As of April 5, there were 128,948 confirmed cases, 15,887 deaths, and 21,815 recovered in Italy, with the majority of such cases occurring in Lombardy province.
A CNN report stated that a large combination of an elderly population in Italy and the inability to test all those infected with the viral virus today may be a contributor to high mortality rates.
The United Kingdom's response to the virus first emerged as one of the quietest affected countries, and until 18 March 2020, the British government did not impose any form of social distancing or massive quarantine measures on its citizens.
As a result, the government received criticism because it was seen as less speed and seriousness in its response to the concerns faced by the public. On 16 March, Prime Minister Boris Johnson made an announcement that advised to avoid all non-essential travels and social contacts, proposing citizens to work from their homes whenever possible and avoiding places such as pubs, restaurants, and theaters.
On 20 March, the government announced that all leisure places such as pubs and gyms should be closed as soon as possible, and promised to pay up to 80% of workers’ salaries up to £2,500 per month to avoid unemployment in the crisis. On 23 March, the Prime Minister announced stricter social distancing measures, prohibiting gatherings of more than two people and restricting travel and outdoor activities to those that are determined absolutely necessary.
Unlike previous measures, these restrictions were enforced by the police through the issuance of summonses and rallies.
Most businesses are ordered to close, with the exception for businesses deemed "important", including supermarkets, pharmacies, banks, hardware stores, petrol stations, and workshops.
On January 20, the first known COVID-19 case was confirmed in the Pacific Northwest Washington state in men who had just returned from Wuhan on January 15.
The White House Coronavirus Task Group was established on January 29.
On January 31, the Trump administration announced a general health emergency, and placed restrictions on entry for travellers from China.
On January 28, 2020, the U.S. government’s leading Center for Disease Control—the U.S. government—announced they developed their own set of tests.
Despite doing so, the U.S. is starting to slow in testing, which is protecting the actual rate of outbreaks at the moment.
Tests were affected by faulty test kits produced by the federal government in February, the lack of government approval for non-government test kits (academia, company and hospital releases) until the end of February, and strict criteria for people to qualify for testing until early March (doctor’s instructions are required thereafter).
As of February 27, The Washington Post reported that less than 4,000 tests had been conducted in the United States.
As of March 13, The Atlantic reported less than 14,000 tests had been carried out.
On March 22, Associated Press reported: "Many people who have experienced symptoms and doctor's instructions have been waiting for hours or days for the test." After the first death in the United States was reported in the Washington state on February 29, Governor Jay Inslee announced an emergency, an action shortly thereafter followed by another state.
Schools in the Seattle area cancelled classes on March 3, and by mid-March, schools across the country were closed. On March 6, 2020, the United States was projected for the impact of the new coronavirus on the country by a group of epidemiologists at Imperial College London.
On the same day President Trump signed the Additional Provisions Act for Coronavirus Preparation and Responsion, which provides $8.3 billion in emergency funding for government agencies to respond to the outbreak.
The company imposes employee travel restrictions, cancels conferences, and encourages employees to work from home.
Events and sports seasons were cancelled. On March 11, Trump announced travel restrictions for most areas in Europe excluding the United Kingdom, for 30 days, starting March 13.
The next day, he expanded the restrictions to include the United Kingdom and Ireland.
On March 13, he announced the state's emergency, which made federal funds available to respond to the crisis.
From March 15, many businesses are shutting down or reduced time across the U.S. to try to reduce the spread of the virus.
As of March 17, the epidemic has been confirmed in all 50 states and in the District of Columbia. On 23 March, it was reported that New York City had 10,700 coronavirus cases, more than the total number of cases in South Korea.
On March 25, the governor said that social distancing seemed as effective, when the estimated multiple cases began to slow from 2.0 days to 4.7 days.
As of March 28, there were 32,308 confirmed cases in New York City, and 672 people have died from the virus. On March 26, the United States reportedly had more confirmed coronavirus cases than any country in the world, including China and Italy. As of April 8, 400,3335 cases have been confirmed in the United States, and 12,841 people have died.
As media reports on March 30, U.S. President Trump has decided to extend social distancing guidelines until April 30.
On the same day, USNS Comfort, a hospital vessel with about 1000 beds, was anchored in New York.
On April 3, the U.S. had a record 884 deaths from the coronavirus in a 24-hour period.
In the state of New York the case exceeded 100,000 people on April 3. The White House has been criticized for narrowing threats and controlling messaging by instructing health officials and scientists to coordinate public statements and public publications on the virus with Vice-President Mike Pence's office.
The overall approval for Trump's management of the crisis was beyond partisan lines.
Some U.S. officials and commentators criticize the U.S. dependence over the importation of essential materials, including important medical supplies from China.
An analysis of air travel patterns has been used to map and predict the spread patterns and was published in The Journal of Travel Medicine in mid-January 2020.
Based on 2018 information from the International Air Transport Association, Bangkok, Hong Kong, Tokyo, and Taipei has the largest number of tourists from Wuhan.
Dubai, Sydney and Melbourne are also reported to be popular destinations for those travelling from Wuhan.
Baline is reported to be incapable among the 20 most popular destination cities in terms of availability, while the city in Australia is considered the most capable. Australia released the Emergency Response Plan for the Novel Coronavirus (COVID-19) on 7 February.
It states that much is not yet known about COVID-19, and that Australia will emphasize border control as well as communication in response to the pandemic.
On March 21, human biosafety emergency was declared in Australia.
Considering the implementation of public transport quaranties in Wuhan and Hubei, several countries have planned to evacuate their citizens and diplomatic staff from the area, generally via flights chartered by their countries of origin, with Chinese authorities granting permission.
Canada, the United States of America, India, Sri Lanka, Australia, France, Argentina, Germany, and Thailand are one of the earliest countries to plan to evacuate their citizens.
Pakistan has said it will not evacuate any of its citizens from China.
On February 7, Brazil evacuated 34 Brazilians or their family members in addition to four Polish people, a Chinese, and an Indian.
The people of Poland, China, and India were lowered in Poland, where the Brazilian plane stopped by before proceeding to Brazil.
