Japan donated one million face coverings to Wuhan, Turkey shipped more than 13 tonnes of medical supplies to Whuhan, Malaysia announced the donation of 18 million medical gloves to China, Germany shipped various medical supplies including 10,000 Hazmat's clothes, and the United States donated 17.8 tonnes of medical assistance to China and promised an additional $100 million in financial assistance to the affected countries.
In March, China, Cuba and Russia sent medical aid as well as specialists to help Italy handle the coronavirus outbreak.
Businessman Jack Ma shipped 1.1 million test kits, 6 million face masks, and 60,000 protective clothing to Addis Ababa, Ethiopia to be distributed by the African Union.
He then sent 5,000 test kits, 100,000 face masks and 5 ventilators to Panama.
The Netherlands, Spain, Turkey, Georgia, and the Czech Republic expressed their concerns over Chinese-made masks and test kits.
For example, Spain withdrew 58,000 Chinese-made coronavirus test kits with an accuracy rate of only 30%, while the Netherlands recalled 600,000 damaged Chinese face masks.
Belgium withdrew 100,000 unusablishable face masks, which were thought to be from China, but actually from Colombia.
On the other hand, China’s assistance has been well received in parts of Latin America and Africa. On April 2, the World Bank launched an emergency support operation for developing countries.
WHO has praised the Chinese government's efforts in managing and controlling the epidemic.
WHO observed the differences between the 2002-2004 SARS outbreak, when the Chinese government was accused of secrecy to the detriment of preventive and regulatory efforts, as well as the current crises that saw the central government "have provided regular updates to avoid panic before the Chinese New Year holidays".
On January 23, in response to the central government's decision to impose transportation restrictions in Whuhan, WHO representative Gauden Galea stated that although it was "necessarily non-recommendations made by the WHO", it was also a "very important indicator of the commitment to controlling the epidememic where it was very concentrated" and calling it "unprecedence in the history of public health."
WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom said that PHEIC is the result of "the risk of global transmission especially to low- and middle-income countries without a robust health system.
In response to the implementation of travel restrictions, Tedros stated that "there is no reason for the unnecessary for travel and international trade" and "WHO does not recommend limiting trade and movement."
On February 5, WHO appealed to the global community to get a $675 million dollar donation to fund strategic preparations in a low-income country, citing an interest in supporting the country "does not have a system available to detect individuals who have been infected with the virus, if they appear".
Tedros further made a statement declaring that "we are as strong as our weakest chain" and urged the international community to "invest today or paying more later". On 11 February, the WHO in a press conference designated COVID-19 as the name of the disease.
On the same day, Tedros said that UN Secretary-General Antonio Guterres had agreed to "power the entire U.N. system as a response".
As a result a UN Crisis Management team has been activated, enabling the overall coordination of the United Nations response, which the WHO said would allow them to "focus on health responses while other agencies can bring their expertise to bear the wider adverse effects of social, economic and development as a result of the pandemic".
On February 14, A WHO-led China Joint Mission Team was activated to provide international expertise and the WHO's field in China to assist in domestic management and assess "the severity and fluency of the disease" by holding workshops and meetings with major national-level institutions as well as conducting an ottal visit to assess "the effects of regional and village-level response activities, including the urban and rural environments".
In response to the ongoing outbreak in Iran, the WHO sent a Joint Mission Team there to assess the situation. On February 28, WHO officials said that a world-level coronavirus threat assessment would be raised from "high" to "highest", the highest level of warning and risk assessment.
Mike Ryan, executive director of WHO's health emergency program, warned in a statement that "It's a review of the reality for every government on the planetix: Wake up.
The virus is probably on the go and you should be prepared, "urging that the right-responsible measures is capable of helping the world avoid "worst conditions".
Ryan further stated that current data does not allow general health workers to proclaim a global preacher, such as a proclamation means "we fundamentally accept that all humans on this earth will be exposed to the virus."
On March 11, WHO declared that the coronavirus outbreak was a pandemic.
Director-General said the WHO “very concerned with both levels of contagion and alarming severity, and alarming levels of inactivity””. WHO has been faced with tangible criticism for things that are seen as improper handling of the pandemic, including delays in the declaration on public health emergencies and the classification of the virus as a pandemic.
Condemnances included a petition for WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom to resign, signed by 73,000 people until April 6.
On 26 March 2020, dozens of UN human rights experts stressed the rights of each individual during the COVID-19 pandemic.
The expert group stated that everyone is entitled to receive life-saversing intervention and that the government holds this responsibilities.
The group emphasized that the lack of health resources or insurance is not an excuse for discrimination against certain groups.
Experts outline that all individuals have the right to health, including people with disabilities, members of minority groups, the elderly, bedriddened people, homelessness, those living in very difficult conditions, detainees, as well as refugees and other groups in need of government assistance.
International government organizations are dealing with the effects of the COVID-19 crises on the economy and social.
The organization for Economic Cooperation and Development has launched a platform to provide timely and comprehensive information about policy feedback in countries around the world, as well the point of view and advice.
From policies to strengthen the world's health and economic systems to discuss the effects of emergencies and travel restrictions, digital hubs including National Policy Detection, and aims to help countries learn from each other as well as facilitate global responses coordinated to coronavirus challenges.
The Chinese government has been criticized by the U.S., the UK's Minister for the Cabinet Office of Michael Gove and the son of Brazilian President Jair Bolsonaro Eduardo Bolsonaro following the handling of the pandemic, which began in Hubei province, China.
Regional officials from the Communist Party of China (CPC) have been sacked due to the handling of quarantine at central China, as a dissatisfaction of the political organization's response to the spread in the region.
Some commentators believe that the movement aims to protect Chinese Communist Party secretary chief Xi Jinping from public outrage from the spread of the coronavirus.
Several Chinese officials e.g. Zhao Lijian rejected an early notification that the coronavirus outbreak had begun from Wuhan, agreeing to a conspiracy theory about COVID-19 originated in the U.S. or Italy.
The U.S. administration under Donald Trump has referred to the coronavirus as the "Chinese virus" or "Wuhan virus" saying that "China's fiscination has produced a virus that has now turned into a global pandemic", which was later criticized by several critics as racist and "trying to divert attention away from its administration's failure in curbing the disease".
The Daily Beast acquired U.S. government channels that outlined communication tricks with clear origins in the National Security Council, with the strategy referred to as "Everything is about China.
"Outlets such as Politico, Foreign Policy and Bloomberg have claimed that China's efforts to send aid to countries affected by viruses are part of a propaganda effort to influence the world.
EU foreign policy leader Josep Borrell has warned that there is "a geo-political component including a struggle to gain influence by distorting and "cheerful politics".
Borrell also said "China aggressively pushes the message that, unlike the U.S., it is a responsible and reliable partner."
China has also urged the U.S. to lift sanctions from Syria, Venezuela and Iran, when reportedly sending aid to the last two countries.
The contribution of 100,000 face masks from Jack Ma to Cuba was hampered by U.S. sanctions on April 3.
U.S. authorities have also been accused of diverting assistance for other countries to their own countries.
And there are mask-related disputes reported among other countries, such as Germany, Austria and Switzerland; and the Czech Republic and Italy.
Moreover, Turkey seized hundreds of ventilators heading to Spain.
In early March, the Italian government criticized the European Union's lack of solidarity with Italy affected by the coronavirus.
Maurizio Massari, Italy's ambassador to the EU, said "Only China gave a two-sided answer.
Of course, this is not a good signal of European solidarity."
On 22 March, after a phone call with Italian Prime Minister Giuseppe Conte, Russian President Vladimir Putin ordered Russian troops to send military medical forces, special disinfecting vehicles and other medical equipment to Italy.
Italian newspaper La Stampa cited "high-level political resources" anonymously saying that 80 percent of Russia's aid was "less useful or little useful to Italy".
The source accuses Russia of committing "geopolitical and diplomatic" charm.
Lombardy President Attilio Fontana and Italian Foreign Minister Luigi Di Maio rejected the media's reports and expressed their appreciation.
Russia also sends cargo aircraft with medical assistance to the United States.
Kremlin Dmitry Peskov spokesman said that "while offering assistance to partners in the U.S., [Putin] assumed that when U.S. medical equipment and material manufacturers gain momentum, they would also be able to respond if necessary."
"Defender 2020" NATO's planned "Defender" in Germany, and the Balkan states, the largest NATO warfare exercise since the end of the Cold War, will be held on a smaller scale.
"In the current public health crisis, it endangers the lives of not only soldiers from the U.S. and many other European countries that take part but also the inhabitants of the country where they operate." The Iranian government has been severely affected by the virus, with about two dozen members of parliament as well as fifteen current or other former political figures.
Iranian President Hassan Rouhani wrote an open letter to world leaders seeking help on March 14, 2020, saying that his country was struggling to fight the pandemic due to a lack of access to the international market as a result of U.S. sanctions on Iran. The epidemic has called for a call to the United States to adopt common social policies in other wealthy countries, including universal healthcare, universal childcare, paid family holidays, and higher levels of funding for public health.
Political analysts expect the situation to have a negative impact on Donald Trump's chances of being re-elected in the 2020 presidential election. Diplomatic relations between Japan and South Korea have worsened due to the pandemic.
South Korea criticized "unclear and passive quarantine efforts" after Japan announced anyone coming from South Korea would be placed in a two-week quarantine on a government-determined site.
The South Korean community was initially not clear by President Moon Jae-in's response to the crisis.
Many Koreans have signed a petition calling for Moon's dismissal for what the government's mis-operated against the pandemic, or praised his response. The pandemic has allowed the country to pass emergency laws in return.
