In Hungary, his parliament voted to allow the prime minister, Viktor Orbán, to rule according to decrees permanently, suspend parliament as well as elections and punish those deemed to have disseminated misinformation about the virus and the handling of the crisis by the government.
The coronavirus outbreak has been blamed for a number of supply shortages, stemming from the use of equipment to fighting an outbreak around the world, panic buying, and disruptions to factories as well as logistical operations.
The U.S. Food and Drug Administration has issued warnings about the lack of medicine and medical equipment due to increased consumer demand as well as supplier disorders.
Some areas have also seen panic purchases that cause smooth shelves from essential items such as food, toilet wipes, and bottled water, causing a lack of supply.
The technology industry in particular has warned about delays in the delivery of electronic goods.
According to WHO director-general Tedros Adhanom, the demand for personal protective equipment has multiplied.
This demand has led to an increase in prices up to twenty-ficient prices and also led to a delay in the supply of medical items for four to six months.
