Short-term work compensation schemes have been implemented by France and Britain.
The performing arts and cultural heritage sectors have been deeply affected by the pandemic, impacting the organization’s operations as well as individuals - both working payroll and free-to-earnestries—all over the world.
Art and cultural sectors are trying to enhance their mission (often public-funded) to provide access to cultural heritage to the community, maintain the safety of their workers and the community, as well as supporting artists whenever possible.
As of March 2022, around the world and at varying levels, museums, libraries, display venues, and other cultural institutions have closed exhibitions, events and shows being cancelled and postponed until an indefiniable time.
In feedback, there are intensive measures to provide alternative services through a digital platform. Another recent and increasingly plummeting fall as a result of the pandemic is the cancellation of religious services, major events in sports, and other social events, such as music and concert festivals, technology seminars, and fashion shows.
The film industry was also affected. The Vatican announced that the celebration of the Holy Weak in Rome, which took place during Lent's last week of Christian repentant season, had been annulled.
Many bishops have recommended elderly Christians to stay at home instead of attending the Church Rime on Sunday; some churches have conducted church services via radio, live streaming online or television while others offer drive-through worship.
With the Roman Catholic Bishop of Rome closing churches and challies as well as St. Peter's plains vacated from Christian congregations, other religious bodies also cancelled services and limited public gatherings in churches, mosques, synagoges, temples and gudwaras.
Iran's health minister announced Friday's revocation of prayers in areas affected by outbreaks and tommums was later closed, while Saudi Arabia prevented the entry of foreign pilgrims and residents into the holy sites of Mecca and Medina.
The pandemic has resulted a very significant disruption in sports calendars around the world since the Second World War.
Most major sporting events have been either cancelled or postponed, including the 2019–20 UEFA Champions League, 2019–20 Premier League, UEFA Euro 2020, the 2019–20 NBA season and the 2019–20 NHL season.
The outbreak undermined plans for the 2020 Summer Olympics, which was initially scheduled to begin at the end of July; the International Olympic Committee announced on March 24 that the event would be "rescheduled to the date after 2020 but did not exceed the summer of 2021′′′′′”. Casinos and gambling venues around the world have been closed as well as live poker matches have either been postponed or cancelled.
This has caused many gamblers to shift online, with many online gambling sites reporting a significant increase in their new enrollment rates. The entertainment world has also been affected, with various bands suspending or cancing tours.
Many big theaters such as theatrices on Broadway also suspended all performances.
A number of artists have explored ways to continuously produce and share work over the internet as an alternative to traditional live performances, such as live-streaming concerts or creating web-based "festivals" for artists to present, distributing and introducing their works.
Online, a lot of coronavirus-the-themed Internet memes have spread as many turn to humor and entertainment despite uncertainty.
Since the outbreak of COVID-19, increased prejudice, xenophobia and racism have been observed against people of Chinese and East Asian descent, and against people from hot sites in Europe, the United States and other countries.
Incidents of fear, suspicion, and roughness have been observed in many countries, particularly in Europe, East Asia, North America and the Asia-Pacific region.
Reports from February (when the majority of cases remain limited in China) have recorded racist sentiment depicted in various groups around the world saying Chinese people are eligible for the viral virus or accept things claimed to be fair retaliation.
Some countries in Africa have also seen an increase in anti-China sentiment.
Most residents of Wuhan and Hubei have reported discreparation based on their territory.
There is support for China, both inside and offline, as well as against them in areas severely hit by the virus.
Following the development of an outbreak into a new-hot country, people from Italy, the first country in Europe to experience a serious COVID-19 outbreak, could also be a victim of suspicion and xenophobaia. Citizens including Malaysia, New Zealand, Singapore and South Kerose initially signed a lobbying petition to ban Chinese citizens from entering their countries in an effort to stop the disease.
In Japan, the hashtag #ChineseDontComeToJapan went viral on Twitter.
Chinese and other Asians in the United Kingdom and the United States have reported levels of racist disturbances, as well as rising attacks.
U.S. President Donald Trump has faced criticism for referring to the coronavirus as "China's virus", a term considered by critics as racist and anti-China.
Protesters in Ukraine attacked a bus carrying Ukrainians and foreign transferers from Wuhan to Novi Sanzhary.
Students from the Northeast of India, who share borders with China, and study in India's top cities have reported experiencing disruptions related to the coronavirus outbreak.
President of the Bharatiya Janata State unit in West Bengal Dilip Ghosh said that the Chinese people have destroyed the environment and "that's why God retaliated for their deeds."
In China, xenophobia and racism against the non-Chinese population have been madified by the pandemic, with foreigners being described as "foreign garbage" and targeted for "removal".
Many newspapers with paid ads have removed them for some or all of their coronavirus coverage.
Many scientific publishers make epidemic-related scientific papers available with open access.
Some scientists choose to share their results immediately on preprint servers such as bioRxiv.
