Laboratory tests for respiratory coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) and SARS-CoV-2 viruses associated include methods for detecting the presence of the virus as well as methods that detect antibodies produced in response to infection.
The presence of the virus in the sample is confirmed by RT-PCR, which detects coronavirus RNA.
This test is specifically designed only to detect SARS-CoV-2 virus RNA.
The test is used to confirm a very new or active infection.
Detection of antibodies (serology) can be used for both the diagnosis and supervision of the population.
Anti-anti-antibody tests showed the number of patients with the disease, including those whose symptoms were too mild to report or asymptomatic.
The rate of fatalities due to an accurate disease and the level of herd immunity in the population can be determined from the results of this test.
As a result of the limited tests, as of March 2020 no country has the reliable data about the prevalence of the virus in their population.
As of March 23, none countries have tested more than 3% of their population, and there is abundant diversity in the number of tests that have been carried out across countries.
This diversity is also likely to significantly affect the death rate of reported cases, which may be significantly overestimated in some countries.
Using real-time polymerase chain reaction (rRT-PCR) transcription can be performed on respiratory samples obtained through a variety of methods, including nasopharyngeal swabs and sputum samples.
The results are usually available within a few hours to 2 days.
An RT-PCR test performed with swabs of the throat can only be trusted in the first week of the disease.
Then the virus can disappear in the throat while constantly multiplying in the lungs.
For those infected who are tested in the second week, alternatively the sample material can then be taken from the deep air route using an inhalation catheter or a cough-causing substance (sputum) can be used.
One of the earliest PCR tests was developed in Charité in Berlin in January 2020 using real-time polyemerase (rRT-PCR) reversible transcription, and became the basis for 250,000 kits for distribution by the World Health Organization (WHO).
The United Kingdom has also developed tests by 23 January 2020. South Korean company Kogeneiotech is developed PCR-based SARS-CoV-2 detection kid, cylinical grade (PowerChek Coronavirus) on 28 January 2020.
It finds the ��“E” gene shared by all beta coronaviruses, and the RdRp gene dedicated to SARS-CoV-2. In China, BGI Group was one of the first companies to receive emergency use approvals from China’s National Medical Products Administration’s “PCR’s” for SARS-COV-based PCR’s detection kit.
One of the three genetic tests in an older version of the test kit resulted in uncertain results due to a faulty reagent, and a bottle neck test at the CD's in Atlanta; this produced on an average of less than 100 samples per day successfully processed throughout February 2020.
The two-companiment test is uncertainably reliable up to 28 February 2020, and prior to that state and local laboratories have not been allowed to start testing.
The test has been approved by the Food and Drug Administration under Emergency Use Authorization. The U.S. commercial labors began testing in early March 2020.
As of 5 March 2020, LabCorp announced the availability of COVID-19 tests based on RT-PCR nationwide.
Quest Diagnostics also makes COVID-19 testing available nationwide from 9 March 2020.
No quantity limit has been announced; the collection of specimens and processing must be carried out in accordance with CDC requirements.
In Russia, COVID-19 tests have been developed and produced by VECTOR National Virology and Biotechnology Research Center.
On 11 February 2020 the test was registered by the Federal Service for Supervision in Health. On 12 March 2020, the Mayo Clinic was reported to have developed tests to detect COVID-19 infections. On 13 March 2020, Roche Diagnostics received FDA approval for testing that could be done within 3.5 hours in a high number, thus allowing one machine to perform approximately 4,128 tests in 24 hours.
As of March 19, 2020, the FDA issued an emergency use authorization (EUA) to Abbott Laboratories for testing of Abbott’s m2000 systems; the FDA had earlier issued similar authorizations to Hologic, LabCorp, and Term Fisher Scientific.
As of March 21, 2020, Cepheid also accepted the EUA from the FDA for testing that took about 45 minutes.
The FDA has approved tests that use isothermal nucleic acid magnification technology instead of PCR.
As this test does not require a series of a fluctuating temperature cycle of this method can give a positive result within five minutes and a negative result within 13 minutes.
There are currently about 18,000 of these machines in the U.S. and Abbott expects to accelerate production to deliver 50,000 tests a day. Tests using monoclonous antibodies that are specifically binding to proteon nucleocapsid (N protein) novel coronavirus is being developed in Taiwan, in the hope that the test can provide results within 15 to 20 minutes just like a rapid influenza test.
In March 2020 an inscription commenting that "the bradiography of the chest had minor diagnostic values at an early stage, while CT[computerized tomography] findings may have been present even before the symptoms began."
Typical CT features include bilasis multilowar soil opacities with peripheral, asymmetric and posterior distributions.
Subpleural dominations, crazy openings and incorporations are formed when the disease evolves.
A study comparing PCR to CT in Whahu at the original point of the current pandemic had suggested that CT was significantly more sensitive than PCR, although less specialized, with many of its imaging features acting with the pneumonia process and other diseases.
As of March 2020, the American Radiological College recommends that "CT should not be used for filtering or as a first-line test to diagnose COVID-19". As of March 2020, the CDC recommends PCR for initial screening.
Part of the immune response to infection is the production of antibodies including IgM and IgG.
This can be used to detect infections in an individual starting 7 days or next after symptoms begin, to determine immunity, and in population surveillance. Evaluations can be done in a central laboratory (CLT) or through point-of-care (POCT testing).
An automatic high-trancing system in many clinical laboratories will be able to carry out this assessment but its availability will depend on the production rate for each system.
For CLT single specimens of peripheral blood are usually used, although serial specimens can be used to follow immune responses.
For single-blood specimen PoCT is usually obtained by puncture on the skin.
Unlike the PCR method of the practification measure was not required prior to the evaluation. On March 26, 2020, the FDA named 29 entities that provided notifications to agencies as required and are therefore now able to distribute their antibody tests.
As of April 7, 2020, only one test was approved by the FDA under emergency use authorization. At the end of March 2020, Euroimmun Medical Laboratory Diagnostics and Epitope Diagnostics received European approvals for their test kits, which could detect IgG and IgA antibodies against viruses in blood samples.
The test capacity is several hundred samples within a few hours and is therefore much faster than conventional PCR assessment for viral RNA.
Antibodies can usually be detected 14 days after the infection begins. In early April, the U.K. found no antibody-tested kits purchased were good enough to use.
Hong Kong has prepared a schema where patients are suspected to be able to stay at home, “emergency’s department will provide specimen tubes”, spit into the tubue, sending them back and getting test results shortly thereafter. The British NHS has announced that it is pioneering the scheme to test a suspected case at home, which removes the risk of patients infecting someone else’s if they go to the hospital or need to disinfect an ambulance if used.
Drive-through centers have helped South Korea perform one of the fastest, most comprehensive tests compared to other countries. In Germany, the National Association of Health Insurance Physician Statutories said on March 2, that they had a capacity of 12,000 tests a day in an ambulatory setting and 10,7070 had been tested in the previous week.
Expension is borne by health insurance when tests are requested by a physician.
According to the President of the Robert Koch Institute, Jerrman has a total capacity of 160,000 tests per week.
As of March 19 drive-through tests have been offered in several big cities.
As of March 26, 2020, the total number of tests conducted in Germany is unknown, as only the positive results were reported.
The first laboratory survey revealed that up to the 12/2020 calendar week of at least 483,295 samples had been tested including the week of 12/2020 and 33,491 samples (6.9%) tested positive for SARS-CoV-2. In Israel, researchers at Technion and Rambam Hospitals developed and tested methods to test samples from 64 patients simultaneously, by collecting samples and only tested further if the combined samples were found to be positive.
With construction being monitored by BGI’s founder Wan Jian and takes 5 days, models have shown cases in Hubei will be 47% higher and the corresponding cost to handle the quarantine will double if the test capacity does not reach the target.
Wuhan laboratories have been immediately followed by Hou Yan laboratories in Shenzhen, Tianjin, Beijing, and Shanghai, in a total of 12 cities across China.
As of 4 March 2020 the total daily volume is 50,000 tests per day. Open source, multiplex designs issued by Origami Assays have been issued that can test a total of 1122 patient samples for COVID19 using only 93 assessments. This balanced design can be carried out in small laboratories without the need for robotic liquid operators.
As of March, inadequate shortages and number of reagents have made crowded for large-scale tests in the EU and in the UK and in the U.S.
This has prompted several authors to explore sample preparation protocols involving heating the sample at 98 C (208 F) for 5 minutes to remove the RNA genome for further testing. On March 31, it was announced that the United Arab Emirates is testing more of its citizens for the coronavirus of each head than any other country, and was on the right path to scale the test level to reach a large proportion of the population.
This is a combination of drive-through affordability, and the purchase of large-scale population-scale-scale-processing forces from Group 42 and BGI (based on "Hou-Yan" emergency detection laboratory in China)
Constructed within 14 days, the laboratory is capable of carrying out thousands of RT-PCR tests per day and is the first in the world on this scale to operate outside China.
Different test checkers targeting different parts of coronavirus genetic profiles have been developed in China, France, Germany, Hong Kong, Japan and the United States.
The World Health Organization adopts German methods for the production of kits sent to low-income countries without resources to develop their own kit.
The German method was published on January 17, 2020; a protocol developed by the U.S. Centers of Disassist is not available until January 28, delaying existing tests in the U.S. China and the United States had problems with the reliability of the test kit at the beginning of its initiation, and the country and Australia were unable to supply enough kits to meet the demands and recommendations for testing by health experts.
On the other hand, experts said South Korea’s broad readiness in testing helped reduce the spread of novel coronavirus.
The testing capacity, largely in the private sector laboratory, which was built in several years by the South Korean government.
On March 16, the World Health Organization called for increasing the testing program as the best way to slow the speed of the COVID-19 pandmace. High demand for tests due to the widespread spread of the viral virus led to the arrears of hundreds of thousands of tests in U.S. private laboratories, and the supply of chemical sweeps and regants became limited.
In March 2020 China reported problems with accuracy on their test kits.
In the United States, the test kits developed by the CDC had a “weake;” the government subsequently issued bureaucratic sanctions that had prevented private trials. Spain’s countries bought test kits from Chinese firm Shenzhen Biotechnology Co ltd, but found that the results were inaccurate.
The firm explains that inaccurate results may be the result of failure to collect samples or using the kit correctly.
Spain’s ministry said it would retract the kits that returned the wrong results, and would replace it with a different test kit provided by Shenzhen Bioeasy.80% of the test kit purchased the Chech Republic from China gave the wrong results. The Slovak country bought 1.2 million test kits from China that were found to be inaccurate.
Prime Ministr Matovic proposed that the kit be dumped to Danube.Ates Kara from the Turkish Ministry of Health told the test kits purchased by Turkey from China had a “high rate of offenses” and did not “use”.
Tests, followed by quarantinizing those who tested positive and detected those who were in contact with an individual positive SARS-CoV-2 in, provided positive results.
The researchers working in the Italian city of Vo, Italy's first COVID-19 death site, conducted two test rounds across a population of nearly 3,400, about ten days.
Nearly half of people who tested positive had no symptoms, and all cases have been quarantined.
With travel restrictions, this has eliminated new infections as a whole.
With aggressive contact tracing, inbound travel restrictions, testing, and quarantine, the 2020 coronavirus pandemic in Singapore has moved more later than other developing countries, but without strict restrictions such as forcibly closure of restaurants and grocery stores.
Many events have been cancelled, and Singapore has started advising its citizens to stay at home on March 28, but schools will be reopening in time after the holidays on March 23.
Several other countries have also handled the pandemic by aggressive contact tracing, inbound travel restrictions, testing and quarantine, but with less aggressive state of emergency, such as Iceland and South Korea.
A statistical study found that more countries are testing, relative to the death toll, have a low mortality rate, probably because these countries are able to detect those who only have mild or non-direct symptoms better.
WHO proposes that countries that do not have national testing and laboratory capacity with limited experience of COVID-19 sent the first five positive samples and ten COVID-19-negitive samples to one of the WHO’s 16 reference laboratories for validation testing.
Out of the 16 reference laboratories, 7 are available in Asia, 5 in Europe, 2 in Africa, 1 in North America and 1 in Australia.
In the following charts, the "Positive as % test" column is influenced by the country's testing policy.
Countries that only test hospitalized individuals will get a higher positive value as % of tests compared to countries testing all citizens, whether or not they show symptoms, the rest are the same.
Washing hands (or hand washing), also known as hand hygiene, is the act of cleaning a person’s hands intended to get rid of soil, fats, microorganisms, or other undesirable substances.
Washing hands with soap consistently at certain "critical times" during the day prevents the spread of many diseases, e.g. diarrhoea and cholera, which spread through the passage of feces into the mouth.
Individuals can also become infected with respiratory diseases such as influenza or the common cold, for example, if they don’t wash their hands before touching the eyes, nose, or mouth (e.g., mucous membranes).
Five critical times during the day witnessing hand washing with soap is important: before and after defecation, after cleaning the child's buttocks or changing diapers, before feeding the child, before eating and before and after preparing food or handling raw meat, fish or birds.
If water and soap are absent, the hands can be washed with ash. The World Health Organization recommends washing hands:
Before, during, and after preparing the food.
before and after controlling a sick person.
After changing diapers or cleaning children who have used the toilet.
After exhaling the nose, cough or sneezing.
After touching animals, animal feed, or animal feces.
Medical hand hygiene refers to sanitary practices related to medical procedures.
Washing your hands before administering medication or medical care can prevent or minimize the spread of the disease.
The main medical purpose of hand washing is to clean the hands of pathogens (bacteria, viruses or other microorganisms that can cause diseases) and chemicals that can cause harm or disease.
This is especially important to people who handle food or work in the medical field, but are also an important practice for the general public.
Washing hands has many health benefits, including reducing the spread of colds, coronaviruses and other infectious diseases; preventing infectious causes of diarrhoea; reducing respiratory infections;
and lower the rate of infant mortality at birth at home.
A 2013 study showed improved hand washing practices would lead to a small increase in high growth of children under the age of five.
In developing countries, the mortality rate in childhood associated with respiratory diseases and diarrhoea can be reduced by introducing simple behavioral changes, for example washing hands with soap.
This simple measure can reduce the mortality rate from this disease to almost 50%.
Interventions that organize hand washing can reduce episodes of diarrhoea by about a third, and this can be compared by providing clean water in low-income areas.
48% reduction in episodes of diarrhoea can be attributed to washing hands with soap. Hand washing with soap is the only effective and non-expensive way to avoid diarrhea and respiratory acute infections (ARIs), as an automated behavior done in homes, schools and communities around the world.
Pneumonia, the main ARI, is the number one cause of death among children under the age of five, kills about 1.8 million children a year.
Diarrhea and pneumonia together causes nearly 3.5 million child deaths each year.
According to UNICEF, instilcating the habit of hand washing with soap before eating and after using the toilet can save more lives than any single vaccine or a mixture of medicine, reduce the death from diarrhea nearly half and die from an acute respiratory infection of nearly a quarter.
Washiping is usually combined with other sanitation interventions as part of water, sanitation and sanitary (WASH) programs.
Washing can also protect against impetigo that is transferred through direct physical contact.
The disadvantageous small effect when washing your hands too often is washing your hands too hard can lead to skin damage due to dryness on the skin.
A Danish study in 2012 found excessive hand washing can lead to itching, a scaly skin condition known as hand eczema or hand dermatitis, which is common among health care workers.
Too often wash your hands is also seen as one of the symptoms of obsessive compulsive disorder (OCD).
There are five critical times throughout the day where washing hands with soap is important to reduce the transfer of fecal epidemics to the mouth: after using the toilet (urination, poop), after cleaning the child's buttock (converting diapers), before feeding and before/after preparing food or handling raw meat, fish or birds.
Other times whishing your hands properly should be practiced to prevent the spread of the disease including before and after treating cuts or wounds; after sneezing, coughing, or exhaling the nose; after touching the animal's feces or handling the animal; and after holding the garbage.
The rate of hand washing using soap is low in most countries.
In 2015, a handwashing study in 54 countries found that on average 38.7% of households had hands-washing practices with soap. In 2014, a study showed Saudi Arabia had the highest rate of 97 percent; the United States was almost in the middle with 77 percent; and China at the lowest rate of 23 percent. Some behavioral change methodologies now exist to improve hand washing with soap at critical times. School children wash hands in groups at the specified time is one of the options in developing countries to apply hand washing behavior in children.
The Essential Health Care programs implemented by the Department of Education in the Philippines are an example of large-scale actions to encourage children's health and education.
Deworming twice a year, coupled with hand washing daily with soap, and brushing your teeth every day with fluoride. is the core of this national program.
It has been successfully implemented in Indonesia.
Removation of microorganisms from the skin can be increased by adding soap or detergent to water.
The main action of soaps and detergents is to reduce barriers on the solution, and increase solubility.
Water alone isn’t an efficient skin cleanser due to fats and proteins, which are components of organic soil, are insoluble in water.
However, the proper flow of water helps to clean up.
Due to its reusable properties, lumpy soaps may store bacteria derived from previous uses.
A small number of studies on the transfer of bacteria from polluted lumps found that transplantation is unlikely to occur because the bacteria are discarded when washed with foam.
The CDC's site still states that "liquid soap removed without the use of hands is better".
Antibacterial soap gets a high promotion to people who care about health.
There is no evidence so far that using the recommended antiseptic or disinfectantly choose an antibiotic-resistant organism.
However, antibacterial soaps contain common antibacterial agents such as triclosan, which has a broad list of resistance to the organism's strains.
So, although antibody-resistant strains aren't selected by antibacterial soaps, they may not be as effective as they're marketed.
In addition to surfactants and skin protective agents, these advanced formulations may contain acids (acetic acid, ascorbic acid, lactic acid) as pH regulators, anti-microbial active benzoic acid and skin conditioners (aloe vera, vitamins, molt bulbs, plant extracts). A comprehensive analysis of the Faculty of Public Half at the University of Oregon shows that regular soaps have the same effect as consumer-class antibacterial soaps containing triclosants in preventing disease and removing bacteria from the hands.
It's no exaggeration to wash your hands not hot enough to kill bacteria.
Bacteria multiply faster at body temperature (37 °C).
However, soapy warm water is more effective than soapy cold water to get rid of natural oils that have soil and bacteria.
Unlike many people believe, scientific studies have shown that using warm water has no effect on reducing the microbial content on the hands.
A hand sanitizer is a non-wait-based hand sanitizer.
In the late 1990s and parts of the early 21st of the 21st century, non-water-based hand sanitizer (also known as alcohol-based rubbing, antiseptic rubbing, or hand sanitizer) began to become popular.
Most are based on isopropyl or ethanol alcohol formulated with thickening agents such as Carbomer (acrylic acid polymers) into a gel, or humecktan such as glycerin into a liquid, or foam for ease of use and to reduce the drying effect of alcohol.
By addicting diluted hydrogen peroxide further increase the antimicrobial activity. Hand sanitizer containing a minimum of 60 to 95% alcohol is an efficient germ remover.”
Rubbing alcohol kills bacteria, drug-resistant bacteria (MRSA and VRE), tuberculosis and some viruses (including HIV, herpes, RSV, rhinovirus, vaccinia, influenza and hepatitis).
Alcohol rubbing cleaners containing 70% of alcohol kill 99.97% (reducement of 3.5 logs, similar to a reduction of 35 decibels) of bacteria on the hands 30 seconds after use and 99.99% (reduction of 4 to 5 logs) of bacteria on the hands 1 minute after use. Hand sanitizer is most effective against bacteria and is less effective against some viruses.
Alcohol-based hand sanitizer is almost entirely ineffective against norovirus-type viruses (or Norwalk), the most common causation of infectious gastroenteritis. Sufficient hand antiseptics or alcohol rubbing must be used to wet or protect both hands correctly.
The front and back of both hands and between the ends of all fingers are rubbing for about 30 seconds until the liquid, foam or gel becomes dry.
Finger tips must also be well washed, by rubbing them on both palms. U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommend hand washing instead of hand-cleaning rubbing, especially when the hands look dirty.
The increasing use of this agent is due to its convenient use and rapid killing activity against microorganisms. However, it should not replace proper hand washing except when there is no soap and water.
Regular use of alcohol-based hand sanitizers can cause drying skin except emollients and/or skin moissures added to the formula.
Drying alcohol effects can be reduced or eliminated by adding glycerin and/or other emollients to the formula.
In clinical trials, an alcohol-based hand sanitizer containing emollients caused a significant reduction of irritation and dryness of the skin versus antimicrobial soap or detergent.
Allergic contact dermatitis, contact urticaria syndrome or hypersensitivity to alcohol or additives in alcohol rubbing is rare.
Tendency to further reduce contact dermatitis that irritates attracts rather than using soaps and water to wash their hands.
Non-water agents, although effective, don’t clean their hands from organic matter.
That's why hand sanitizers aren’t very effective compared to soaps and water to prevent the spread of many pathogens, as pathogens are still in the hands.
The effectiveness of an alcohol-free hand sanitizer depends heavily on its ingredients and its formulation, and historically it was less successful compared to alcohol and alcohol rubbing.
Lately, formulations that use benzalkonium chloride have been shown to have persistent antimicrobial activity and cumulatively after consumption, unlike alcohols that have been shown to decrease their effectiveness after being used many times, perhaps due to progressively inappropriate reactions on the skin.
Most low-income people can't afford soap, instead they use ashes or land.
Ash or soil may be more effective than using water alone but may be less effective than soap.
Anxiety exists if soil or ash is contaminated with microorganisms that may increase from reducing the spread of the disease.
Ash is also a disinfectant such as soap because it forms an alkaline solution in contact with water.
WHO recommends ash or sand as an an alternative to soap if soap is not available.
The U.S. Center for Disease Control recommends proper hand washing techniques to prevent the spread of diseases including the following steps:
Wet hands with water flowing warm or cold.
Running water is recommended because the standing basin may be contaminated, at the same time the water temperature does not seem to have an effect.
Wash your hands while rubbing it with a lot of soap, including the back of the hands, between the fingers, and under the nails.
Soap remove germs from the skin, and studies have shown that people tend to wash their hands more carefully when using soap instead of just with water.
Sent for at least 20 seconds.
Stingling creates a shift, which helps remove germs out of the skin, and squirrels for longer removing more germs.
Willy rinse with running water.
Rinsing in the basin can re-squeeze the hands.
Dry with a clean towel or let it dry by air.
Wet and damp hands are easier to get dirty again. The thumbs, wrists, parts between the fingers and the bottom of the toenail are the parts that are always overlooked.
Microorganisms may be stored in fake nails and flaky nail polish.
Moisturizing lotion is often recommended to prevent the hands from drying out; dry skin can cause skin damage that may increase the risk of infection transfer.
There is a variety of cheap options to facilitate hand washing if there is no tap water and/or soap, e.g. pouring water from hanging cans or pumpkins with suitable holes and/or using ash if necessary in a developing country. If the water supply is limited (such as in schools or rural areas in developing countries), there are water-saving solutions such as “tippi pipe” and other cheap options.
Typi pipes are simple technologies that use jag suspended with a rope, and lever operated by the feet to pour a small amount of water over the hands and a piece of soap.
An important part of the hand-cleaning process is an improper hand drying, but there is a debate about the most effective forms of drying in public bathrooms.
An increasing number of studies have shown that paper towels are much cleaner than electrical hand dryers found in most bathrooms.
In 2008, a study was conducted by the University of Westminster, London, and sponsored by the paper towel industry, the European Tissue Symposium, to compare the level of cleanliness when using paper towels, warm air hand dryers and more modern jet air hand dryers.
After washing and drying hands with a warm air dryer, the number of bacteria at the bottom of the fingertips was found on average increased by 194% and on the palm of the hands by 254%.
Drying with jet air dryers leads to an increase in the number of bacteria at the bottom of their fingertips by an average of 42% and on the palms by 15%.
After washing and drying hands with a paper towel, the amount of bacteria at the bottom of the end so on average was reduced by up to 76% and on the palms up to 77%. Scientists also conducted tests to determine whether cross-contamination could potentially occur from other bathroom users and bathroom environments from each type of drying method.
The jet air dryer, which exhales air out of the unit at the alleged speed of 180 m/s (650 km/h; 400 bsh), is capable of exhaling the microorganisms from the hands and the unit and has the potential to pollute other bathroom users and a 2-metre bathroom environment.
The use of warm air hand dryer spreads microorganisms up to 0.25 meters from the dryer.
Paper towels did not show significant dissemination of microorganisms. In 2005, a study made by TÜV Productst und Umwelt evaluated different hand drying methods.
The following changes are found in bacterial calculations after hand-drying:
There are many different hand dryer manufacturers, and hand dryers are compared with paper towels for drying.
Washing your hands using hand cleaning wipes is an alternative option while traveling when there is no soap and water.
Alcoholic hand sanitizer should contain at least 60% alcohol.
Washing hands for medicine has long been an obligation when doctor Ignaz Semmelweis of Hungary discovered its effectiveness (in 1846) in avoidance of diseases in the hospital environment.
There are electronic devices that alert hospital staff to wash their hands when they forgot.
A study found that its use could lower the rate of infection.
Wash hands in medicine for at least 15 seconds, using plenty of soap and water or gels to scrub and rub each part of the hands.
The hands need to be rubbed together with the fingers that are infused.
If there are stains under the nails, a bristle brush can be used to remove it.
As germs may remain in the water on the hands, we need to rinse it well and wipe them until dry with a clean towel.
After drying the hands, the paper towel should be used to cover the water (and open the exit door if necessary).
This thing prevents the hands from getting dirty again when touching the surface.
Washing hands in a health care environment aims to remove pathogenic microorganism ("germs") and avoid spreading them.
The New England Journal of Medicine reports that underserved hand washing is unacceptable in most medical settings, with a large number of doctors and nurses routinely forget to wash their hands before touching the patient, further spreading microorganisms.
One study showed proper hand washing and other simple procedures could reduce the rate of catheter-related blood flow infection by 66 percent. The World Health Organization published a document showing the standard of how to wash and rubbing hands in the health care sector.
This organization's hand hygiene guidelines can also be found on its website for public comments.
A relevant review was carried out by Whitby et al.
Commercial devices can measure and confirm hand hygiene, if a demonstration of regulatory compliance is required.
The World Health Organization has "Five Moments" for hand washing:
after being exposed to the body's
prior to an aceptic task, and
after treating patients. The addition of antiseptic chemicals to the soap (soap "medicated" or "anti-microbial") gives a killing action to handwashing agents.
Such action of murder may be desirable before performing surgery or in a situation where the antibiotic-resistant organism is very widespread. To ‘wash’ a person’s hands for a surgical operation, there should be a pipe that can be turned on and turned off without touching with the hands, some chlorhexidine or iodine washing, sterile towers to dry the hands after washing, and sterile comb for rubbing and other steriler tools to clean under the fingernail.
All jewellery needs to be removed.
This procedure requires washing the hands and forearms up to the elbows, usually 2-6 minutes.
A long rubbing time (10 minutes) is not required.
When rinsing, water in the forearm must be prevented from flowing back to the hands.
After finishing hand washing, the hands are dried with a sterile cloth and a surgical shirt is worn.
To reduce the spread of germs, it is best to wash your hands or use antiseptics before and after treating sick people.
To control in-hospital-in-hospital squirrels, it was found that the greatest benefit from hand washing came from the first 20% wash, and very few added benefits were obtained when the frequency of hand washing increased by more than 35%.
Washing with regular soap produces more than three times the rate of infectious diseases transferred to food compared to washing with antibacterial soap. Comparing hand rubbing with an alcohol solution by washing hands with antibacterial soap for 30 seconds, each indicating that rubbing hands with alcohol reduces bacterial contamination 26% more than antibacterial soap.
But soap and water are more effective than alcohol-based hand rubbing to reduce influenza A H1N1 viruses and Klostridium diphysilar spores than hands. Interventions to improve hygiene in a health care environment can involve education for staff about hand washing, increasing the availability of alcohol-based hand-rubbed hanuring, and an oral and verbal warning to staff.
There is a need for more research on which intervention is most effective in different health environments.
In developing countries, hand washing with soap is recognized as a cost-effective and important tool to achieve good health, even good nutrition.
However, the lack of religenant water supply, soap or hand-washing facilities at home, at school and at work makes it a challenge to achieve universal hand washing behavior.
For example, in most rural areas of Africa, hand-washing pipes close to every private and public toilet are very rare, although there is an existing cheap option for building handwashing stations.
However, a low rate of hand washing can also be caused by a habit embedded from the lack of soap or water.
Promotion and advocacy for hand washing with soap can influence policy decisions, raise awareness of the benefits of hand washing and leading to long-term behavioral changes in the population.
As for this to work effectively, it is necessary to monitor and evaluation.
A systematic review of 70 studies found an effective community-based approach to improving handwashing at LMIC, while social marketing campaigns were less effective. One example of hand washing promotion in schools is “Three Star Approaches” by UNICEP’s “Star Approach” which encourages schools to take simple and inexpensive measures to keep students washing their hands with soap, among other hygiene conditions.
When the minimum standard is reached, the school can move from one to finally three stars.
Developing hand washing stations can be part of hand washing promotional campaigns carried out to reduce disease and child mortality.
World Washing Day is another example of an awareness-raising campaign that is trying to change behavior. As a result of the 2019-20 coronavirus outbreak, UNICEF has been promoting the use of hand-washing emoji.
A number of studies have considered the overall cost of hand washing in developing countries in relation to DALY.
Yet one survey showed that promoting hand wash with soap is far more cost-effective than other water and sanitation interventions.
The importance of washing hands for human health – especially for people in vulnerable conditions such as newborn mothers or wounded soldiers in the hospital — was first recognized in the middle of the 19th century by two pioneers hand hygiene: doctors from Hungarian Ignaz Semmelweis who worked in Vienna, Austria and Forence Nightingale, the "financer of modern nursing" the UK.
At that time most people still believe that the infection was caused by a foul odor called miasmas.
In the 1980's, food-borne outbreaks and healthcare-linked infections caused U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention to more actively promote hand hygiene as an essential way to prevent the spread of infection.
The swine flu outbreak in 2009 and the COVID-19 pandemic in 2020 led to increased awareness about in many countries about the importance of hand washing with soap to protect themselves from such infectious diseases.
For example, posters with "proprimate hand washing techniques" are hung next to the hand-washing sink in a public toistry and in the toilets of office buildings and airports in Germany.
The phrase "wassing someone's hand than" something, means declaring someone's unwillingness to take responsibility for the matter or to share involvement in it.
It is derived from the Biblical verses in Matthew when Pontius Pilate wash his hands from the decision to crucify Jesus Christ, but has become a phrase with more widespread use in some English communities.
In Macbeth by Shakespreare, Lady Macbeth commultically washed his hands in an attempt to wash an imaginable stain, representing her guilt over a crime she had committed and that she had influenced her husband.
It is found that people, after remembering or staring back unethical acts, tend to wash their hands more often than others and more appreciate hand washing equipment.
In addition, those who are allowed to wash their hands after such contemplation are less likely to be involved in "wassing" other payments, such as doing voluntary work.
Religion prescribes hand washing for hygiene and symbolic purposes. Symbolically wash your hands, by using non-soappy water for hand washing, is an integral part of hand washing rituals found in many religions including Bahá'ís, Hindus, tevilah and yadayim netilat in Judaism, Lavabo in Christianity and Wudhu in Islam. Religion also prescribes clean hand washing especially after certain actions.
Hinduism, Judaism and Islam make it mandatory to wash their hands after using the toilet.
Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikhism, Judaism and Islam also make it mandatory to wash their hands before and after meals.
Control of workplace hazards to COVID-19
Workplace hazard control for COVID-19 is an application for occupational health and safety methodologies for hazard control to the prevention of coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19).
Proper hazard control at the workplace depends on the site of the work and work duties, based on the risk assessment for the source of exposure, the severity of the disease in the community, and the risk factors of each employee who may be exposed to COVID-19 infection.
According to the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), employment with low-exposibility risks has minimal employment contact with the public and other colleagues, whose basic infection prevention measures are proposed including hand washing, encouraging workers to stay at home if they're sick, breathing etiquetcy, and keeping hygiene routinely and disinfecting in the workplace.
Jobs with moderate exposure risks include jobs that require frequent or close contact with people who are unknown or suspected of having COVID-19, but may be infected due to contagious communities or international travel.
This includes employees who have come into contact with the general public such as in schools, a working environment with high population density, and some high-volutal retail environments.
Hazard control for this group, in addition to basic infection prevention measures including ventilation using high-efficiency air filters, sneezing controllers, and having personal protective equipment in case they clash with people infected with COVID-19.
OSHA considers healthcare workers and morgues exposed to people who are known or suspected of having COVID-19 as having a high risk of exposure, which increases to a very high risk of exposure if the employee performs an aerosol-producing procedure on, or collecting or handling specimens from people who are known or suspected to be infected with COVID-19.
Efficiable hazard control for these workers include engineering controls such as ventilation rooms with negative pressure and personal protective equipment that correspond to the working task.
COVID-19 outbreaks can have a number of effects in the workplace.
Employees may not be present to work due to illness, having to take care of others or be afraid of being exposed.
The trade pattern may change, both in terms of requested goods, and how to get these goods (such as spending outside peak hours or through a delivery or drive-through service).
Finally, the delivery of goods from the affected geographic areas continues by COVID-19 may be disrupted. The preparednession and response plans of infectious diseases can be used as a guide to protective action.
The plan addresses levels of risks associated with a variety of job sites and occupational tasks including sources of exposure, risk factors arising from home and community environments, and risk factors of each employee such as old age or chronic medical conditions.
It also outlifies the necessary controls to override such risks, and contingency plans for situations that may arise as a result of an epidemic.
The preparedness and response plan of infectious diseases may be subject to the recommendations of the national or subnational plan.
The objectives to respond to outbreaks include reducing transmission among personnel, protecting high-risk people for severe health complications, maintaining business operations, and minimizing adverse effects on other entities in their supply chain.
Severeness of the disease in the community lies that the business affects the response taken.
Hazard control hierarchy is a framework that is widely used in occupational safety and health to group hazard control according to effectiveness.
When the danger of COVID-19 cannot be eliminated, the most effective control is engineering control, followed by an administrative control, and the latter is personal protective equipment.
Engineering control involves segregation of workers from work-related hazards without relying on the employee's behavior, and can be the most cost-effective solution to execute.
An administrative control is a change in a working or procedural policy that requires the actions of an employee or an employer.
Personal protective equipment (PPE) is considered less effective compared to engineering and administrative controls but can help prevent some exposure.
All types of PPE must be selected based on the hazard to the employee, properly installed as appropriate (e.g. respirator), worn correctly consistently, constantly inspected, preserved and replaced, if necessary, and correctly removed, cleaned and stored or discarded to avoid contamination.
According to the U.S. Occupational Safety and Health Administration (OSHA), lower-exposibility risk jobs have minimal employment contacts with the public and other co-workers.
Basic infection prevention measures that are recommended for all workplaces including hand washing regularly and thoroughly, encouraging workers to stay at home if they are sick, respiratory etiques including closing when coughing and sneezing, preparing wipes and garbage containers, preparing for teleworks or turns of work if necessary, not encouraging employees to use other people’s tools or equipment, and maintaining routine cleaning and disinfecting at work.
Identification and rapid segregation of potentially infectious individuals is a critical step in protecting employees, customers, visitors, and others in the workplace.
U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) recommends that workers with acute respiratory symptoms stay at home until they are free from fever, signs of fever or other symptoms for at least 24 hours without using fever-reducing medication or other symptoms, and flexible sick leave policies, allowing workers to stay at home to take care of sick family members, and workers are aware of this policy.
According to OSHA, jobs at risk of moderate exposure include jobs that require frequent or close contacts within six feet (1.8 m) from people who are unknown or suspected COVID-19 patients, but may be infected with SARS-CoV-2 due to community outbreaks that are taking place around business locations, or because the individual has recently traveld internationally to a wide-ranging COVID-19 outbreak.
This includes employees who are in contact with the general public such as in schools, working environments with high-popul population density and some high-volume retail environments. Engineering controls for these groups and higher-risk groups include installing air filters with high-efficiencies, increasing ventilation rates, installing physical barriers such as plastic sneezing protectors and plug-and-through windows. Administrative controls for this group and higher-risky groups include encouraging sick workers to stay at home, replace face-to-house meetings with virtual communications, establishing phased rotations, creating non-important travel-to-the-committees.
Employees in this risk group rarely require the use of respirators.
If someone gets sick in an airplane, proper control to protect workers and other passengers include isolating the sick person from someone else with a distance of 6 feet, assigning a crew to take care of the sick person, and give a face mask to the sick person or ask the sick person to cover his mouth and nose with tissue when coughing or sneezing.
The cabin crew should wear disposable medical gloves when treating sick travelers or touching potentially contaminated body fluids or surfaces, and possibly additional personal protective equipment if the sick traveler has a fever, a persistent cough or difficulty breathing.
Gloves and other disposable items should be disposed of in bio-harmful bags, and polluted surfaces need to be cleaned and disinfected afterwards. For commercial shipments including cruise ships and other passenger vessels, hazard controls including delaying travel when sick, segregating and informing the medical center on the vessel immediately if a person has a fever or other symptoms while on board.
For schools and childcare facilities, the CDC recommends short-term closures to clean or germinate if an infected person has been in a school building regardless of the spread of the community.
When there is minimal to moderate community transmission, social distancing strategies can be implemented such as cancelling field trips, gatherings and other major gatherings such as physical education and choir classes or dining in cafeterias, increasing the space between the tables, times of arriving and returning that are staggered, limiting insignificant visitors, and usising a separate health office location for children with flu-like symptoms.
When there is a high transmission in the local community, in addition to social distancing strategies, an extension of school discontinuation can be considered. The CDC assumes direct health risks for law enforcement personnel who perform routine activities are low.
Law enforcement officers who must make contact with individuals who are confirmed or suspected of having COVID-19 are recommended to follow the same guidelines as emergency medical technicians including proper personal protective equipment.
If close contact occurs during arrest, employees need to clean and disinfect their belts and work equipment before reusing with a spray or home cleaner wiper, and follow the standard operating procedures for PPE containment and disposising used and for placing and washing clothes.
OSHA considers healthcare workers and murry homes to be in a high or very high category for the risk of exposure.
High-risk occupations are exposed including the delivery of healthcare, support, laboratories and medical transport workers exposed to known or suspected COVID-19 patients.
It becomes a very high risk of exposure if an employee performs an aerosol-producing procedure on, or collects or handles specimens from known or suspected COVID-19 patients.
The aerosol-producing procedure includes intubation, cough induction procedures, bronchoscopy, a number of dental procedures and examinations, or the accumulation of invasive specimens.
High-exposioned mortatom jobs include workers involved in providing the bodies of people who are known or suspected of COVID-19 cases at the time of their death; it becomes a very high risk of exposure if they perform autopsy. Additional engineering controls for these risk groups include isolation rooms for known or suspected COVID-19 patients, including when aerosol-producing procedures are carried out.
Ventination with specific negative pressure may be suitable in some health care sites and mortuaries.
Speciments should be handled vigilanty of Level 3 Biosafety.
The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends incoming patients isolated into different waiting places depending on whether they are suspected COVID-19 cases. In addition to PPE, OSHA recommends respirators for those who work within 6 feet from known patients, or suspected, are infected with SARS-CoV-2, and those who perform aerosol production procedures.
In the United States, NIOSH-approved or better NIOSH-approved filter facial respirator respirator respiratory responsiors must be used in the context of a comprehensive and written respiratory protection program including fit tests, training and medical examinations.
Other types of respirators can provide higher protection and improve the comfort of workers. The WHO does not recommend coverings because COVID-19 is a respiratory disease that is not transmitted through bodily fluids.
WHO recommends only surgical masks for entry check-up personnel.
For those who collect respiratory specimens from, treating, or transporting COVID-19 patients without aerosol-producing procedures, the WHO recommends surgical masks, eye protectors, or facial protectors, protective shirts and gloves.
If an aerosol-producing procedure is carried out, the surgical mask is replaced with N95 or FFP2 respirator.
Given the inadequatable PPE's global supply, WHO recommends minimizing PPE's needs through telemedicy, physical obstacles such as clear windows, by allowing only those involved in direct care to enter the COVID-19 patient's room, using only PPE-necessary for specific tasks, continuing the use of similar respions without removing it when caring for many patients with similar diagnoses, monitoring and coordinating PPE's supply chains, and discourage the use of masks for asymptomatic individuals.
FROM: Katherine Maher, CEO of the Wikimedia Foundation
TO: All employees of the Wikimedia Foundation
SUBJECT: [Covid-19] Eases the load and ready for the future
DATE/TIME DATE: 14 March 2020, 00:24 UTC
LICENSING: CC0: There is no rights reserved
We find ourselves in a very unusual situation this month.
The COVID-19 epidemic is something that makes our connectivity in the world and the responsibilities we have towards each other become clear.
We don't have such a challenge before, but we know that our best response depends on empathy type, collaboration and community-building in the middle of this organization.
The friendships and concerns we've seen among all our co-workers via e-mail, phone and talk are an extraordinary confirmation for extraordinary humans we're lucky enough to work together.
I am very grateful and proud to be your co-workers.
Last week, someone shared with me their appreciation for our work.
This reminds me of how meaningful the world is now when it comes to being able to refer to Wikipedia, and how it becomes a powerful symbol because these critical sources remain online and are available to all.
Your work makes all of this possible, whether you make sure the website remains active or that your co-workeer is paid or our community is safe.
The world needs the information provided by Wikipedia, especially nowadays.
It's a moment that not just what we're doing but the way we do, will have a meaningful impact on the world.
Due to the importance of this mission and your role in it, we will make some significant adjustments to how we work together, starting next week.
Co-ordination to our work and schedule
As Robyn mentioned earlier, the c team met last night to discuss our approach and the schedule for the coming days and months.
During the discussion, we assumed what we thought would be a response in accordance with what we encountered and the best way to make sure this organization could survive this time.
Jointly, we want to relieve stress and support our long-term mission.
If you need to slow down a bit, that's okay.
To all staff, contractors and contract workers:
Our daily work expectations will be about 4 hours a day or 20 hours a week until further notice.
We don't announce the holidays - if you're able to work at normal times, the mission can use you.
However, today’s world can’t be predicted. Whether you need to take care of your loved ones, buy kitchenware or consult a doctor, your well-being our priority.
We won't record your time.
If you're sick, don’t work.
It shouldn't have been told, but we told it.
Don �t have a sick day or PTO – just tell your manager and help your team re-check your calendar and schedule to make sure the main areas of the work are covered.
(If you're tested positive for COVID-19, notify Bryan in T&C Ops so that T&C can help with support and to ensure that your situation gets proper attention from the management).
Those paid by the hour will be paid in full.
We have already informed and reaffirmed the commitment to respect the commitment of our contractors and staff colleagues who are paid by the hour.
Everyone will be paid based on their normal working hours under normal circumstances.
This includes if you're sick and can't work.
If you want to work, we support you.
Many people use their jobs as a means of transmitting their stress with the world around us.
What we're doing can be so satisfying for us, especially in times like this.
Once again, it's about taking care of yourself.
We'll just ask you to communicate with your manager so that we know what to expect and adapt it accordingly.
Some work is considered important.
There are some things that we need to keep running.
SRE, HR Ops, Trust & Security, and Fundraising teams (including) are doing critical work that may require additional assistance.
We'll start a process with all departments to evaluate the current objectives and shift our focus to supporting what's important for our mission.
There's so much to do for all of us, we'll just focus on the most important projects.
It's slowing now won't hurt later.
We don't plan to move "twe-fold to chase what's missing" as soon as this pandemic passes.
You're not expected to work over-the-time to meet an unrealistic deadline.
We acknowledge that the situation has changed, and will strive to set new targets and timelines if necessary.
What happened with the AP (Annual Plan)?
To adapt to the reality and expectations of our daily working hours, we would like to adjust the delivery timeline of our 2020-2021 Annual Plan.
Our intention to recommend an extension of the 2019-2020 plan that allows overtime for a budget to allow employees to prioritise critical work, self-care, and the care of their loved ones while assisting those who need or wish to work with a reduced working period for the next few weeks.
The extension of the timeline greatly reduces the workload and the pressure of current plans throughout the organization.
We will introduce our proposal to the Board next week and will inform the representatives and teams of the next steps as soon as we get confirmation.
Thanks to the APP team for your leadership in this regard.
Office status, disclosure and cleaning
Last week, we were informed that one of our SF co-workers might be exposed to the COVID-19 virus.
However, as a precaution, we've hired an antiviral cleaning team to disinfect all surfaces in the San Francisco office.
They use hospital-graded antiviral solutions to disinfect every surface as well as the lobby and an efforth system that reaches our floor.
The building adopts its own caring duty protocols in using products that support the safety of its tenants.
We feel comfortable that the office will be well available when we decided to come back.
Our DC office is located in WeWork, which shares COVID-19 protocols with us and all members of staff based in D.C.
As of last week, our D.C.'s office has shifted to a long way in line with San Francisco.
As we all know by NYC-based co-workers, we also talked about a one-losing location in Brooklyn.
This discussion continues but it may be postponed.
Some of our co-workers are working remotely for the first time.
Colleagues have long known that it could be an adjustment and wanted to give you some advice:
Limit the meeting period for an additional one or two hours.
If a longer session is needed, consider how to divide for a few days.
Specifies the meeting clearly, has an agenda and submit the reading materials in advance.
Make a default video, with tools such as Google Docs and Zoom to make it easier to collaborate and direct connections.
Have a leader to handle each meeting, someone to monitor the chat for questions and track the list of speakers, and someone helps to take notes (or do collection of notes collaboratively).
Send an e-mail to technical support if you'll need a comfortable headphone.
Use your well-being reimbursement payments for snacks.
Participate #remoties in Slack to chat with your co-workeer about distributed tasks
The HR Operations team is looking at ergonomic webinar guides to support improvements in tasks distributed across the Foundation.
Over the past few weeks we've asked all community grant recipients to cancel Wikimedia-funded public events, such as editatons until WHO announces the end of the pandemic.
We told them that we understand that our request for cancellations and other restrictions could be something impossible in completing their agreed grant activities and that nothing would be subject to action due to a delay or modifying such goals.
In the coming week we'll follow up with additional guidance on Wikimania and other regional and thematic community conferences.
General sentiment from across the global community appears to be grief over distractions but is relieved of the clarity and ability to focus on their own community, Wikimedia and vice versa.
Moving forward, CRT strives to develop a page about Meta-Wiki to provide space for the community to monitor the impact and follow our communications with them.
Remain in relation to COVID-19-related issues
We will send an invitation to your calendar for the next Thursday, at 14:00 UTC/07:00 PT for a special meeting of the staff.
We'll use this time to share additional updates, answer your questions and spend some time getting in touch with each other.
We're here together and we're here to help as much as we can.
For now, you can continue to search for information from these emails, and all other important COVID-19-related information on Office Wiki.
CRT will make sure all these pages are updated and all information is in one place.
We are also working to ensure regular communication with staff who are very affected at the moment.
If you have any questions about traveling, events, a major workflow, or coverage challenge, or whatever assistance you need, don’t hesitate to inform and work with CRT.
We are here to assist in providing support and become a liaison if needed.
If you're having something confidential or sensitive, please e-mail it to Bryan Judan - HR International Global Operations Director.
None of these changes should be seen as a waiver of our work and responsibility.
However, all of this is recognition at the moment, our work and responsies may need to be adapted in a way that is unlike the past.
These are steps that we believe as necessary to support each other so that we can continue to work, provide our movements with the support they need, and the services of a world they can expect.
We'll be there to wait for us when the time comes.
For now, it's time to support each other and create space for important tasks to arrive in the weeks and possibly the coming months.
We need all of you to make a reality, and we need you all to take care of yourself and your family so that you'll be prepared at the best level when the need arrives.
Now, please -- wash your hand and don’t touch your face!
Katherine, CRT (Amanda K, Amy V, Bryan J, Doreen D, Gregory V, Jaime V, Joel L, Lynette L, Ryan M and Tony S) and the others in the Leadership Team (Grant I, Heather W, Jaime V, Janeen U, Lisa S, Robyn A, Ryan M and Toby N).
Angiotensin 2 (ACE2) is an enzyme connected to the outer surface (cell membrane) of cells in the lungs, arteries, hearts, kidneys and intestines.
ACE2 deals with the activity of angiotensin-converting enzymes (ACE) associated with reducing the amount of angiotensin-II and increasing Ang(1-7) making it a reliable drug targeted to treat cardiovascular disease. ACE2 also acts as an entry center into cells for some coronaviruses.
The human version for enzymes is often referred to as hACE2.
Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 is zinc that contains metaloenzyme located on the surface of the endothelium and other cells.
ACE2 protein contains M2 peptidase N-terminal domains and C-terminal renoral amino acid carrier domain.
ACE2 is a single-line-type 1 membrane protein, with the domain of its enzymes actively exposed to the surface of cells in the lungs and other tissues.
ACE2's out-of-cell external domain attached to the transmembrane domain by another enzyme known as sheddase, and the dissolved protein yield is released into the bloodstream and is eventually excreted into urine.
ACE2 is present in most of organs: twin ACE2 on the cell membranes of most of alveolus type II cells of the lungs, the entrocytes of the small intestine, arteries and venous endothelial cells and soft muscle cells of the arteries in most organs.
ACE2 mRNA expression is also found in the cerebrural cortex, striatum, hypothalamus and brain stem.
The main function of ACE2 is to accommodate as a counterweight to the ACE.
ACE sticks the hormone I angiotensin into angiotensin vaso nerphication.
ACE2, on the other hand, attaches the carboxy-terminal amino acid phenylalanine from angiotensin II (Asp-Arg-Val-Tyr-His-Pro-Phe) and its hydrolysis into angiotensin vasodilator (1-7), (H-Asp-Arg-Val-Val-Tyr-Ile-His-Pro-OH).
ACE2 can also attach a number of other peptides including [des-Arg9]— bradikinin, appeline, neurotensin, dinorphines A and ghrelin.
ACE2 also regulates the distribution of transmitting membranes of SLC6A19-neutral amino acids and is indicated in Hartnup's disease.
As a transmembrane protein, ACE2 acts as the primary entry center into cells for some coronaviruses including HCoV-NL63; SARS-CoV (the SARS-causative virus); and SARS-CoV-2 (COVID-19-causative virus).
In particularly, the binding for the S1 protein of the nail for SARS-CoV and SARS-COS2 on the enzyme domain on the ACE2 above the surface of the cell resulting in endocytosis and translocation of viruses and enzymes into the endosomes are located between the cells.
The inclusion process also requires the namplection of S-proteins by TMPRSS2 hose serin protests, inhibitors under the current study as a potential therapeutic. This has led to some hypothesizing that reducing ACE2 levels in cells may be helpful in fighting infections.
However, various professional associations and regulatory bodies recommend to pursue standard ACE inhibitors and ARB therapy.
A systematic review and meta-analysis published on July 11, 2012 found that "the use of ACE inhibitors is associated with a significant 34% decrease in the risk of pneumonia compared to control."
Furthermore, "the risk of pneumonia also decreases for patients treated with ACE inhibitors who have a higher risk for pneumonia, in particular those with stroke and heart failure.
The use of ACE inhibitors is also linked to a reduction in pneumonia-related mortality rates, although the results are less robust than the overall risk of pneumonia."
Human ACES2 (rhACE2) recognisation is considered novel therapy for acute lung injuries, and appears to increase pulmonary hemodynamics and oxygen saturation in swives with acute respiratory distres syndrome induced lipopolysaccharides.
The half-life of rhACE2 in humans is 10 hours and the beginning of action is 30 minutes in addition to 24-hourd effect (duration).
Some findings recommend that rhACE2 can be a reliable remedy for them with intolerance in inhibitors of the classical angiotensin renin system (RAS inhibitors) or in diseases that surround angiotensin II is enhanced. rhACE2 infusion has been evaluated in clinical studies for the treatment of acute respiration distres syndrome.
The b'COVID-19 application is a mobile software application designed to help network tracking in response to the 2019-20 coronavirus pandemic, which is the process of identifying people ("relations") those who may be in contact with an infected individual.
Many applications are developed or proposed with government officials supporting in several regions and jurisdictions.
Several frameworks for building contact tracking applications have already been developed.
Privacy concerns are raised, especially about systems that are based on geographical location tracking of user applications.
Less disruptive alternatives include the use of a Bluetooth signal to record a user’s proximity log on another cell phone.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple jointly announced that they will integrate functionality to support Bluetooth-based applications directly into their Android and iOS operating systems.
In China, the Chinese government, in collaboration with Alipay, has launched an app that allows citizens to check if they have ever had contact with those infected with COVID-19.
It is used across over 200 cities in China. In Singapore, an application called TraceTogether is in use.
The app developed by the local IT community, was launched as an open-source and will be handed over to the government. North Macedonia launched "Stop Korona!", a Bluetooth-based application to track exposure with potentially infected people and provide quick response to the health care authorities.
The application was developed by the Ministry of Communications and Technology and the Ministry of Health.
As of April 14, 2020, the app is awaiting approval by the Google Play Store and the Apple App Store.
On April 12, the government stated that the contact tracking app is at an advanced level of development, and will be available for launch in a few weeks. A similar application is planned in Ireland and France ("StopCovid").
Australia and New Zealand are considering apps based on Singapore’s TraceTogether app and the BlueTrace protocol. Russia intends to introduce geobatas applications for patients diagnosed with COVID-19 living in Moscow, designed to make sure they don’t leave homes.
Ross Anderson, a security engineering professor at Cambridge University, listed a number of potential practical problems with application-based systems, including incorrect positives and a potential lack of effectiveness if application implementation is limited in only a small part of the population.
Responding to concerns about misleading or dangerous "coronavirus" applications, Apple sets limits for the type of organization that could add coronavirus-related applications to its Apps Store, limiting them to "authorized" sides alone or reputable organizations.
Google and Amazon have implemented the same restrictions.
Privacy campaigners expressed their concerns about the effects of mass surveillance using coronavirus applications, in particular on whether a surveillance infrastructure created to deal with the coronavirus pandemic will be halted as soon as the threat passes.
Amnesty International and more than 100 other organizations issued a statement requesting a limit on such supervision.
The organization declares eight conditions on government projects:
supervision shall be "legalized, if necessary and proportional";
advanced monitoring and oversighting should have an embedded clause;
The use of data shall be limited to the purpose of COVID-19;
data security and anonymity shall be protected and demonstrated as evidence-protected;
digital scrutiny should avoid aggravating discrimination and marginalization;
any sharing of data with third parties shall be defined in law;
shall be protected against abuse and the right of citizens to provide feedback on abuse;
German Chaos Computer Club (CCCC) and Reporters Without Borders (RSF) also remove checklists.
Google/Apple's plan proposal intends to address ongoing surveillance issues by removing the tracking mechanism from their device's operating system as soon as it's no longer needed.
Some countries use network-based location tracking instead of applications, eliminating both the need to download applications and the ability to avoid tracking.
In Israel, network-based tracking is approved.
Network-based solutions that have access to raw location data have significant potential privacy issues.
However, not all systems with a centralized server need to have access to personal location data; a number of privacy preservation systems are already created that use a centralized server only for interconnection (see section below).
In South Korea, an application-based system is used to make contact tracking.
Instead of using specific applications, the system collects tracking information from a variety of sources including mobile device tracking data and card transaction data, and merging all of this generates notices via text messages to potentially infected individuals.
In addition to using this information to alert potential contacts, the government also makes location information available to the public, which is allowed due to major changes to information privacy laws after the MERS outbreak in the country.
This information is available to the public through a number of applications and websites. Countries including Germany are considering using centralized systems and the preservation of privacy.
As of 6 April 2020, details have not been published yet.
Contact tracking with the preservation of privacy is an organized concept with an important body study literature starting in 2013. As of April 7, 2020, more than a group of experts working for privacy-friendly solutions such as using Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) for user proximity logs on other cellular phones.
However, PEPP-PT is a coordination effort that contains a centralized and decentralized approach, and is not a single protocol. Decentralized protocols include Decentralized Privacy Preservence Tracking (DP-PPT/DP-3T), Temporary Contact Number (TCN, Fka Contact Event Number, CEN), Privacy Sensitive Protocol and Mechanism for Mobile Contact Tracing (PACT) and others.
In this protocol, the identified personal data has never left the device, and all matching takes place on the device.
MIT Media Lab's Privacy Group is developing SafePaths, a platform to use privacy preservation techniques when collecting and using locations or interconnected sections to track the spread of COVID-19.
This is based on research from the white paper "Apps Gone Rogue: Maintaining Personal Privacy in an Epidemic" published in March 2020. Another similar venture is the SafeTrace platform by Enigma MPC, a company developing privacy technology that was also originally founded at MIT Media Lab.
SafeTrace uses secure hardware technologies to allow users to share sensitive locations and health data with other users and authorities, without compromising data privacy.
On April 5, 2020, the global TCN Coalition was founded by a number of unified groups on the same important methods and large overlapping protocols, with the goal of reducing fragmentation, and enabling global interoperability for tracking and warning applications, an important aspect to achieve widespread use.
On 9 April 2020, the Singapore government announced that it had made an open source for the BlueTrace protocol used by official government applications.
On April 10, 2020, Google and Apple, the company that controls Android and iOS mobile platforms, announced an initiative for contact tracking that they claimed would preserve privacy, based on a combination of Bluetooth low-energy technology and privacy-preserving cryptography.
They also publish core technology specifications used in systems.
According to Apple and Google, the system intends to launch in three stages:
launch tools to enable the government to create a coronavirus tracking application for privacy preservation
The integration of these functions directly into iOS and Android Google and Apple's plans to address ongoing implementation and surveillance problems by first distributing the system through operating system updates, and then releasing it in the same way as the threat passes.
Repositing the drug (also known as changing the purpose of the drug, re-provision, reassignment or therapeutic conversion) is to change the purpose of a drug approved for the treatment of different diseases or health conditions from the reason that it was originally developed.
This is an ongoing scientific research effort to develop a safe and effective COVID-19 treatment.
Other research directions include the development of the COVID-19 vaccine and restractive plasma transfer. SARS-CoV-2 has approximately 66 medicated proteins, each of which has multiple lining sites.
Analyzing the fastening site provides an affordable project to develop effective antiviral drugs against COVID-19 proteins.
Of most important SARS-CoV-2's target proteins are proteases such as papains, RNA-dependent polymerases, helicases, S proteins and ADP ribophosphatases.
Hussein A, et al studied a number of candidate compounds that were later optimized and analysed for skeletal similarities with the same-highest-approved drugs to accelerate the development of a powerful SARS-CoV-2 anti-drug in its preclinical studies to be recommended in the design of clinical studies.
Chloroquine is an antimalaria treatment that is also used against a number of autoimmune diseases.
On March 18, WHO announced that chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine would be among the four-reviewed drugs as part of the Solidarity clinical trial.
New York Governor Andrew Cuomo announced that the New York state trial on chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine would begin on March 24. On March 28, the FDA authorized the use of hydroxychloroquine sulfate and chloroquine phosphate under the Emergency Use Authorization (EUA).
Treatment was not approved by the FDA's clinical trial process and is allowed under the EUA only as an experimental treatment for emergency use in patients hospitalized but could not receive treatment in clinical studies.
The CDC has said that "the use, dosage, or duration of hydroxychloroquine for prophylaxis or the treatment of SARS-CoV-2 infection has not yet been established.
Doctors have already said that they use the drug when "there are no other options".
A team of Turkish researchers in Istanbul is conducting a small study on the use of chloroquine in a combination of zinc, vitamin A, vitamin C and vitamin D.
A large study is currently being conducted at the Duke University and the University of Oxford.
NYU Langone Medical School is conducting an experiment on the safety and effectiveness of hydroxychloroquine prevention.
China's clinical trials in Wuhan and Shenzhen claimed that favipiravir was "obviously effective".
35 patients in Shenzhen tested negative in the median for 4 days, while the period of illness is 11 days for 45 patients who did not receive it.
In a study conducted in Wuhan of 240 patients with pneumonia, half were given favipiravir and the other half received umifenovir.
Italy's pharmaceutical agency reminds the general public that current evidence in supporting the drug is still lacking and early.
On April 2, Germany announced that it would be buying medicine from Japan for its storage and using the military to deliver medicine to a university hospital, which would use the drug to treat COVID-19 patients.
According to the South China Morning Post, Shinzo Abe changed his stance against the Trump administration regarding the purchase of the drug. The drug may be less effective in cases of severe illness when the virus has doubled.
It may not be safe for use by pregnant women or those trying to get pregnant.
A study lopinavir/ritonavir (Kaletra), a combination of antiviral lopinavir and ritonavir, concluded that "no advantages can be observed".
The drug is designed to inhibit HIV from mimicking by binding to protease.
A team of researchers at the University of Colorado is trying to modify the drug to discover compounds that will bind with SARS-CoV-2 protease. There have been criticisms in the scientific community about directing resources to change the purpose of the drug developed specifically for HIV/AIDS.
WHO included lopinavir/ritonavir in Solidarity international trials.
Remdesivir was created and developed by Gilead Sciences as a treatment for Ebola virus disease and Marburg viral infection. Gilead Sciences subsequently discovered that Remdesivir has antiviral in vitro activity against various filo—, pneumo—, paramyxo— and corona—virus.”
An issue with antiviral treatment is the indicator of resistance through mutations that can cause more severe diseases and transplants.
Several preliminary pre-trial studies suggest remdesivir may have a high-genetic barrier to resistance. There are a number of clinical trials currently being implemented including two conducted by the Cleveland University Hospital; one for people with a moderate illness and the other for those with more severe diseases.
There were three ongoing clinical trials for intravenous vitamin C for those hospitalized and severely ill with COVID-19, two placebo-controlled (China, Canada) and one uncontrollable (Italy).
The state of New York began a trial for azithromycin antibiotics on March 24, 2020.
Japan's National Center for Global Health and Medicine (NCGM) is planning a clinical trial for Teijin’s Alvesco (ciclesonide), a corticosteroid inhaling for asthma, for the treatment of presymphystmatic patients infected with the novel coronavirus.
A form of angiotensin-converging enzyme 2, a Phase II trial is being carried out of 200 patients selected from hospital and severe hospitalization cases in Denmark, Germany and Austria to determine the effectiveness of treatment.
Researchers from the Montreal Heart Institute in Canada are studying the role of colchicine in reducing inflammatory and pulmonary complications in patients with mild COVID-19 symptoms.
The study, named COLCORONA, took 6000 adults aged 40 and above who were diagnosed with COVID-19 and experienced mild symptoms that did not require hospitalization.
Women who are pregnant or breastfeeding or do not have an effective contraceptive method are ineligible.
Some anticoagulants are being tested in Italy.
Heparin with low molecular weight is widely used for treating patients, prompting the Italian Medical Agency to publish guidelines on its use.
A multi-centralized study of 300 patients researched the use of sodium enoxaparin at prophylactic and therapeutic doses announced in Italy on April 14.
Since SARS-CoV-2 is a virus, sufficient scientific attention has been focused on changing the purpose of approved antiviral drugs developed for epidemic purposes such as MERS, SARS and Western Nile viruses.
Ribavirin: ribavirin recommended for treatment of COVID-19 in accordance with China's 7th guidelines
Umifenovir: umifenovir recommended for COVID-19 treatment according to China's 7th guidelines
Some antibiotics have been identified as a potential role-accommodating:
Tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 Receptors): Approved by China.
Also trials in Italy and China, and see Tocilizumab#COVID-19.
The COVID-19 vaccine is a hypothetical vaccine against the 2019 coronavirus (COVID-2019).
While there is no vaccine to complete a clinical trial, there have been many attempts to develop the vaccine.
At the end of February 2020, the World Health Organization (WHO) said it did not anticipate a vaccine against SARS-CoV-2, a causative viral, to be available less than 18 months.
Five vaccine candidates were in Phase 1 safety studies in April.
COVID-19 was identified in December 2019.
Major outbreaks around the world in 2020, providing investments and proportionaled research activities to develop vaccines.
Many organizations are using published genomes to develop a vaccine possible against SARS-CoV-2.
Specified in April, CEPI's imperative initiative for accelerated vaccine development, manufacturing capacity, scalable implementation and global access.
In April, CEPI scientists reported that 10 different technology platforms under research and development during the early 2020 were to create an effective vaccine against COVID-19.
Key platforms aiming to progress into Phase I security studies include:
nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) (Phase 1 developers and vaccine candidates: Moderna, mRNA-1273)
Viral vectors (Phase I Developer and vaccine candidates: CanSino Biologics, adenovirus vector type 5)
As reported by CEPI scientists in April, a total of 115 vaccine candidates are in the early stages of development, with 78 certified as active projects (79, according to the Milken Institute), and 37 others are announced, but with little public information available (considered to be in the planning or being designed).
Phase I-II trials conducted initial safety and immunogenital tests, usually randomly, placebo-controlled and multi-sites, while more accurately-expericulating dosages.
Stage III trials typically involve more participation including the control group and the effectiveness of tests on the vaccine to prevent the disease, while monitoring for adverse effects at an optimal dose.
Of 79 vaccine candidates in active development (confirmed as early April 2020), 74 have yet to under human evaluation) are still under "praclinical" research.
On 24 January 2020 in Australia, the University of Queensland announced that it was investigating the potential molecular clamp vaccine that would genetically modify viral proteins to stimulate immune response.
On 24 January 2020 in Canada, the International Vaccine Center (VIDO-InterVac) at the University of Saskatchewan announced a continuation of its efforts on vaccines, aiming to start human testing in 2021.
The vaccine development project was announced at China's Centers for Disease Control and Prevention on 26 January 2020 and the University of Hong Kong on January 28.
On January 29, 2020, Janssen Pharmaceutical Companies, spearheaded by Hanneke Schuitemaker, announced that it had already embarked efforts in developing the vaccine.
Janssen is jointly developing an oral vaccine with Vaxart which is his biotechnology partner.
On March 18, 2020, Emergent BioSolutions announced a partnership with Vaxart to develop the vaccine.
On 8 February 2020, OncoGen's laboratory in Romania published a paper on the design of a vaccine with the same technology as used for cancer neoantigen vaccination therapy.
On March 25, the head of the research institute announced they had finalised the vaccine's systhesis and they had started testing.
On 27 February 2020, a subsidiary company of Generex Immuno-Oncology, announced that they had embarked on a vaccine project to create a peptid li-Key vaccine against COVID-19.
They want to produce vaccine candidates that can be tested in humans "within 90 days."
On March 5, 2020, Washington University at St. Louis announced its projects to develop a vaccine.
On March 5, 2020, the U.S. Military Medical Research and Substantial Research Center at Fort Detrick and the Walter Reed Military Research Institute in Silver Spring, both in western Maryland, announced that they were working on a vaccine.
Between 10 March 2020, Emergent Biosolutions announced that they have partnered with Novavax Inc.
in the development and development of vaccines.
These partners further announced plans for preclinical testing and Phase I clinical trials by July 2020.
On 12 March 2020, India's Ministry of Health announced they were working with 11 isolates that although on a fast track it would take at least one-and-a-a-a-and-a-half-year-to-te-two years to develop the vaccine.
On 12 March 2020, Medicago, a biotechnology company in Quebec City, Quebec, reported the development of particles such as the coronavirus under some of Canada's Health Research Institutes.
Candidates of the vaccine are in laboratory research, with human testing planned in July or August 2020.
Earlier that week, The Guardian reported U.S. President Donald Trump offering CureVac "a lot of money to gain exclusive access to the Covid-19 vaccine", and this was opposed by the German government.
On March 17, 2020, American pharmaceutical company Pfizer announced a partnership with German company BioNTech to jointly develop mRNA-based vaccines.
BNT162 mRNA-based vaccine candidates are currently in pre-clinical trials while clinical trials are expected to begin in April 2020.
On March 17, 2020 in Italy, Takis Biotech, an Italian biotechnology company, announced they would get pre-clinical testing results in April 2020 and that their final vaccine candidates could begin human testing by autumn.
On March 19, 2020 in France, the Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations (CEPI) announced a US$4.9 million investment for a COVID-19 vaccine research consortium involving the Pasteur Institute, Themis Bioscience (Vienna, Austria) and the University of Pittsburgh, bringing total CEPI investments in the development of the COVID-19 vaccine to US$29 million.
Other CEPI investment partners for COVID-19 vaccine development are Moderna, Curevac, Inovio, Novavax, Hong Kong University, the University of Oxford and the University of Queensland.
On 20 March 2020, Russian health officials announced that scientists had started animal testing for six different vaccine candidates.
Imperial College London researchers announced on 20 March 2020 that they are developing a self-reinforced RNA vaccine for COVID-19.
Candidates of the vaccine were developed within 14 days after receiving a sequence from China.
At the end of March, the Canadian government announced C$275 million funding for a research project on medical prevention measures against COVID-19, including numerous vaccine candidates at Canadian companies and universities such as Medicago and the University of Saskatchewan initiative.
At the same time, the Canadian government announced C$192 million specifically to develop a COVID-19 vaccine, with plans to establish a national "vaccination bank" from several new vaccines that could be used if another coronavirus outbreak occurred.
On April 2, 2020, researchers at the University of Pittsburgh School of Medicine reported on the trial of PittCoVaccc, a possible COVID-19 vaccine in mice, noting that "MNA provided the SARS-CoV-2 S1 subunit vaccine that provokes a powerful antigen-specific antian antibody response [in mice] that was proven starting 2 weeks after immunization."
In Canada on April 16, 2020, the University of Waterloo School of Pharmacies announced the design of DNA-based vaccine candidates as a possible nasal spray.
By using bacteriophages, DNA will be designed to replicate human bacteria to produce particles such as harmful viruses, which can stimulate the immune system to produce antibodies against the SARS-CoV-2 virus.
In March 2020, the U.S. government, industry and three universities gathered resources for supercomputer access from IBM, combined with cloud computing resources from Hewlett Packard Enterprise, Amazon, Microsoft and Google.
Some vaccines have a heterological effect, also called non-specific effects.
This means that they can have benefits other than their prevented diseases.
A random trial in Australia aims to register 4,170 health care workers.
There is a possibility that vaccines in development are unsafe or effective.
Initial research to assess the effectiveness of vaccines using COVID-19-specific animal models such as ACE2 transgenic mice, other laboratory animals and non-human primates, demonstrated the need for stage 3 bio-security restraint measures to handle live viruses, and international coordination to ensure standard safety procedures.
Vaccines against SARS and MERS have been tested in non-human animal models.
In 2020, there is no curative or protective vaccine for SARS that has been proven to be safe and effective in humans.
According to research papers published in 2005 and 2006, the identification and development of new vaccines and drugs to treat SARS is a priority for governments and public health agencies around the world. Moreover, there is no proven vaccine against MERS.
As MERS spread widely, existing SARS research is believed to be a useful template for developing vaccines and therapies against MERS-CoV infection.
In March 2020, there is one MERS (DNA-based) vaccine that has completed phase I of clinical trials in humans, and the other three are in progress, all are a viral vector vaccine, two adenoviral vectors (ChAdOx1-MERS, BVRS-GamVac), and one MVAV (AMVA-MERS-S vector).
Posts on social media encourage conspiracy theories claiming that the virus behind COVID-19 has been known and a vaccine is already available.
Patents quoted by various social media releases refer to existing patents for genetic sequences and vaccines for other coronavirus strains such as SARS coronavirus.
Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) is an infectious disease caused by severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus 2 (SARS-CoV-2).
Common symptoms include fever, coughing and shortness of breath.
Other symptoms may include fatigue, muscle pain, diarrhea, sore throat, loss of sense of smell and abdominal pain.
The time from exposure until the onset of symptoms is usually for five days but may range from two to fourteen days.
While the majority of cases cause mild symptoms, some progress to viral pneumonia and multi-organ failure.
As of April 17, 2020, more than 2.24 million cases have been reported across 210 countries and regions, resulting in more than 153,000 deaths.
More than 568,000 patients have recovered. The virual in particular spreads between humans during close contact, usually through small droplets produced by coughing, sneezing or talking.
While these droplets are produced when exhaling, they usually fall to the ground or above the surface instead of becoming an infection for a long distance.
People can also become infected by touching contaminated surfaces and then touching their eyes, nose or mouth.
Virals can survive on the surface for up to 72 hours.
Most infectious during the first three days after the onset of symptoms, although the spread may occur before the symptoms appear and in the subsequent stages of the disease. The standardized method of diagnosis is with a real-time reversive transcription polymerase chain reaction (cRT-PCR) of the nasopharingeal swab.
The use of masks is recommended to those who suspect they carry viruses and their caregivers.
Recommendations for the use of masks by the general public are different, with some authorities not recommend using it, some recommend its use and others require their use.
At the moment, there is no vaccine or viral treatment specifically for COVID-19.
Local transmission of the disease is recorded in most countries across all six WHO regions.
Those infected with the virus may be asymptomatic or showing symptoms such as flu such as fever, cough, fatigue and shortness of breath.
Emergency symptoms include breathing difficulties, persistent pain or chest pressure, confusion, difficulty walking and face or bluish lips; immediate medical attention is advised if these symptoms appear.
Less commonly, upper respiratory symptoms such as sneezing, runny nose or sore throat may be observed.
gastrointestinal symptoms such as nausea, vomiting and diarea are observed in multiple percentages.
Some cases in China had initially only shown data congestion and palpitations.
In some, the disease can progress into pneumonia, multi-organ failure and death.
It's called an incubation period.
The incubation period for COVID-19 is usually five to six days but may range from two to 14 days.
97.5% of patients showing symptoms will do so in 11.5 days since the infection. Reports show that not all infected showed symptoms.
The role of this asymptomatic carrier in the transfer is still not fully known; however, preliminary evidence suggests that it may contribute to the spread of the disease.
Proportions of those infected have not shown symptoms are unknown at the moment and are being studied, with the Korea Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (KDCC) reporting that 20% of all confirmed cases remain asymptomatic while living in their hospitals.
China's National Health Commission began to include asymptomatic cases in their daily cases on April 1; of the 166 infections that day, 130 (78%) were asymptomatic at the time of testing.
Sputum and salury can carry a lot of viral loads.
Talking loudly releases more drops rather than talking normally.
A study in Singapore found that coughing does not cover their mouths can lead up to 4.5 meters (15 feet).
Although the virus is not carried out, Academic National Sciences suggests that bioaerosol transfers are likely and air collectors at an in-lane position outside the public's room showed a positive sample for the RNA of the virus.
Some medical procedures such as intubation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) may cause respiration secretions to become an aerosol and in turn lead to inconstructive dissemination.
Although there are concerns that they spread through feces, this risk is believed to be low. The virus is very contagious when people asymptomatic; although propagation is possible before the appearance of symptoms, the risk is low.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control (ECDC) mentions that although it is not entirely clear about the ease of spreading disease, a person infects two to three people in general. The virus lasts for several hours up to a few days on the surface.
In particular, the virus is found as detectable for a day on cardboard, up to three days above plastic (polypropylene) and stainless steel (AISI 304), and up to four hours above 99% copper.
However, this varies depending on the humidity and temperature.
Soaps and detergents are also effective if used correctly; soap products weaken viral fat-protecting layers, inactivating them as well as releasing them from the skin and other surfaces.
Other solutions such as benzalkonium chloride and gluconate chlorhexidine (surgical infectious substances) are ineffective. In one Hong Kong study, an example of saliva was taken for median for two days after the start of hospitalization.
In five out of six patients, the first sample showed the highest viral load, and the sixth patient showed the highest viral load on the second day tested.
Severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS-CoV-2) is a novel coronavirus severe acute respiration syndrome, first isolated from three people with pneumonia in connection with a cluster of acute respiratory disease cases in Wuhan.
All the novel features of the SARS-CoV-2 virus occur in the naturally related coronavirus.
Outside the human body, the virus is killed by household soap, which leaks its protective bubble. SARS-CoV-2 is almost associated with the original SARS-CoV.
The lungs are the organs most affected by COVID-19 because the virus accesses hose cells through the angiotensin enzyme-converting enzyme (ACE2), the most abundant in type II alveolus cells in the lungs.
Virals use a special surface glycoprotein so-called “calic” (peploper) to connect to ACE2 and enter the hose cell.
Acute cardium injuries were found in 12% of infected people who were hospitalized in Wuhan, Chan and were more common in severe illness.
The rate of cardiovascular symptoms is high, due to systemic inflammatory reactions and diseases of the immune system during the progress of the disease, but acute myocardial injury may also be associated with ACE2 receptors in the heart.
ACE2 receptors are very numerous in the heart and are involved in the functioning of the heart.
High thrombosis (31%) and venous thromboembolism (25%) are found in ICU patients with COVID-19 infection and may be related to poor prognosis. Autopsies of those who died due to COVID-19 have found alveolus diffusion (DAD), and lymphocytic invasion contains inflammation in the lungs.
Although SARS-COV-2 has trophism for ACE2 expression epithetial cells in the respiratory tract, patients with severe COVID-19 have symptoms of systemic hyperinflammation.
In Particular, T-scell secretes pathogenic GM-CSF is indicated for correlating with the ingestion of monocytic inflammation secreting IL-6 and severe lung pathologies in COVID-19 patients.
lymphocytical intrusion was also reported during autopsy.
WHO publishes a number of testing protocols for diseases.
The standard testing method is a real-time reversible transcription polymerase (rPT-PCR).
Tests are usually carried out on breathing samples obtained with a nasopharyngeal swab; however, nasal swabs or phlegmum samples may also be used.
The results are generally available within a few hours up to two days.
A blood test can be used but this requires two blood samples to be taken two weeks apart and the results have a little immediate value.
Chinese scientists are able to isolate coronavirus strains and publish genetic sequences so that laboratories around the world can independently develop polymerase chain reaction (PCR) tests to detect infections by viruses.
As of 4 April 2020, antibody tests (possibly detected an active infection and whether someone had been infected in the past) was in development but was not widely used.
China's experience with testing has shown accuracy of only 60 to 70%.
The FDA in the United States passed the first start-of-the-care test on March 21, 2020 for use at the end of the month. Diagnostic guidelines published by Zhongnan Hospital at Wuhan University suggested methods for the detection of infections based on clinical features and epidemiological risks.
Opacility of glass disintegration of bilateral multilobar with peripheral, asymmetric and posterior distribution is common during an early infection.
Dominance subpleura, many pavets (the thickening of the lobuled septum with multiple alveolus fillings) and consolidation may appear when the disease is developed.
Some data can be found on monoscopic and patophysiological lesions of COVID-19.
The discovery of the main pathological during autopsies is:
Macroscopy: pleurisi, pericarditis, unification of the lungs and pulmonary edema
Four types of severity for viral pneumonia can be noted:
Mild pneumonia: pulmonary edema, pneumocytes, hyperplasia, large atypical pneumocytes, inflammation of the space-intermediate with lymphocytic invasions and multinucleate giant cell formation
Severe pneumonia: alveolus difusal damage (DAD) with exudat alveolus difus.
DAD is the cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) and severe hypoxemia.
recovery of pneumonia: exudate arrangement in alveolus cavities and fibrosis of space-inter-pulmonary
Blood: disseminated intravascular coagulantization (DIC), leukoyerhoblastic design
Preventive measures to reduce the likelihood of infection include staying at home, avoiding people's places, regularly washing their hands with soap and water and for at least 20 seconds, practice good breathing hygiene and avoid touching your eyes, nose or mouth with unwashed hands.
The CDC recommends covering the mouth and nose with tissue when coughing or sneezing and recommend the use of the inside of the shoulders in the absence of tissues.
Proper hand hygiene after any cough or sneezing is recommended.
The CDC recommends the use of cloth face coverings in public places, in part to limit evacuations by asymptomatic individuals. Social distancing strategies aim to reduce contact of infected people with large groups through school and workplace closures, limit travel and cancel large public gatherings.
The distancing guidelines also include for the public to be at least 6 feet (1.8 m) away.
No medications have been identified as effective to prevent COVID-19. As an unexpected vaccine until at the very beginning of 2021, the main thing in managing COVID-19 is to try to reduce epidemic peaks, known as “feating curves”.”
The CDC also recommends that individuals regularly wash their hands with soap and water for at least 20 seconds, especially after going to the toilet or when the hands appear dirty, before eating or after exhaling the nose, cough or sneezing.
Another is recommended to use alcohol-based hand sanitization with at least 60% alcohol but only when soap and water are not provided. For areas that do not have commercial hand sanitization, WHO provides two formulations for local production.
In this formulation, antimicrobial activity emerges from ethanol or isopropanol.
Hydrogen peroxide is used to help get rid of bacterial spores in alcohol; it "it's not an active ingredient for hand antisepsis".
Glycerol is added as a moisturizer.
People are managed with supportive care, which may include fluid therapy, oxygen support, and supporting other affected vital organs.
The CDC recommends that anyone who suspects themselves carries the virus to wear a simple face mask.
The oxygenation of the esctracorporeum membrane (ECMO) is used to deal with the issue of respiratory failure, but its advantages are still under consideration.
Personal hygiene and a healthy lifestyle and diets are recommended to increase immunity.
Supportive treatment may be useful for those with mild symptoms at an early stage of the infection. The WHO and China’s Health Health Commission have published recommendations for caring for those hospitalized with COVID-19.
Critical care and pulmonologists in the U.S. collected the recommendations of treatments from various agencies into a free source, IBCC.
As of April 2020, there is no specific treatment for COVID-19.
For symptoms, some medical professionals recommend paracetamol (acetaminophen) compared to ibuprofen for the use of the first line.
Precautions should be taken to minimise the risk of virus transfer, especially in healthcare settings when performing procedures that can generate aerosols, such as intubation or hand ventilation.
For healthcare professionals to take care of those infected with COVID-19, the CDC recommends placing the person in the Natural Infection Segregation Room (AIIR) in addition to using standard precautions, contact precautions and built-in precautions. The CDC provides guidelines for the use of personal protective equipment (PPE) during the pandemic.
The recommended equipment are: PPE gowns, respirators or face masks, eye protection and medical gloves. If available, respirators (compared to face masks) are preferred.
The N95 respirator is approved by industrial settings but the FDA already allows masks to be used under the Emergency Use Authorization (EUA).
It is designed to protect from built-in particles such as dust but effective against specialized biological agents not guaranteed for out-of-label use.
When masks are not available, the CDC recommends the use of face shields or as an end-of-the-line, home-made mask.
Most COVID-19 cases aren't so severe that they require mechanical or alternative ventilation, but certain percentages require it.
Types of respiratory support for individuals with COVID-19 associated with respiratory failure are being actively studied for patients in hospitals with some evidence that intubation may be avoided with high-flowing nasal cannules or two-stage positive airway pressure.
Whether these thas provide similar benefits to people who are critically ill is unknown.
Some doctors choose to stay invasive mechanical ventilation if available because this technique restricts the diffusion of aerosol particles compared to high-flow nasal cannules. Severe cases are usually common in older adults (those older than 60 years and especially those older than 80).
Many developing countries don �t have enough per capita hospital beds, limiting the capacity of health systems to handle a sharp increase in the number of COVID-19 cases that severely require hospitalization.
A study in China found that as many as 5% were admitted to intensive care units, 2.3% needed mechanical ventilators and 1.4% died.
In China, at least 30% of patients in hospitals with COVID-19 will be admitted to the ICU in the end.
Mechanical ventilation is becoming more complicated because acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) develops in COVID-19 and oxygenation is becoming increasingly difficult.
Ventilators are capable of controlling stress and high PEEP is required to maximize oxygen delivery while minimizing the risk of lung injury related to ventilators and pneumothorax.
High PEEPs may not be available on ventilators longer.
Research for potential treatment began in January 2020, and some antiviral drugs were in clinical trials.
Remdesivir appears to be the most predictable.
Although new drugs may take until 2021 to develop, some drugs are currently being tested have been approved for other uses are already in advanced testing.
Antiviral drugs may be tested in people with severe diseases.
WHO recommends volunteers participate in trials of the effectiveness and safety of potential treatment. The FDA provides temporary authorization for plasma recovery as an experimental treatment in the case for their serious or easily endangered lives.
It has not yet gone through clinical studies needed to stend that it is safe and effective for the disease.
In February 2020, China launched a mobile application to address disease outbreaks.
Users are asked to enter their name and ID.
The application can detect 'closed' using surveillance data and thus a potential risk of infection.
Each user can also check the status of three other users.
If potential risks are detected, the app not only recommends self-quarantine, it also alerts local health authorities. Analysis of major data on cellular phone data, facial recognition technology, mobile phone tracking and artificial intelligence is used to tracing infected people and people they connect in South Korea, Taiwan and Singapore.
In March 2020, the Israeli government allows security agencies to track their mobile phone data.
Measures are taken to enforce quarantine and protect those in contact with infected citizens.
Also in March 2020, Deutche Telekom shared aggregate telephone location data with Germany's federal government agency, Robert Koch Institute, to research and prevent the spread of the virus.
Russia implements facial recognition technology to detect quarantine offenders.
Italian regional health commissioner Giulio Gallera said he was informed by mobile phone operators that "40% of the public continued to move everywhere".
The German government runs a 48-hour weekend hackathon with more than 42,000 participants.
Also Estonia's President Kersti Kaljulaid is calling for a global solution to the spread of the coronavirus.
