Virues are considered to be natural and originate from animals, through overflowing infections.
The actual origin is not known but until December 2019 the overall transmission of infections is almost all driven by human-to-human transmission.
A study for the first 41 confirmed COVID-19 cases, published in January 2020 in The Lancet, revealed that the earliest onset date of symptoms was on December 1, 2019.
Official publications from WHO reported the earliest onset of symptoms was December 8, 2019.
Several measures are usually used to measure the mortality rate.
These figures vary by region and by time and are affected by the number of tests, the quality of the health care system, treatment options, the period since the initial transmission and characteristics of the population such as age, gender and overall health.
At the end of 2019, the WHO prescribed the ICD-10 emergency U07.1 disease code for deaths from laboratory-confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infection and U07.2 for deaths from SARS-CoV-2 infections without clinically or epidemiologically diagnosed COVID-19 laboratory verification. The death-to-case ratio reflects the number of deaths divided by the number of cases diagnosed in intervals given.
According by Johns Hopkins University statistics, the death-to-case global mortality ratio was 6.9% (153,822/2,240,191) as of April 17, 2020.
Number varies by region. Other measures include case death rate (CFR), which reflects the percentage of diagnosed individuals dying from the disease, and the infection mortality rate (IFR) that reflects the percentage of infected individuals (diagnosed and undiagnosed) who died of the disease.
These statistics are not subject on time and according to a specific population from the infection up to case resolution.
While not everyone develops antibodies, the presence of antibodies may provide information about the number of infected people.
