The impact of the pandemic and its fatalities are different for men and women.
The mortality rate was higher for men in studies conducted in China and Italy.
The highest risk for men is those in their 50s, with the gap between males and females being close only at the age of 90.
In China, the mortality rate is 2.8 per cent for men and 1.7 percent for females.
The exact reasons for this sex difference are unknown but genetic and behavioral factors may be the cause.
Immunological differences based on sex, the lack of prevalence of smoking in women and men forms a common morbid condition such as hypertension at a younger age than females may contribute to higher mortality rates in men.
In Europe, 57% of people infected are men and 72% of those who died due to COVID-19 are men.
As of April 2020, the U.S. government has not tracked sex-related data for COVID-19 infection.
Research shows that blue diseases such as Ebola, HIV, influenza and SARS have different effects in men and women.
The higher percentage of health workers, especially nurses, are women and they have higher exposure to the virus.
The World Health Organization announced on 11 February 2020 that the official name of the disease is "COVID-19".
WHO chief Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus explained that CO stands for corona, VI for viruses, D for disease and 19 for the time of the epidemic was first identified: December 31, 2019.
The name was chosen to avoid references to a particular geographical location (e.g. China), an animal species or group of people, in accordance with the international recommendations for the grant of names aimed at evading stigma. The virus that caused COVID-19 was named severe acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus (SARS-CoV-2).
In addition WHO uses "COVID-19 virus" and "virus responsible for COVID-19) in public communication.
Both diseases and viruses are commonly referred as "coronaviruses."
During early transmission in Wuhan, China, viruses and diseases are commonly referred as "coronavirus" and "Wuhan coronavirus".
In January 2020, WHO recommended 2019-nCov and 2019-nCoV acute respiracy as a temporary name for viruses and diseases in line with 2015's guidance on the use of location for diseases and the name of the virus.
The official names of COVID-19 and SARS-CoV-2 were announced on 11 February 2020.
Due to capacity limitations in the standard supply chain, some digital manufacturers manufacture health care materials such as nasal swabs and ventilators.
In an example, when an Italian hospital urgently needs a ventilator valve, and the supplier can not send it on the time scale required, a local reversible engineering startup and produces 100 valves needed overnight.
After the initial transmission of COVID-19, conspiracy theories, misinformation and misreconcible information emerged related to the origins, scale, prevention, treatment and other aspects of the disease and disseminating online quickly.
Humans are able to spread the virus to some other animals.
The studies failed to find evidence of imitation of viruses in pigs, ducks and chickens.
No medications or vaccines are approved to treat the disease.
International research on COVID-19 vaccines and drugs is being run by governmental organizations, academic groups and industry researchers.
In March, the World Health Organization initiated the "SOLIDARITY Trial" to assess the current four antiviral compounds treatment effects with the most predictable effectiveness.
No vaccines are available but various agencies are actively developing vaccine candidates.
Previous efforts on SARS-CoV are being used because SARS-CoV and SARS-CoV-2 use ACE2 receptors to enter human cells.
There are three vaccination strategies being investigated.
First, the researchers aim to build a virus vaccine as a whole.
The use of the virus, whether inactive or died, aims to obtain an immediate immune retaliation in the human body against new infections with COVID-19.
The second strategy, the subunit vaccine, aims to create vaccines that are sensitive to the immune system in some subunits of the virus.
In the case of SARS-CoV-2, the research focuses on the S nail protein that helps the virus encroaches into ACE2 enzyme receptors.
The third strategy is a nucleic acid vaccine (DNA or RNA vaccine, a novel technique to create one vaccination).
An experimental vaccine from any of these strategies needs to be tested for safety and effectiveness. On March 16, 2020, the first clinical trial of the vaccine began with four volunteers in Seattle.
Vaccines containing harmless genetic codes are copied from viruses that cause the disease. An increase in antibody dependence is proposed as a potential challenge for the development of the SARS-COV-2 vaccine, but this is controversial.
There are more than 300 active clinical trials currently being implemented as of April 2020.
Seven trials were evaluated already approved as a treatment for malaria, including four studies on hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine.
Purpose-modified antiviral drugs cover most of China’s research, with nine phase III trials on remdesivir for several countries should be reported by the end of April.
A dynamic review of clinical development for COVID-19 vaccines and drug candidates is provided, as of April 2020. Some current antiviral drugs are being evaluated for the treatment of COVID-19, including remdesivir, chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine, lopinavir/ritonavir and lopinavir/ritonavir combined with interferon beta.
There is tentative evidence for effectiveness by remdesivir as of Mach 2020.
A clinical increase is observed in patients who are treated with the use of remdesivir lovingly.
Phase III clinical trials were carried out in the U.S., China and Italy. Chloroquine, used to treat malaria was previously studied in China in February 2020, providing preliminary results.
However, there is a call for a research equivalent review.
The Korean and Chin authorities recommend the use of chloroquine.
However, the Wuhan Institute of Virology, while recommending a daily dose of one gram, admits that double the dosage is very dangerous and can be fatal.
On March 28, 2020, the FDA published emergency use authorization for hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine at the discretion of physicians treating those infected with COVID-19. China’s 7th edition guidelines also include interferon, ribavirin or umifenovir for use against COVID-19.
Initial data showed that a high dose of ribavirin was necessary to inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in vitro.
Nitazoxanide is recommended for advanced in vivo studies after showing low concentration inhibition in SARS-CoV-2. Studies have shown that the invasion of early-improved proteins by serine protease transmembrane 2 (TMPRSS2) is essential for the inclusion of SARS-CoV-2 through interaction with ACE2 receptors.
Studies of chloroquine and hydroxychloroquine together or without azithromycin have major limitations that prevent medical communities from using this therapy without further studies. Oseltamivir does not inhibit SARS-CoV-2 in vitro and has no known role in the treatment of COVID-19.
Cytokine storms can be a complication in the next stage for severe COVID-19.
There is evidence that hydroxychloroquine has anticyclonic storm properties. Tocilizumab was included in treatment guidelines by China’s National Health Commission’s National Health Commission after a minor study was completed.
It is currently undergoing non-randimatic phase 2 tests at the national level in Italy after showing a positive result with severe illness.
Combined with serum ferritin blood tests to identify cytokine storms, this means to fight the progression, which is considered to be the cause of death for some infected people.
Interleukin-6 receptor antagonists were approved by the FDA based on retrospective case studies for the treatment of steroid refractory cytokine release syndrome due to different causes, CAR T cell therapy in 2017.
To date, there is no randomized controlled evidence that tocilizumab is an effective treatment for CRS.
Transfering purified and concentrated antibodies produced by the immune system of those recovering from COVID-19 to people who need them are being investigated as a non-vaccinable method for passive immunisation.
This strategy is trived on SARS with no conclusive results.
Viral neutralization is an expectable mechanism of action that passive antibody therapy can be intermediating defense against SARS-CoV-2.
Other mechanisms, however, such as cellular cytotoxication of antibody dependence and/or phagocytosis.
Other forms of passive antibody therapy, for example, using monoclone antibodies are produced in development.
The production of the recovery serum, which consists of a liquid part of the blood from the patient who recovers and contains antibodies specific to this virus can be increased for faster implementation.
